var e=JSON.parse("[[2301,\"Components are normally divided by their level of responsibility and of reuse. Primitive UI components, composite components and page components are distinguished. A feature-based or domain-based approach, where the components are grouped by functionality, is often used. That makes navigating the project easier and reduces the coupling. The choice of approach depends on the size of the team and on the complexity of the product.\",\"The organization of components directly affects the readability of the code, the speed of development and the ease of maintenance.\\n\\nA definition\\nA definition: the organization of UI components is the set of rules for dividing and grouping components by folder and by level of abstraction.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- By level of abstraction\\n- UI (Button, Input)\\n- Layout (Header, Sidebar)\\n- Feature (LoginForm, ProductCard)\\n- Page (the pages and the containers)\\n\\n- Feature-based\\n- The components, hooks and styles of one feature sit side by side\\n\\n- Atomic Design\\n- Atoms → Molecules → Organisms → Templates → Pages\\n\\nAn example of a feature-based structure\\n\\nThe key principles\\n\\n- One component — one responsibility\\n- Minimal dependencies between features\\n- UI components know nothing of the business logic\\n- Reusable components do not depend on particular pages\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- Gigantic components of 500+ lines\\n- A rigid tie of the UI to the API\\n- Duplicating similar components\\n\\nConclusion\\nWhat works best is feature-based or combined approaches, where the UI is divided by responsibility and by functionality.\",\"features/\\n  auth/\\n    LoginForm.tsx\\n    useLogin.ts\\n    auth.api.ts\"],[2302,\"Material UI is a library of ready-made React components implementing the Material Design design system. It speeds the development of interfaces up thanks to its ready-made buttons, forms and tables. MUI suits admin panels, internal services and MVPs well. The components can be customized through the theme. The downside is its visual recognizability and the limited freedom of design without extra work.\",\"MUI is often chosen when speed and consistency of UI matter rather than a unique visual style.\\n\\nA definition\\nA definition: Material UI (MUI) is a React component library based on the principles of Material Design and supporting theming.\\n\\nWhen MUI is suitable\\n\\n- Admin panels and back offices\\n- Corporate products\\n- Prototypes and MVPs\\n- Projects with limited design resources\\n\\nThe main capabilities\\n\\n- Ready-made components (Button, TextField, Modal)\\n- Theming (colours, typography, spacing)\\n- Good integration with TypeScript\\n- A large community and good documentation\\n\\nAn example of using a component\\n\\nThe limitations\\n\\n- It is hard to achieve a fully unique design\\n- It requires the theme to be configured carefully\\n- It can increase the bundle size without optimization\\n\\nConclusion\\nMUI is used when you need to put a stable interface together quickly with minimal effort, accepting the design system's limitations.\",\"<Button variant=\\\"contained\\\" color=\\\"primary\\\">\\n  Save\\n</Button>\"],[2303,\"The choice depends on the kind of data and on your application's architecture. Apollo Client is a specialized solution for working with a GraphQL API, which provides caching and the management of query and subscription state. Redux Toolkit is a universal library for managing an application's global state, ideally suited to complex business logic not directly related to network requests. React Query (TanStack Query) focuses on asynchronous state: the caching, synchronization and updating of data obtained over REST, GraphQL or other protocols.\",\"The choice between Apollo Client, Redux Toolkit and React Query is determined by your application's architectural needs. Each library solves different problems, and they can be combined.\\n\\nApollo Client: for GraphQL\\nApollo Client is a comprehensive solution for applications that use a GraphQL API. It provides a built-in normalized cache, which updates the UI automatically when the data changes. It is ideally suited to complex GraphQL operations, including subscriptions, pagination and optimistic updates.\\n\\nRedux Toolkit: for global state\\nRedux Toolkit (RTK) is the standard way of managing global state in React. It is excellent for state unrelated to network requests: UI state, a form's data, complex business logic that has to be available from different components. RTK Query (part of RTK) adds the ability to work with an API, but RTK's main strength is in managing state predictably.\\n\\nReact Query: for asynchronous state\\nReact Query (TanStack Query) specializes in managing asynchronous state: caching, background updates, synchronization, pagination and infinite queries. It is independent of the kind of API (REST, GraphQL) and works excellently together with any state manager, such as Redux or Context, taking on all of the work with the server data.\\n\\nConclusion: use Apollo Client if your backend is GraphQL and you need deep integration with its capabilities. Choose Redux Toolkit for complex client state that requires strict control and predictability. React Query is the best choice for managing the caching, the synchronization and the lifecycle of server data in applications with REST, GraphQL or other APIs, especially when you want to simplify the code related to loading data.\",\"import { useQuery, gql } from '@apollo/client';\\nconst GET_USERS = gql`\\n  query GetUsers {\\n    users {\\n      id\\n      name\\n    }\\n  }\\n`;\\nfunction UsersList() {\\n  const { loading, error, data } = useQuery(GET_USERS);\\n  // Apollo manages the cache and the loading and error states\\n}\\n\\nimport { createSlice, configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'counter',\\n  initialState: { value: 0 },\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment(state) { state.value += 1; }\\n  }\\n});\\n// It holds state that does not depend on the server.\\n\\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';\\nfunction fetchUsers() {\\n  return fetch('/api/users').then(res => res.json());\\n}\\nfunction UsersList() {\\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\\n    queryKey: ['users'],\\n    queryFn: fetchUsers,\\n  });\\n  // Automatic caching, repeated requests when the window regains focus\\n}\"],[2304,\"The approaches divide into local state management (useState, Context) and global (Redux, Zustand). For requests there is the built-in fetch and specialized libraries (React Query, SWR). The advantages of the local approach are simplicity and development speed for small applications; the disadvantage is the difficulty of scaling. Global solutions (Redux) give predictability and good debugging but add boilerplate code. Specialized libraries for requests (React Query) cache the data and manage background updates, which simplifies the logic, but they require you to learn their API.\",\"An overview of the approaches to managing state and requests\\nIn modern web applications, especially React ones, managing state and asynchronous requests is a key architectural task. The choice of approach affects maintainability, performance and development speed. The main division runs between managing a component's local state, the application's global state, and a separate layer for the data obtained from the server.\\n\\nLocal state management (useState, useReducer)\\nThis is the simplest approach, built into React. The state lives inside the component and is passed down through props.\\n- The advantages: no external dependencies, a fast start, ideal for isolated UI logic (a form's state, for example).\\n- The disadvantages: \\\"prop drilling\\\" with deep nesting, it is hard to share state between distant components.\\n\\nGlobal state management (Redux, Zustand, MobX)\\nThe state is moved into a separate store, available from any component.\\n- Redux's advantages: predictability (pure reducers), powerful debugging tools (Redux DevTools), a strict architecture for large teams.\\n- Redux's disadvantages: a large volume of boilerplate code (actions, reducers), a learning curve.\\n- The alternatives (Zustand): less boilerplate, a simpler API, but less strict control of the data flow.\\n\\nManaging server state and requests (React Query, SWR, Apollo)\\nThese libraries specialize in data from the backend: caching, invalidation, background updates, pagination.\\n- React Query's advantages: the automatic caching and updating of data, a minimum of manual management of the loading/error state, powerful hooks for infinite queries and optimistic updates.\\n- The disadvantages: an additional dependency; the concepts (stale-while-revalidate) have to be learned. It does not replace global client state (the UI theme, for example).\\n\\nA practical example: a comparison of the approaches\\nLet us look at a simple component that loads a list of users.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor small applications or isolated components, useState/useEffect is enough. For complex client state that many components change (a shopping cart, for example), choose Zustand or Redux. For data from the server (lists, details) it is almost always worth using a specialized library (React Query/SWR), in order to avoid managing the cache by hand and to improve performance.\",\"// 1. The native approach with fetch and useState\\nconst [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\nconst [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  setLoading(true);\\n  fetch('/api/users')\\n    .then(r => r.json())\\n    .then(setUsers)\\n    .finally(() => setLoading(false));\\n}, []);\\n\\n// 2. The approach with React Query\\nconst { data: users, isLoading } = useQuery('users', () =>\\n  fetch('/api/users').then(r => r.json())\\n);\\n// The caching, the repeated requests when the window regains focus and the errors are handled automatically.\"],[2305,\"With a partial failure of a micro-frontend you have to make the application fault-tolerant. The main approaches: show a fallback UI, use cached versions of the module, or hide the broken section. It is critical not to let the whole application fail because of one part that does not work. The implementation depends on the framework chosen (Module Federation in Webpack, for example) and should include global handlers for loading errors.\",\"In a micro-frontend architecture an application consists of independent remote modules that are loaded at run time. If one of those modules (the product catalogue or the admin panel, for example) crashes or becomes unavailable, that must not lead to the whole application collapsing. Strategies for partial fault tolerance have to be implemented.\\n\\nThe main strategies for handling failures\\n\\n- A fallback UI: the most widespread approach. When a module fails to load or to run, show the user a fallback interface — an error message, a spinner, or a simplified but functional version of the component.\\n- Caching: keep the previous successfully loaded version of the module (in localStorage or a Service Worker, for example) and use it on the next failed attempt to load.\\n- Conditional hiding: if the functionality is not critical to the main flow, you can simply hide the broken section of the interface and leave the rest of the application working.\\n- Retries with exponential backoff: with temporary network failures you can make several attempts to reload the module, with increasing pauses between them.\\n\\nA practical implementation\\nLet us look at an example using Webpack Module Federation and React. The loading of the remote module has to be wrapped in a component with error handling.\\n\\nAt the container application's level it is also worth setting up a global error handler (by means of `window.addEventListener('error', ...)`, for example) in order to catch failed script loads.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is critically important in large distributed web applications, where different teams develop and deploy their own parts independently. It gives resilience and uninterrupted operation for the end user even when individual services fail.\\n\\nConclusion: handling partial failures is an essential practice for production micro-frontend applications. It lets you isolate failures while keeping the main functionality working, and it improves the user experience. Use fallback interfaces and error monitoring for the key remote modules.\",\"import React, { Suspense, lazy, useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nconst RemoteWidget = () => {\\n  const [Component, setComponent] = useState(null);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(false);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The dynamic import of the remote module\\n    const loadComponent = async () => {\\n      try {\\n        // 'remoteApp' and 'Widget' are the Module Federation configuration\\n        const comp = await import('remoteApp/Widget');\\n        setComponent(() => comp.default);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        console.error('Failed to load remote module:', err);\\n        setError(true);\\n        // Here the error can be sent to a monitoring system\\n      }\\n    };\\n    loadComponent();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  if (error) {\\n    // The fallback UI on an error\\n    return (\\n      <div className=\\\"remote-widget-fallback\\\">\\n        <p>This section is temporarily unavailable. Please try again later.</p>\\n        <button onClick={() => window.location.reload()}>Refresh</button>\\n      </div>\\n    );\\n  }\\n\\n  if (!Component) {\\n    // The loading state\\n    return <div>Loading the module...</div>;\\n  }\\n\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Component />\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport default RemoteWidget;\"],[2306,\"The main way is to compile the C++ code into WebAssembly (Wasm) by means of tools such as Emscripten. The compiled Wasm module is loaded into the React application as an ordinary JavaScript module. The data is passed through WebAssembly's memory or by calling the functions exported from the C++. For simple scenarios you can use Node.js Native Addons, if the application runs in an Electron environment.\",\"To get data from native C++ code into a React application running in the browser, the standard modern solution is to use WebAssembly (Wasm). WebAssembly is a binary instruction format that lets you run code written in languages such as C/C++ with almost native performance right in the browser.\\n\\nThe main approach: compiling to WebAssembly\\nThe Emscripten tool compiles C/C++ code into a .wasm module and generates JavaScript \\\"glue code\\\", which handles the loading and the interaction. In the React application you load that module and call the functions it exports.\\n\\nAn example of the steps and the code\\n1. Write the C++ code with the functions you need to export, using Emscripten's macros.\\n\\n2. Compile the code into WebAssembly by means of Emscripten.\\n\\nThis command will create the files `native_lib.wasm` and `native_lib.js`.\\n\\n3. Integrate the module into a React component.\\n\\nThe alternative methods and their uses\\n\\n- Node.js Native Addons + Electron: if your React application is packaged in Electron (a desktop application), you can create Native Addons in C++ and call them through Node.js's API. That gives direct access to the system's resources.\\n- A server proxy (Backend-for-Frontend): the C++ application runs as a separate service (on localhost, for example). The React application sends HTTP/WebSocket requests to a server application (in Node.js/Python), which in turn interacts with the C++ library through a Foreign Function Interface (FFI). This approach separates the native logic from the frontend.\\n\\nConclusion: use the compilation of C++ into WebAssembly when you need high computational performance (image processing or physics simulations, for example) right in the browser. For Electron-based desktop applications Native Addons are preferable, since they give full access to the system. A server proxy is suitable when the native code has to run on a separate server or needs access to resources unavailable from the browser.\",\"// native_lib.cpp\\n#include <emscripten/emscripten.h>\\n\\n// An exported function returning a number\\nEMSCRIPTEN_KEEPALIVE\\nint getValueFromNativeLayer() {\\n    // Complex C++ logic can go here\\n    return 42;\\n}\\n\\n// A function that takes a parameter and returns a result\\nEMSCRIPTEN_KEEPALIVE\\nint processData(int input) {\\n    return input * 2;\\n}\\n\\nemcc native_lib.cpp \\\\\\n     -o native_lib.js \\\\\\n     -s EXPORTED_FUNCTIONS=\\\"['_getValueFromNativeLayer', '_processData']\\\" \\\\\\n     -s MODULARIZE=1 -s EXPORT_ES6=1\\n\\nimport React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    const [nativeValue, setNativeValue] = useState(null);\\n\\n    useEffect(() => {\\n        // We import the generated module dynamically\\n        import('./native_lib.js').then(module => {\\n            // Once the module has loaded we call the exported function\\n            const value = module._getValueFromNativeLayer();\\n            setNativeValue(value);\\n\\n            // An example of calling a function with a parameter\\n            const processed = module._processData(10);\\n            console.log('Processed data:', processed);\\n        });\\n    }, []);\\n\\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <p>The value from the native layer (C++):</p>\\n            <strong>{nativeValue !== null ? nativeValue : 'Loading...'}</strong>\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nexport default App;\"],[2307,\"The integration of a React application with native code in a WebView is done through a special \\\"bridge\\\". The native part provides an interface that can be called from JavaScript. In React you use that interface in order to send data or call native functions. In the other direction, the native code can run JavaScript in the WebView in order to pass data into a React component. The main limitations: performance is lower than on native screens, access to some of the device's APIs can be limited or require additional configuration through the bridge, and delays in the communication between the layers are possible.\",\"Hybrid applications, where a React interface runs inside a WebView, call for a reliable channel of communication between the JavaScript code and the native platform (Java/Kotlin for Android, Objective-C/Swift for iOS). That channel is called a bridge.\\n\\nHow to organize the integration\\nThe process normally involves the following steps:\\n- Setting the WebView up: in the native application a WebView component is created, which loads your React application (either from a local server or from a static HTML file).\\n- Creating the bridge: the native platform registers an interface that becomes available in JavaScript's global context (`window.NativeBridge`, for example).\\n- Calling native methods from React: React components can call that interface's methods in order to access the device's features (the camera, GPS, the file system).\\n- Feedback (from the native code to React): the native code can run JavaScript in the WebView's context — by calling predefined callback functions, for example — in order to pass data or report events.\\n\\nAn example of the code (schematically)\\nBelow is a simplified example for Android (Kotlin) and the corresponding React code.\\n\\nThe limitations compared with a browser\\n\\n- Performance: a WebView can be slower, especially for complex animation or for processing large amounts of data. Scrolling and responsiveness sometimes lose out to native components.\\n- Access to APIs: the standard browser APIs (Service Workers, some properties of window, for example) can be unavailable or behave differently. Access to device-specific features (contacts, sensors) almost always requires an implementation through the bridge.\\n- Debugging: debugging code in a WebView can be harder than in a browser, although the modern tools (Chrome DevTools for Android, Safari Web Inspector for iOS) help.\\n- Loading and caching: the management of the cache and of resource loading can differ, which affects the speed of the first start.\\n- Security: the data passed through the bridge has to be checked thoroughly, in order to avoid injections.\\n\\nConclusion: this approach is worth applying when you need to develop a cross-platform interface quickly using React while requiring some native capabilities. It suits prototypes, internal tools or applications where performance is not a critical factor. For high-performance applications or ones with a complex UI it is worth considering native solutions or frameworks such as React Native.\",\"// Android (Kotlin) - setting the WebView up\\nclass MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\\n        val webView = WebView(this).apply {\\n            settings.javaScriptEnabled = true\\n            addJavascriptInterface(NativeBridge(this@MainActivity), \\\"NativeBridge\\\")\\n            loadUrl(\\\"file:///android_asset/index.html\\\")\\n        }\\n        setContentView(webView)\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass NativeBridge(private val context: Context) {\\n    @JavascriptInterface\\n    fun showToast(message: String) {\\n        Toast.makeText(context, message, Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show()\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The React component\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nconst App = () => {\\n    const sendToNative = () => {\\n        if (window.NativeBridge && window.NativeBridge.showToast) {\\n            window.NativeBridge.showToast('Hello from React!');\\n        } else {\\n            console.warn('Native bridge not available');\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    return (\\n        <button onClick={sendToNative}>\\n            Show Native Toast\\n        </button>\\n    );\\n};\\n\\nexport default App;\"],[2308,\"The base path is the part of the URL between the domain and the start of the path to a particular resource of the application. It can be determined from the `window.location` object. If the application is deployed at `https://example.com/my-app/`, for example, then the base path is `/my-app/`. For SPAs that use the HTML5 History API, the base path is often set in the router's configuration (in React Router — the `basename` prop). It can also be computed by comparing `window.location.pathname` against the known structure of the application's paths.\",\"The base path (or public path) is the URL prefix under which a web application is deployed on the server. It separates the application's static resources and routes from the root of the domain. Understanding and determining the base path correctly is necessary for client-side routing to work properly, for resources (images, scripts, styles) to load and for links to be formed.\\n\\nThe main ways of determining it\\nMost often the base path is extracted from the browser's global `window.location` object:\\n\\nWorking with the modern frameworks\\nIn the React, Vue and Angular ecosystems the base path is normally configured in the router:\\n- React Router: the `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` component.\\n- Vue Router: the `base` option when creating the router instance.\\n- Angular: configuring `APP_BASE_HREF` in the providers.\\n\\nThese frameworks take the base path into account automatically when generating links and when navigating.\\n\\nA practical example with an environment variable\\nIn builds using Webpack or Vite the base path is often set through an environment variable or the configuration:\\n\\nConclusion: determining the base path is necessary in order to deploy an application in a subdirectory (rather than at the root of the domain). Use your framework's router settings for centralized control, or compute the path from `window.location` if dynamic determination is required. That guarantees that every relative path and route will work correctly in any deployment environment.\",\"// Example 1: getting the full path from the URL\\nconst fullPath = window.location.pathname; // '/my-app/users/123', for example\\n\\n// If the application always sits at a fixed path\\nconst hardcodedBasePath = '/my-app/';\\n\\n// Example 2: determining it dynamically, if the application may start from the root\\nlet basePath = '/';\\nif (fullPath.startsWith('/my-app/')) {\\n    basePath = '/my-app/';\\n}\\nconsole.log('Base Path:', basePath);\\n\\n// The configuration for a production build\\nconst BASE_PATH = process.env.PUBLIC_URL || '';\\n\\n// Using it as a prefix for API requests or links\\nfunction getApiUrl(endpoint) {\\n    return `${BASE_PATH}/api/v1${endpoint}`;\\n}\\n\\n// Using it in the markup for static resources\\nconst logoUrl = `${BASE_PATH}/static/logo.png`;\"],[2309,\"Deploying an application somewhere other than the root (in `/app/`, for example) causes several problems. The relative paths to the resources (CSS, JS, images) can break if they are not configured properly. The routing in single-page applications (SPAs) can conflict with the web server's settings and require URLs to be rewritten. Problems with CORS and with cookies can arise if the subpaths are treated as different origins. SEO also suffers, since search engines can index pages with incorrect absolute paths wrongly.\",\"When a web application is deployed not at the root of the domain but in a subdirectory (`https://example.com/myapp/`, for example), that affects a great many aspects of how it works. The main difficulty is that the application expects every resource and route to start from the server's root (`/`), whereas in fact they have to be tied to the base path (`/myapp/`).\\n\\nProblems with the paths to resources\\nRelative paths in the HTML, the CSS and the JavaScript start resolving relative to the browser's current URL. If the file `style.css` is given as `./styles.css` on the page `/myapp/dashboard`, the browser will try to load it from `/myapp/dashboard/styles.css`, which is most likely wrong. The solution is to use absolute paths beginning with the application's base path, or to configure the `<base>` tag in the HTML.\\n\\nProblems with routing in SPAs (React, Vue, Angular)\\nRouting libraries such as React Router work with the root by default. If the application lives in `/myapp/`, every route has to be configured with that prefix. Without that, navigation inside the application will work, but on a page reload or on a direct visit to a URL the web server will return a 404 error, since it will not find the corresponding file on disk. The server has to be configured to rewrite every request inside `/myapp/` to the index HTML file.\\n\\nProblems with API requests and CORS\\nIf the backend API is deployed separately or also under a subpath, the frontend has to form the URLs for its requests correctly. Using relative paths for the API (`/api/users`) will lead to a request to `https://example.com/myapp/api/users`, which may be the wrong address. That can cause CORS errors, if the API's server is not configured to allow requests from such a path.\\n\\nSEO and social meta tags\\nSearch engines and social networks (Open Graph, Twitter Cards) use absolute URLs. If the application generates meta tags with relative paths for its images or canonical URLs, those will be incomplete, which will make indexing and the display in social networks worse.\\n\\nConclusion: deploying somewhere other than the root requires careful configuration of the base path in the frontend application, configuration of the web server (Nginx, Apache) for handling the routes properly, and the use of absolute paths for resources and for the API. This is especially critical for SPAs and for applications where deep links and SEO matter.\",\"// An example of configuring the base path in React Router v6\\nimport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    // It is important to state the basename\\n    <BrowserRouter basename=\\\"/myapp\\\">\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\\"/\\\" element={<Home />} />\\n        <Route path=\\\"/about\\\" element={<About />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2310,\"A dynamic base path lets a React application work correctly when it is deployed not at the root of the domain but in a subdirectory (/app/, for example). For that you have to configure the router and the bundler. In React Router, use the `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` component. The basename's value can be taken from an environment variable or a configuration file. You also have to configure Webpack or your other bundler by setting `publicPath`, so that the static resources (JS, CSS) are loaded from the right path.\",\"Support for a dynamic base path is necessary when your React application is deployed not at the root of the domain (`https://example.com`, for example) but in a subdirectory (`https://example.com/my-app/`, for example). This is often encountered in microservice architectures, where several applications live on one domain, or on shared hosting. Without the right configuration the application will look for its resources and routes at the wrong paths, which will lead to 404 errors and broken navigation.\\n\\nConfiguring React Router\\nThe main tool for managing paths in React is the router. In React Router (v6) you can set the base path by means of the `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` or `<HashRouter>` component. The value has to be a string corresponding to the subdirectory in which your application lives. To make it dynamic, you can read the value from an environment variable or from a configuration file loaded at build or run time.\\n\\nConfiguring the bundler (Webpack)\\nFor the static resources (JavaScript, CSS, images) to load properly, you have to configure `publicPath` in your bundler. In Webpack that is done in the configuration. The value of `publicPath` has to match your application's base path. It too can be made dynamic by using environment variables.\\n\\nIf you use Create React App, you can set the environment variable `PUBLIC_URL` before the build. For example, `PUBLIC_URL=/my-app npm run build`. That will set `publicPath` in Webpack automatically and will be used to prefix the paths to the resources in the HTML.\\n\\nWorking with links and API requests\\nWhen a base path is used, every relative link to a resource (images, for example) and every API endpoint has to take that prefix into account. For links inside the application use the `<Link>` component from React Router, which takes the `basename` into account automatically. For API requests it is recommended that you use absolute URLs or create a base axios/fetch instance with the prefix set.\\n\\nConclusion: a dynamic base path is worth implementing when your React application is going to be deployed in a subdirectory. It makes the routing work properly and the resources load correctly in environments such as microservices, corporate portals or shared hosting.\",\"import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\\n\\n// The base path's value can be taken from an environment variable or a config.\\nconst BASE_PATH = process.env.REACT_APP_BASE_PATH || '/';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <BrowserRouter basename={BASE_PATH}>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\\"/\\\" element={<HomePage />} />\\n        <Route path=\\\"/about\\\" element={<AboutPage />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  output: {\\n    publicPath: process.env.PUBLIC_PATH || '/',\\n  },\\n  // ... the rest of the configuration\\n};\\n\\n// An example of configuring axios with a base URL\\nimport axios from 'axios';\\n\\nconst api = axios.create({\\n  baseURL: process.env.REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL || '/api',\\n});\\n\\n// Every request through this instance will have the right prefix.\\napi.get('/users').then(response => console.log(response));\"],[2311,\"The base URL (the base path) in React Router is the prefix that is added automatically to every relative route in the application. It is needed when your React application is deployed not at the root of the domain (on `example.com/my-app/`, for example) but in a subdirectory. Without the right base URL the routing can break, since React Router will look for the paths relative to the server's root rather than to your subdirectory. You set it by means of the `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` component. That makes links and navigation work properly in a production environment.\",\"In web development, especially when deploying single-page applications (SPAs), the situation often arises where the application is placed not in the domain's root directory but inside a subfolder. It may be available at `https://company.com/dashboard/` or `https://github.com/username/project-name/` (for GitHub Pages), for example. By default React Router assumes the application runs from the root (`/`). If that is not so, every relative route (`/users` or `/settings`, for example) will be resolved relative to the server's root, which will lead to 404 errors or to components being displayed incorrectly.\\n\\nWhat is basename?\\nThe `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` (or `<HashRouter>`) component lets you state the base path for the whole application. Every route defined inside that router will have that prefix automatically. It affects:\\n\\n- The generation of links by the `<Link>` component.\\n- The matching of paths in `<Routes>`.\\n- The working of hooks such as `useNavigate()` and `useLocation()`.\\n\\nHow do you use it?\\nYou set the `basename` when creating the router. Here is a practical example:\\n\\nIn this example, if the user goes to `/admin/users`, React Router will display the `UsersList` component correctly, because it knows the base path is `/admin`.\\n\\nThe important subtleties\\n\\n- The server's configuration: stating a `basename` in React Router does not replace the server's configuration. Your web server (Nginx, Apache) or your hosting (GitHub Pages, Netlify) also has to be configured so that every request within `/your-base-path` is redirected to your `index.html` (the \\\"catch-all\\\" rule for SPAs).\\n- Absolute paths: if you use absolute URLs (beginning with `/`) inside the `basename`, they will be completed automatically. Relative paths (without a leading slash) work relative to the current URL.\\n- The hash router: for `HashRouter` the principle is the same, but the hash fragment (`#`) is added after the base path.\\n\\nConclusion: use `basename` in React Router when your application is deployed in a subdirectory on the server. It is an essential condition for the routing and the navigation to work properly and for avoiding 404 errors in a production environment.\",\"import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  // The application is deployed in the subdirectory /admin\\n  return (\\n    <BrowserRouter basename=\\\"/admin\\\">\\n      <nav>\\n        <Link to=\\\"/\\\">Dashboard</Link> // The actual path: /admin/\\n        <Link to=\\\"/users\\\">Users</Link> // The actual path: /admin/users\\n      </nav>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\\"/\\\" element={<Dashboard />} />\\n        <Route path=\\\"/users\\\" element={<UsersList />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2312,\"The main problems include the explosive growth of the bundle size, which slows loading down. State management and the communication between a great many components become more complicated. Difficulties grow with organizing the codebase, with the dependencies and with the consistency of the UI/UX. It becomes hard for the teams to coordinate their work without clear conventions and tools.\",\"Scaling a frontend is not only about performance but about maintaining the codebase, managing the team and ensuring the product is consistent. As an application grows, systemic difficulties arise.\\n\\nThe architectural problems\\n\\n- The bundle size: a single build becomes enormous, increasing the initial loading time. The solution is code splitting by route or by component, and lazy loading.\\n- State management: the data flow becomes tangled. Global state (through Redux, for example) can become overloaded and hard to debug. It is important to divide the state by domain and to use normalized structures.\\n- Rendering performance: large lists or complex components can block the main thread. Virtualization, memoization (React.memo, useMemo) and the optimization of re-renders are required.\\n\\nThe organizational and process problems\\n\\n- A monolithic codebase: a single repository becomes the bottleneck. The solutions: moving to a monorepo with tools such as Lerna or Nx, or dividing things into micro-frontends.\\n- The consistency of the UI and the logic: without strict conventions and design systems, components start being duplicated and their behaviour diverges. A library of UI components and shared hooks is needed.\\n- Managing the dependencies: updating libraries (React, for example) in a large project becomes a risky operation. Automated testing and tools for gradual upgrades help.\\n\\nAn example: optimizing the bundle with a dynamic import\\n\\nConclusion: frontend scaling problems call for a well-thought-out architecture from the very first stages: splitting the code, choosing a suitable state-management strategy, introducing a design system and tools for working in a monorepo. This is critically important to maintaining development speed and the application's performance in the long run.\",\"// Instead of a static import of the whole module\\nimport { HeavyChartLibrary } from './chartLibrary';\\n\\n// We use a dynamic import for lazy loading\\nconst renderChart = async () => {\\n  // This code will be loaded only when the function is called\\n  const { HeavyChartLibrary } = await import('./chartLibrary');\\n  HeavyChartLibrary.draw();\\n};\\n\\n// In React Router v6\\nconst Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./pages/Dashboard'));\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\\"/dashboard\\\" element={<Dashboard />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2313,\"The key lies in a well-thought-out information architecture and in the principle of progressive disclosure. Show the user only the main, frequently used features first. The additional, specialized actions can be hidden in context menus, behind \\\"More\\\" buttons or in advanced modes. An adaptive toolbar that changes according to the context of the task matters. Do not forget personalization, letting experienced users configure the interface to suit themselves, and good search across the features.\",\"When designing an interface for a feature-rich application, the main challenge is the balance between power and simplicity. Showing every capability at once leads to cognitive overload and frightens newcomers. The solution lies in the area of information architecture and of UX patterns oriented towards managing complexity.\\n\\nThe key strategies\\n\\n- Progressive disclosure: show the features as they are needed. The main workflow should use a minimal set of elements. The additional options open on a click, on hover, or in a separate dialogue.\\n- Contextual interfaces: the toolbars and the menus should change dynamically according to the object selected or the mode of work. That reduces the visual noise.\\n- Modularity and grouping: combine related features into groups with clear headings. Use dividers, tabs or accordions to organize them.\\n- Personalization: give experienced users the ability to pin the tools they use often to the main panel, to hide the ones they do not need and to create keyboard shortcuts of their own.\\n- Effective search and a command palette: introduce a global search across the features and actions. That lets you get to any command quickly, bypassing the menu navigation.\\n\\nAn example of implementing a command search\\nHere is a simplified example of a command-search component in React, which filters the available actions:\\n\\nThis approach, inspired by code editors, lets you get to hundreds of features instantly.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThese principles are critically important in professional software: graphics editors (Photoshop, Figma), IDEs (VS Code, IntelliJ), complex CRM and ERP systems, and the administration panels of web applications.\\n\\nConclusion: to ensure convenience in a multi-feature application, use progressive disclosure, contextual interfaces and a powerful search, in order to lower the barrier to entry for newcomers without limiting what the experts can do. Personalization and an adaptive interface are the key to good UX in the long run.\",\"function CommandPalette({ commands }) {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  const filteredCommands = commands.filter(cmd =>\\n    cmd.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()) ||\\n    cmd.keywords.some(kw => kw.includes(query))\\n  );\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\\"command-palette\\\">\\n      <input\\n        value={query}\\n        onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)}\\n        placeholder=\\\"Type a command...\\\"\\n      />\\n      <ul>\\n        {filteredCommands.map(cmd => (\\n          <li key={cmd.id} onClick={cmd.action}>\\n            {cmd.name}: {cmd.description}\\n          </li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// Usage: the commands are an array of objects {id, name, action, keywords}\"],[2314,\"There are a great many UI libraries for React providing ready-made components. PrimeReact, Material-UI, Ant Design and Chakra UI are among the most popular. PrimeReact offers an extensive set of components with various themes, Material and Bootstrap included. Material-UI follows Google's Material Design principles strictly. Ant Design is popular for corporate applications, with clear, functional components. The choice depends on the requirements for the design, for accessibility and for development speed.\",\"UI libraries for React provide a set of ready-made, styled, interactive components (buttons, tables, modal windows), which speeds the development of interfaces up considerably and ensures the design is uniform. They save the developer from having to write the CSS and the logic for the common elements from scratch.\\n\\nThe popular libraries and their characteristics\\n\\n- Material-UI (MUI): the most popular library, implementing Google's Material Design design system. It stands out for its high customizability, its excellent documentation and its large community. It is ideal for projects that want to follow the modern Material guidelines.\\n- Ant Design: a powerful library for enterprise applications. It offers an extremely rich set of professional-looking components, especially for data (complex tables, forms, calendars). It is popular in the React ecosystem alongside Next.js.\\n- PrimeReact: it stands out for its enormous number of components (more than 80), complex ones such as charts and trees included. It supports several built-in themes (Material, Bootstrap, its own) and has excellent accessibility (a11y). A good choice when you need maximum functionality out of the box.\\n- Chakra UI: a library gaining popularity, focused on simplicity, accessibility and composability. The styling is based on props, which makes building a UI very declarative and fast. It suits start-ups and projects where speed and a modern look and feel matter.\\n\\nAn example of using PrimeReact\\nOnce the library is installed you can use its components straight away. Here is an example of a simple button and a dialogue window:\\n\\nThis code shows how quickly you can add a styled button with an icon and a controlled modal window without writing a single line of CSS.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of UI library depends on the project's requirements: for strict adherence to Material Design choose MUI, for corporate dashboards — Ant Design, for maximum functionality and themes — PrimeReact, and for fast prototyping and a modern approach — Chakra UI. Using any of them cuts development time down and improves the interface's consistency.\",\"import { Button } from 'primereact/button';\\nimport { Dialog } from 'primereact/dialog';\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [visible, setVisible] = useState(false);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Button\\n        label=\\\"Show\\\"\\n        icon=\\\"pi pi-external-link\\\"\\n        onClick={() => setVisible(true)}\\n      />\\n      <Dialog\\n        header=\\\"Header\\\"\\n        visible={visible}\\n        style={{ width: '50vw' }}\\n        onHide={() => setVisible(false)}\\n      >\\n        <p>This is PrimeReact Dialog content.</p>\\n      </Dialog>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\nexport default App;\"],[2315,\"Base filters are added to user filters through composition or the 'Decorator' pattern. First the base filtering logic is defined — by a date range, for example. Then the user filters are applied on top, combining the conditions with 'AND' or 'OR'. That lets you keep the standard constraints while adding flexible settings. It is implemented through a chain of functions or a wrapper object that applies every filter in sequence.\",\"Adding base (system) filters to user filters is a task of extending filtering functionality, often encountered in the interfaces of admin panels, of reports or of APIs. The base filters provide the mandatory constraints — filtering by the user's organization or by a period of time, for example — while the user filters provide the flexibility to configure the queries.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\nThere are two main architectural approaches:\\n- The composition of functions (or predicates): each filter is a function that takes the data (or the query criteria) and returns a filtered result. The base and the user filters are combined into a chain, where the result of one is passed to the next.\\n- The \\\"Decorator\\\" pattern: a base class or object implementing the filter's interface is created. It is then \\\"wrapped\\\" in decorators, each of which adds its own filtering logic. This is especially useful when the filters have complex state or have to be applied in a particular order.\\n\\nA practical example in JavaScript\\nLet us look at a simple example of the composition of functions for filtering an array of user objects.\\n\\nIn this example the base filters (`filterByActive`, `filterByOrg`) set the mandatory conditions, while the user one (`filterByName`) adds an additional refinement. The order of application can matter: normally the base filters are applied first, in order to reduce the volume of data.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is widely used in:\\n- Backend development: building queries to a database (in an ORM such as Sequelize or TypeORM, for example), where conditions from the API's parameters are added to a base query (WHERE org_id = X).\\n- Frontend development: filtering the data in tables (in React components, for example), where there are fixed filters (the default sorting) and dynamic ones chosen by the user.\\n- Reporting systems and BI tools: global filters (a period, for example) are combined with the user's detailed settings.\\n\\nConclusion: base filters should be added to user filters through composition or a decorator, which ensures the system's constraints are observed and gives flexibility. This approach is worth applying wherever you need to separate the mandatory business logic from the data-selection conditions the user can configure.\",\"// The base (system) filters\\nconst filterByActive = (users) => users.filter(u => u.isActive);\\nconst filterByOrg = (orgId) => (users) => users.filter(u => u.orgId === orgId);\\n\\n// A user filter (dynamic)\\nconst filterByName = (name) => (users) => users.filter(u => u.name.includes(name));\\n\\n// A function for composing the filters\\nconst applyFilters = (data, filters) => {\\n    return filters.reduce((result, filterFn) => filterFn(result), data);\\n};\\n\\n// An example of its use\\nconst allUsers = [\\n    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', isActive: true, orgId: 101 },\\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', isActive: false, orgId: 101 },\\n    { id: 3, name: 'Charlie', isActive: true, orgId: 102 }\\n];\\n\\n// We create the chain: the base ones first, then the user ones\\nconst filters = [\\n    filterByActive,               // Base: only the active ones\\n    filterByOrg(101),             // Base: only organization 101\\n    filterByName('A')             // User: the name contains 'A'\\n];\\n\\nconst filteredUsers = applyFilters(allUsers, filters);\\nconsole.log(filteredUsers); // [{ id: 1, name: 'Alice', ... }]\"],[2316,\"The Virtual DOM is a concept rather than an exclusive part of React. It is a programming pattern in which a lightweight representation of the real DOM is created in memory. Other libraries, such as Vue.js and Inferno, also use this idea in order to optimize updates to an interface. The point is to compare the changes in the virtual tree and apply only the necessary edits to the real DOM, which speeds rendering up. You can even implement a simplified version of a Virtual DOM of your own for learning purposes.\",\"The Virtual DOM (VDOM) is an architectural pattern rather than a technology tied exclusively to React. It solves a fundamental performance problem in web applications where the DOM is manipulated frequently. Working with the DOM directly through JavaScript (`document.createElement`, for example) is an expensive operation, especially with mass updates. The VDOM proposes creating and manipulating lightweight JavaScript objects that represent the structure of the real DOM. A special algorithm (reconciliation) then determines the minimal set of changes between two states of the VDOM and applies only those to the real browser DOM.\\n\\nWhere else is the Virtual DOM used?\\nMany modern UI libraries and frameworks have adopted this concept:\\n- Vue.js uses its own reactive Virtual DOM in order to track the dependencies and update things efficiently.\\n- Inferno and Preact present themselves as lighter, faster alternatives to React, also built on a VDOM.\\n- Mithril and Snabbdom are libraries offering a minimalist, modular implementation of a VDOM.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation of your own\\nThe simplest implementation helps you understand the essence of it. Let us look at creating a VDOM element and a function for rendering it:\\n\\nIn real libraries, of course, the implementation includes a complex comparison (diffing) algorithm in order to update only the parts that have changed rather than repainting the whole tree.\\n\\nConclusion: the concept of the Virtual DOM is worth applying when you are developing a dynamic web application with intensive interface updates and want to abstract away from manual, potentially inefficient DOM manipulation. It is useful not only in React but as a general pattern for improving UI performance.\",\"// A function for creating a VDOM node (an object)\\nfunction createElement(tag, props, ...children) {\\n    return {\\n        tag,\\n        props: props || {},\\n        children: children.flat() // The children can be strings or other VDOM nodes\\n    };\\n}\\n\\n// A function for turning a VDOM object into a real DOM element\\nfunction render(vnode) {\\n    if (typeof vnode === 'string') {\\n        return document.createTextNode(vnode);\\n    }\\n    const element = document.createElement(vnode.tag);\\n    // Setting the attributes (the props)\\n    Object.keys(vnode.props).forEach(key => {\\n        element.setAttribute(key, vnode.props[key]);\\n    });\\n    // Rendering the children recursively\\n    vnode.children.forEach(child => {\\n        element.appendChild(render(child));\\n    });\\n    return element;\\n}\\n\\n// Usage: we create the VDOM tree\\nconst vdomTree = createElement('div', { id: 'app' },\\n    createElement('h1', null, 'Hello, VDOM!'),\\n    createElement('p', { class: 'text' }, 'This is an example.')\\n);\\n// We turn it into a real DOM and add it to the page\\ndocument.body.appendChild(render(vdomTree));\"],[2317,\"In React functional components, access to the moments of mounting and unmounting is gained by means of the useEffect hook. If an empty array of dependencies [] is passed, the effect will run only on mounting. The function returned from useEffect will run when the component unmounts and serves for clean-up. In class components the componentDidMount and componentWillUnmount methods are used for this.\",\"In React components pass through various stages of their lifecycle, and the key moments are their mounting (the first render into the DOM) and their unmounting (removal from the DOM). To perform operations at those moments — subscribing to events, making API requests or clearing timers — special methods or hooks are used.\\n\\nFunctional components (with hooks)\\nWith the arrival of hooks, the standard way is to use `useEffect`. Its logic is as follows:\\n- The effect runs after React has updated the DOM.\\n- If an empty array `[]` is passed as the second argument, the effect will run only once — after the first render (the mounting).\\n- The function the effect returns is the clean-up function. React will call it immediately before the component unmounts.\\n\\nClass components\\nIn class components special lifecycle methods are used for this:\\n- `componentDidMount()` is called immediately after the component has mounted.\\n- `componentWillUnmount()` is called immediately before the component is unmounted and removed.\\n\\nThis approach is used to manage side effects that should exist only while the component is displayed on the screen. The typical use cases: subscriptions to external services, event listeners, timers and data requests that can be cancelled.\\n\\nIn summary: use the `useEffect` hook with an empty array of dependencies and a returned clean-up function in functional components in order to handle mounting and unmounting. That is necessary in order to prevent memory leaks and for the application to work properly as the interface updates dynamically.\",\"import React, { useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The code here runs on mounting\\n    console.log('The component has mounted');\\n    const timerId = setInterval(() => console.log('Tick'), 1000);\\n\\n    // The returned function is the cleanup for the unmounting\\n    return () => {\\n      console.log('The component is about to unmount');\\n      clearInterval(timerId); // Clearing the timer\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies\\n\\n  return <div>An example</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nclass MyClassComponent extends React.Component {\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    console.log('The component has mounted');\\n    this.timerId = setInterval(() => console.log('Tick'), 1000);\\n  }\\n\\n  componentWillUnmount() {\\n    console.log('The component is about to unmount');\\n    clearInterval(this.timerId);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>A class example</div>;\\n  }\\n}\"],[2318,\"Cleaning side effects up in React matters in order to prevent memory leaks and bugs. Side effects such as event subscriptions or timers go on running after the component has been removed from the DOM, unless they are stopped. The useEffect hook lets you return a clean-up function, which runs before the component unmounts or before the next effect. That ensures resources are released properly and the application is stable.\",\"In React components can create side effects, such as API requests, event subscriptions, timers or manual changes to the DOM. These operations are often performed inside the `useEffect` hook. If the component is removed from the DOM (unmounted) while the effect goes on running, that can lead to memory leaks, to bugs in the logic (an attempt to update the state of a component that no longer exists, for example) or to an unnecessary load on the system.\\n\\nHow the clean-up works\\nThe `useEffect` hook lets you return a cleanup function. React calls that function automatically in two cases:\\n- Before running the same effect on the next render (if the dependencies have changed).\\n- Before the component unmounts (when it is removed from the DOM).\\n\\nThis mechanism guarantees that the resources created by the effect will be released in good time.\\n\\nExamples of the code\\nLet us look at an example with a subscription to the browser window's `resize` event:\\n\\nWithout the clean-up function, every time the component unmounted the event listener would stay active, causing unnecessary updates and potential memory leaks.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nCleaning effects up is critically important for:\\n- Cancelling network requests (by means of an `AbortController`, for example).\\n- Stopping intervals (`setInterval`) and timeouts (`setTimeout`).\\n- Closing WebSocket connections or subscriptions to external stores (RxJS, for example).\\n- Cancelling any asynchronous operations that might try to update the state after the unmount.\\n\\nConclusion: always return a clean-up function in `useEffect` if the effect creates resources that live longer than the component's lifecycle. It is an essential practice for writing reliable React applications, especially in SPAs, where components mount and unmount often.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction WindowSize() {\\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The side effect: a subscription to an event\\n    const handleResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);\\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function: unsubscribing from the event\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect runs on mounting only\\n\\n  return <div>The window's width: {width}px</div>;\\n}\"],[2319,\"A custom hook for working with an eventListener lets you manage the subscription to DOM events centrally. It uses useEffect to add and remove the listener, and useRef to keep a stable reference to the callback function. That prevents memory leaks and ensures a proper clean-up when the component unmounts. The hook takes the type of event, the handler and an optional target element.\",\"Custom hooks in React are functions that encapsulate logic with state and side effects for reuse. A hook for working with an eventListener is a typical example, since subscribing to browser events is a side effect that requires clean-up.\\n\\nThe hook's main logic\\nThe hook should take three arguments: the type of event ('click', for example), the handler (a callback function) and an optional element to which the listener will be attached (the window object by default). Inside the hook, useEffect is used in order to manage the subscription's lifecycle.\\n\\nThe key aspects of the implementation\\n\\n- Use useRef to keep the handler. That lets you avoid resubscribing on every render if the handler has changed.\\n- In useEffect, add the listener by means of addEventListener.\\n- The clean-up function returned from useEffect should remove the listener by means of removeEventListener.\\n- useEffect's dependencies should include the type of event and the target element, so that the hook responds to their changing.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nUsing the hook\\nThis hook can be used in any functional component in order to subscribe to events such as clicks, scrolling, a change of window size or keyboard events. To track the pressing of the Escape key, for example:\\n\\nConclusion: a custom useEventListener hook is useful for centralizing the logic of working with DOM events, for ensuring a proper clean-up and for improving code reuse in React applications.\",\"import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useEventListener(eventType, handler, element = window) {\\n  // We keep the handler in a ref, so that it does not trigger the effect on every render\\n  const savedHandler = useRef();\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    savedHandler.current = handler;\\n  }, [handler]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // We check whether the element supports addEventListener\\n    const isSupported = element && element.addEventListener;\\n    if (!isSupported) return;\\n\\n    // We create a wrapper that calls the saved handler\\n    const eventListener = (event) => savedHandler.current(event);\\n\\n    // We add the listener\\n    element.addEventListener(eventType, eventListener);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function: we remove the listener\\n    return () => {\\n      element.removeEventListener(eventType, eventListener);\\n    };\\n  }, [eventType, element]); // The effect restarts when the type or the element changes\\n}\\n\\nexport default useEventListener;\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const handleEscape = (event) => {\\n    if (event.key === 'Escape') {\\n      console.log('Escape pressed');\\n      // Close the modal window and so on\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  useEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);\\n\\n  return <div>Press Escape</div>;\\n}\"],[2320,\"In React functional components, use the useEffect hook. The function returned from useEffect runs when the component unmounts. In it, call clearInterval, passing the interval's identifier that you saved when you created it. In class components, use the componentWillUnmount lifecycle method to call clearInterval. It is an essential practice for preventing memory leaks and bugs.\",\"In React components can create side effects such as timers, event subscriptions or API requests. If those effects are not cleaned up when the component is removed from the DOM (when it unmounts), they will go on running in the background, which can lead to memory leaks, to bugs (an attempt to update the state of a component that no longer exists, for example) and to the application behaving unexpectedly.\\n\\nClean-up in functional components (with hooks)\\nTo manage side effects in functional components the `useEffect` hook is used. The function passed to `useEffect` can return another function, which will be run before the effect's next call or when the component unmounts. That is the place for the clean-up.\\n\\nClean-up in class components\\nIn class components the `componentDidMount` method is normally used to create the interval, and the `componentWillUnmount` lifecycle method to clean it up.\\n\\nWhy this matters and where it is used\\nThis practice is critically important in any React application where components mount and unmount dynamically — for example:\\n- Pages with timers or countdowns.\\n- Components that poll the server periodically.\\n- Applications with routing (React Router), where the components change as the user moves between pages.\\n- Any components using `setInterval`, `setTimeout` or WebSocket subscriptions.\\n\\nIn summary: always clear intervals and timers in `useEffect`'s clean-up function or in the `componentWillUnmount` method. It is an essential rule for writing clean, safe, efficient React code, and it prevents serious bugs in an application's operation.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimerComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // We create the interval\\n    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function: it will run on unmounting\\n    return () => {\\n      clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect runs on mounting only\\n\\n  return <div>The count: {count}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nclass TimerComponent extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.state = { count: 0 };\\n    this.intervalId = null;\\n  }\\n\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    this.intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n      this.setState(prevState => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));\\n    }, 1000);\\n  }\\n\\n  componentWillUnmount() {\\n    // We clear the interval on unmounting\\n    clearInterval(this.intervalId);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>The count: {this.state.count}</div>;\\n  }\\n}\"],[2321,\"If setInterval is used in useEffect without a clean-up, the interval will go on running after the component has unmounted, which can cause a memory leak and errors when the state of a component that no longer exists is addressed. It is also important to state the dependencies in useEffect's dependency array properly, so that the interval is not recreated on every render. To work with the current state inside a setInterval you have to use refs or the functional form of the state update.\",\"Using `setInterval` inside `useEffect` in React calls for particular attention to the component's lifecycle and to the management of side effects. The main difficulty is that the interval goes on running in the background even if the component that created it has been removed from the screen (unmounted).\\n\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- A memory leak: if the interval is not stopped when the component unmounts, it will go on running, taking up resources and potentially causing a memory leak.\\n- Addressing stale state: the callback passed to `setInterval` \\\"captures\\\" the values of the variables (the state included) at the moment the interval was created. That means inside the interval you will be working with the old (closed-over) values rather than the current ones.\\n- The uncontrolled creation of intervals: if you forget to state the empty array of dependencies `[]` in `useEffect`, the hook will run on every render, creating new intervals, which will quickly lead to a collapse in performance.\\n\\nThe correct pattern with a clean-up\\nThe key rule is always to return a cleanup function from `useEffect`. That function will run before the component unmounts or before the effect's next call.\\n\\nWorking with the current state\\nTo have access to the state's latest value inside the interval, you can use a ref (`useRef`) or the functional form of the state update (`setState(prev => prev + 1)`). The functional form guarantees that you are working with the current previous value, which React passes into the updater function.\\n\\nThis approach is used to implement periodic actions in an interface: updating data (a stock ticker, for example), creating timers, polling the server, or any other logic that has to run at a given interval.\\n\\nConclusion: always clear your intervals in `useEffect`'s clean-up function and use functional state updates or refs in order to work with the current data. This pattern is necessary for building reliable React components with timers, and it avoids memory leaks and unexpected behaviour.\",\"useEffect(() => {\\n  const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log('The timer fired');\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  // The clean-up function\\n  return () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\\n  };\\n}, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect runs on mounting only\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n    // We use a functional update in order to get the current count\\n    setCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  return () => clearInterval(intervalId);\\n}, []);\"],[2322,\"Using one and the same function reference matters in order to prevent unnecessary re-renders in React and in other frameworks. If the function is created anew on every render, React treats it as a new dependency, which can cause effects (useEffect) to fire or child components to repaint. That leads to reduced performance. To keep the reference, useCallback is used or the function is moved outside the component.\",\"In JavaScript functions are objects, and every call that creates a new function (`() => {}` or `function() {}`, for example) produces a new object in memory with a new reference. This is a fundamental behaviour of the language that directly affects how modern UI frameworks work, React especially.\\n\\nThe problem with new references\\nLet us look at a typical React component:\\n\\nOn every render of `MyComponent` a new `handleClick` function is created. For the React component `ChildComponent`, which uses `React.memo` or `shouldComponentUpdate`, the `onClick` prop will be a new object every time, which will lead to an unnecessary re-render of the child component even if its state has not changed.\\n\\nHow this affects the hooks\\nThe `useEffect` hook relies on its array of dependencies. If you pass a function into that array and it is created anew on every render, the effect will run every time, which is often a mistake.\\n\\nThe solution: keeping the reference\\nTo keep the references stable people use:\\n - useCallback: it caches the function between renders until the dependencies given change.\\n - Moving it outside the component: if the function does not depend on props or state, it can be declared outside the component.\\n - useRef: you can keep a stable reference to a function that can change.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nThis principle is critically important in React for optimizing the performance of memoized components (`React.memo`, `PureComponent`), for the hooks (`useEffect`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`) to work properly, and when passing callbacks down the component tree. It is also relevant in other contexts — when unsubscribing from events, for example, where you have to pass the same function you used when subscribing.\\n\\nConclusion: keeping a function's reference is a key optimization for preventing unnecessary computation and re-renders in reactive applications. It is worth applying whenever a function is passed as a prop into optimized child components or used in a hook's array of dependencies.\",\"function MyComponent() {\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    console.log('Clicked!');\\n  };\\n\\n  return <ChildComponent onClick={handleClick} />;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // It runs on every render, if fetchData is new\\n  fetchData();\\n}, [fetchData]); // fetchData is a new reference every time\\n\\nconst fetchData = () => {\\n  // Fetching the data\\n};\\n\\n// The solution with useCallback\\nconst handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n  console.log('Clicked!');\\n}, []); // An empty array — the function is created once\\n\\n// Moving it outside the component\\nfunction stableLog() {\\n  console.log('Stable!');\\n}\\nfunction Component() {\\n  return <button onClick={stableLog}>Click</button>;\\n}\"],[2323,\"useRef returns a mutable object whose .current property can be changed. Changing .current does not cause the component to re-render, because React does not track changes inside a ref object for the purposes of updating the UI. Unlike useState, a call to setState marks the component as needing a re-render. useRef is intended for holding values that have to be preserved between renders but do not affect the visual output.\",\"The `useRef` hook in React creates a mutable object that exists throughout the component's whole lifecycle. Its main purpose is to hold values that have to be preserved between renders but whose change must not start a repeated render of the component.\\n\\nHow useRef works\\nWhen `useRef(initialValue)` is called, React returns an object `{ current: initialValue }`. That object stays the same (it keeps its referential identity) across every subsequent render of the component. You can read and write the value in its `.current` property. Since React does not track changes to that object as a dependency for effects (unlike state), updating `.current` does not lead to a new render being scheduled.\\n\\nA comparison with useState\\n\\n- useState: changing the state through the set function tells React that the component's data has changed and the UI may need updating. React marks the component as \\\"dirty\\\" and performs a re-render.\\n- useRef: a ref object is simply a container for an arbitrary value. React does not include it in its reactivity system. Changing `.current` is a mutation of an ordinary JavaScript object, which does not start React's update mechanisms.\\n\\nPractical use and an example\\n`useRef` is often used for:\\n- Holding references to DOM elements (through the ref attribute).\\n- Keeping the values of timers (setInterval/setTimeout).\\n- Caching previous values or computations that must not trigger a re-render.\\n- Holding mutable flags or the instances of third-party libraries.\\n\\nIn the example `renderCount.current` increases on every render, but that does not create an infinite loop, since changing a ref causes no new render. The timer is held in `intervalRef`, which lets you clear it from anywhere in the component.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `useRef` when you need to hold a mutable value that has to \\\"survive\\\" the component's renders but must not affect its visual presentation. It is ideal for side effects, for integration with an imperative API (the DOM, for example) or for avoiding unnecessary re-renders when state is not required.\",\"import { useRef, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const renderCount = useRef(0); // It causes no re-render\\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  renderCount.current += 1; // A mutation! With no re-render.\\n\\n  const startTimer = () => {\\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1); // setCount will cause a re-render\\n    }, 1000);\\n  };\\n\\n  const stopTimer = () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <p>The component has rendered {renderCount.current} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={startTimer}>Start</button>\\n      <button onClick={stopTimer}>Stop</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2324,\"useEffect runs asynchronously after the browser has painted the changes on the screen. useLayoutEffect, by contrast, runs synchronously immediately after the changes in the DOM have been computed but before the browser paints them. That makes useLayoutEffect ideal for DOM operations that have to be finished before the visual update, so as to avoid flicker. In most cases useEffect is enough for data requests or subscriptions. Use useLayoutEffect only if the effect changes the styles or the layout directly and you see unwanted flicker.\",\"In React the `useEffect` and `useLayoutEffect` hooks let you perform side effects in functional components, but the key difference lies in when they run relative to the browser's painting phase.\\n\\nuseEffect: asynchronous execution\\nThe `useEffect` hook schedules the function it is given to run after the component has rendered and the browser has already painted the changes on the screen. That happens asynchronously and does not block the interface's update. It is ideal for most side effects, such as:\\n- API data requests.\\n- Setting up and cleaning up subscriptions (to events, for example).\\n- Manual state changes that do not have to be reflected in the DOM instantly.\\n\\nuseLayoutEffect: synchronous execution before the paint\\nThe `useLayoutEffect` hook runs synchronously immediately after React has computed every change to the DOM (the \\\"commit\\\" phase) but before the browser paints those changes on the screen. That blocks the visual update until the effect has finished.\\nIts main use is DOM or style manipulations that have to be applied before the user sees the result. Without that, visible flicker or a jump in the content can occur.\\n\\nWhen to use which?\\n\\n- Use `useEffect` by default for most tasks: data requests, logging, subscriptions. It is safe and does not block the rendering.\\n- Use `useLayoutEffect` only when the effect changes the DOM's appearance directly (styles, position, dimensions) and you observe visual artefacts (an element being displayed in one place at first and then \\\"jumping\\\" to another, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: `useLayoutEffect` is a specialized tool for the synchronous DOM manipulations needed in order to prevent visual flicker. In 90% of cases the standard `useEffect` is enough, and it does not block the paint and gives better performance.\",\"// An example with useEffect: measuring an element after the paint\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // This code will run AFTER the user has seen the element\\n  const rect = elementRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();\\n  console.log('The dimensions after the paint:', rect);\\n}, [dependencies]);\\n\\n// An example with useLayoutEffect: measuring and changing a style synchronously\\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\\n  // This code will run BEFORE the user sees the element\\n  const button = buttonRef.current;\\n  const rect = button.getBoundingClientRect();\\n  // We change the style immediately, from the measurements\\n  if (rect.width > 200) {\\n    button.style.fontSize = '10px';\\n  }\\n}, [dependencies]);\"],[2325,\"The useLayoutEffect hook fires synchronously immediately after React has computed the changes to the DOM, but before the browser displays (\\\"paints\\\") those changes on the screen. That lets you perform operations that require the DOM to be measured (getting an element's dimensions, for example) or the DOM to be manipulated synchronously before the paint, so as to avoid visual \\\"flicker\\\". useEffect, by contrast, fires asynchronously after the browser has painted.\",\"In React the `useEffect` and `useLayoutEffect` hooks serve to perform side effects in functional components, but the moment at which they run in the lifecycle differs, which is critical in certain scenarios.\\n\\nThe sequence of execution\\nWhen React updates a component, the process can be represented in simplified form like this:\\n - The component renders (the component's function is called and the React elements are created).\\n - The new and the old Virtual DOM are compared (reconciliation).\\n - The changes are committed to the real DOM (the commit phase).\\n - `useLayoutEffect` is called synchronously.\\n - The browser paints — the pixels appear on the screen.\\n - `useEffect` is called asynchronously, after the paint.\\n\\nThe practical uses of useLayoutEffect\\nThe main use of `useLayoutEffect` is operations that depend on the DOM's layout and have to be finished before the user sees the updated interface. The typical examples:\\n - Measuring a DOM element's dimensions or position (by means of `getBoundingClientRect`, for example).\\n - Changing an element's styles synchronously (animation or the positioning of a tooltip, for example), so as to avoid visual jerkiness.\\n - Scrolling programmatically to a particular element.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nSuppose we need to measure an element's width after the render and update the state from it:\\n\\nImportant remarks\\nSince `useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously and blocks the browser's paint, it should be used only when it really is necessary in order to prevent visual artefacts. For most side effects (data requests, event subscriptions) the asynchronous `useEffect` is enough, and it does not block the paint and gives better performance.\\n\\nConclusion: use `useLayoutEffect` in those rare cases where you need to measure or change the DOM synchronously immediately after the component has updated but before the browser displays the changes on the screen, in order to avoid visual flicker. For every other effect the standard `useEffect` is preferable.\",\"import { useLayoutEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MeasurableComponent() {\\n  const ref = useRef(null);\\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useLayoutEffect(() => {\\n    // This code will run BEFORE the browser paints.\\n    if (ref.current) {\\n      const measuredWidth = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect().width;\\n      setWidth(measuredWidth); // A synchronous state update\\n      // If we had used useEffect, the user might have\\n      // seen the element at its original size and then its change.\\n    }\\n  }, []); // The dependencies\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div ref={ref}>\\n      <p>This element's width: {width}px</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2326,\"Memoizing functions in hooks such as useCallback and useMemo can lead to problems if it is applied unnecessarily. The main problems include excessive complexity of the code, a false sense of optimization when the function is cheap anyway, and potential bugs from incorrect dependencies. Excessive memoization can also increase memory consumption, since the cached values are kept until the dependencies change. It is important to memoize only expensive computations or functions passed down to child components, in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders.\",\"Memoization in React by means of the `useCallback` and `useMemo` hooks is a powerful tool for optimizing performance, but using it incorrectly can have the opposite effect. Let us look at the key problems that can arise.\\n\\n1. Excessive complexity and premature optimization\\nDevelopers often wrap every function or computation in a `useCallback`/`useMemo`, thinking that will improve performance. For simple operations, however, the overhead of the memoization (comparing the dependencies, holding things in memory) can exceed the benefit. That complicates the code to no real purpose.\\n\\n2. Incorrect dependencies\\nOne of the most frequent mistakes is an incorrect array of dependencies. If you forget to include a variable the function depends on, the memoized value will go stale, which will lead to bugs. For example:\\n\\nHere `fetchData` will always refer to the original `userId`, which will cause bugs when the prop changes.\\n\\n3. Problems with memory and leaks\\nMemoized values are held in memory until the dependencies change. If you memoize a great many large objects or functions, that can increase memory consumption. In the worst case, if the dependencies change often, the memoization becomes useless but the memory is spent all the same.\\n\\n4. A false sense of security about re-renders\\n`useCallback` does not prevent a function from being created — it returns a memoized reference. If you pass that function into a child component wrapped in `React.memo`, that will help you avoid unnecessary re-renders. But if the child component is not memoized or the function is used in another way, the optimization will not take effect.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a component that filters a list:\\n\\nHere the memoization is useful, since the filtering can be expensive for large lists and we avoid recomputing it on every render as long as `users` and `searchTerm` have not changed.\\n\\nConclusion: memoizing functions is worth applying deliberately: for expensive computations, for functions passed into memoized child components, or when you need to keep a reference stable (in effects, for example). In every other case it can add unnecessary complexity and even harm performance.\",\"const MyComponent = ({ userId }) => {\\n  const fetchData = useCallback(() => {\\n    // userId is not included in the dependencies\\n    api.fetch(userId);\\n  }, []); // An empty array — the function will never be updated\\n  // ...\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useState, useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nconst UserList = ({ users, searchTerm }) => {\\n  const filteredUsers = useMemo(() => {\\n    console.log('Filtering...');\\n    return users.filter(user => \\n      user.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())\\n    );\\n  }, [users, searchTerm]); // The right dependencies\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {filteredUsers.map(user => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// If the array of dependencies had been [users], then when searchTerm changed\\n// the list would not have updated, which is a bug.\"],[2327,\"React repaints a component when its state or the props it has received change. It creates a new virtual DOM tree and compares it against the previous one (the reconciliation process). React then computes the minimal set of changes and applies them to the real DOM. That lets it update the interface efficiently.\",\"Updating (re-rendering) in React is the process in which a component recreates its JSX representation in response to changes. The main triggers for that are a change in the component's internal state (through `setState` or the `useState` hook) or the receipt of new props from the parent component.\\n\\nHow a repaint is started\\nWhen a state-update function is called (`setCount`, for example), React marks the component as needing an update. That does not mean the DOM changes instantly. React schedules the update and performs it later, usually within one event-loop cycle, which lets it group several updates for efficiency.\\n\\nThe reconciliation process\\nOnce the component has been marked for an update, React calls its render function (the render method or the functional component's body). That function returns a new tree of React elements (the virtual DOM). React then compares that new tree against the previous one, held in memory. This process is called reconciliation.\\n\\nThe key stages of reconciliation\\n\\n - The diffing algorithm: React compares the elements at each level of the tree recursively. If an element's type (`<div>` or another component, for example) has changed, React destroys the old subtree and builds a new one.\\n - Keys: when rendering lists, keys help React identify which elements have been changed, added or removed. That is critically important to performance.\\n - Applying the changes: once the difference (the diff) has been computed, React makes the minimum necessary changes to the browser's real DOM tree.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nLet us look at a simple counter component:\\n\\nEvery click on the button calls `setCount`, which leads to a change of state. React schedules an update of the `Counter` component, runs its body again, compares the result against the previous virtual DOM and updates only the text inside the `<p>` tag in the real DOM.\\n\\nOptimizing the repaints\\nNot every change of state or props ought to lead to the whole subtree being repainted. For optimization the following are used:\\n - `React.memo`, for memoizing functional components.\\n - `useMemo` and `useCallback`, for memoizing values and functions, in order to prevent unnecessary updates of child components.\\n - The proper use of keys in lists.\\n\\nConclusion: React's re-render mechanism, based on the virtual DOM and on reconciliation, gives a declarative, efficient way of updating a user interface. Understanding this process is necessary for writing performant React applications and for optimizing components properly.\",\"import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // This function causes a re-render\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // A change of state -> an update is scheduled\\n  };\\n\\n  // The component's body runs on every re-render\\n  console.log('The component has rendered, count =', count);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2328,\"In React functional components the equivalent of `componentWillUnmount` is the clean-up function returned from the `useEffect` hook. The `useEffect` hook lets you perform side effects. If you return a function from its callback, React will run it when the component unmounts. It is the ideal place for unsubscribing from events, cancelling timers or cleaning resources up.\",\"In React class components the `componentWillUnmount` method is used to clean resources up before the component is removed from the DOM. In functional components using hooks, that logic is implemented by means of the `useEffect` hook.\\n\\nHow useEffect works with a clean-up\\nThe `useEffect` hook takes two arguments: an effect function and an optional array of dependencies. If the effect function returns another function, React runs that returned function in order to clean up. The clean-up happens in two cases: immediately before the same effect runs again (if the dependencies have changed) and when the component unmounts.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a component that subscribes to an external event — to a change in the window's size, for example. On unmounting the subscription has to be removed, in order to avoid memory leaks.\\n\\nThe key points\\n\\n - An empty array of dependencies (`[]`) guarantees that the effect (and the clean-up) will run only once, on mounting/unmounting.\\n - An array with dependencies will make React perform the clean-up before every new run of the effect, if the dependencies have changed.\\n - The clean-up function has access to the closure (the values of the variables and the props) as of the moment the effect was created, which helps you avoid \\\"stale\\\" values.\\n\\nConclusion: use the function returned from `useEffect` for any clean-up logic (cancelling network requests, timers, subscriptions). It is the main and the only way of imitating `componentWillUnmount` in React functional components.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction WindowSizeTracker() {\\n  const [windowWidth, setWindowWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The effect: we subscribe to the event\\n    const handleResize = () => setWindowWidth(window.innerWidth);\\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function: the equivalent of componentWillUnmount\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n      console.log('The EventListener has been removed, the component is unmounting.');\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect runs on mounting only\\n\\n  return <div>The window's width: {windowWidth}px</div>;\\n}\"],[2329,\"The main frontend frameworks are React, Vue and Angular. React is a library for building UI with a declarative approach and a virtual DOM. Vue is a progressive framework that integrates easily and has a simple syntax. Angular is a fully fledged framework with TypeScript, built-in routing and DI. They differ in their architecture, their complexity and their ecosystem.\",\"Frontend frameworks are tools that help developers build complex, interactive web applications by managing the state, the rendering and the structure of the code. Their main aim is to improve productivity and the maintainability of the code compared with using plain JavaScript.\\n\\nThe key differences between the frameworks\\nLet us look at the three most popular solutions.\\n- React (a library from Meta): it is focused on building the user interface. It uses a component-based approach, JSX (a syntax mixing HTML and JavaScript) and a virtual DOM in order to update the real DOM efficiently. React gives great freedom, but it requires you to choose additional libraries for routing or state management.\\n- Vue (a progressive framework): developed by Evan You. It combines React's and Angular's approaches. It has a simple, intuitive template syntax, a reactive data system and a gradual learning curve. Vue can be used as a library for improving individual parts of a page, or as a fully fledged framework by means of Vue Router and Vuex.\\n- Angular (a platform from Google): this is a fully fledged framework for enterprise applications. It uses TypeScript by default and has built-in solutions for routing, an HTTP client, forms and dependency injection (DI). Its architecture is based on modules, components and services, which makes it strict and suited to large teams.\\n\\nAn example of the code: a simple component\\nLet us compare what a simple counter component looks like in each of the frameworks.\\n\\nAs the examples show, React and Vue use a more imperative approach to updating the state (calling a function), while Vue lets you change it directly in the template. Angular requires a stricter class structure and decorators.\\n\\nWhere they are used\\n\\n- React: ideal for dynamic SPAs, for mobile applications (React Native) and for projects where flexibility and a rich ecosystem are needed. It is widely used at Facebook, Instagram and Airbnb.\\n- Vue: excellent for fast prototyping, for the gradual migration of legacy projects, and for applications of medium complexity. It is popular among start-ups and in the Chinese IT community.\\n- Angular: the choice for large enterprise applications, where type safety, a strict architecture and a full set of tools out of the box matter. It is used at Google, Microsoft and IBM.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of framework depends on the scale of the project, on the team's experience and on the performance requirements. React offers flexibility, Vue simplicity and progressiveness, and Angular a comprehensive solution for large, complex applications.\",\"// React (using hooks)\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n        Click me\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Vue (the Composition API)\\nimport { ref } from 'vue';\\nexport default {\\n  setup() {\\n    const count = ref(0);\\n    return { count };\\n  },\\n  template: `\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {{ count }} times</p>\\n      <button @click=\\\"count++\\\">Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  `\\n}\\n\\n// Angular (TypeScript)\\nimport { Component } from '@angular/core';\\n@Component({\\n  selector: 'app-counter',\\n  template: `\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {{ count }} times</p>\\n      <button (click)=\\\"increment()\\\">Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  `\\n})\\nexport class CounterComponent {\\n  count = 0;\\n  increment() { this.count++; }\\n}\"],[2330,\"A hydration mismatch is an error that arises when the HTML generated on the server (SSR) does not match what React tries to paint on the client on the first load. React expects the DOM to be identical so that it can \\\"hydrate\\\" it by adding event handlers. The main causes: different times or data on the server and the client, the use of browser APIs (`window`, for example) on the server. To avoid one you have to synchronize the data, use `useEffect` for client-specific code and libraries such as `next/dynamic` for the lazy loading of components.\",\"A hydration mismatch is a critical error in React applications that use server-side rendering (SSR). It arises at the moment of \\\"hydration\\\", when React on the client tries to \\\"bring to life\\\" the static HTML received from the server by adding event handlers and state to it. If the DOM tree created by the client does not match the server's HTML bit for bit, React cannot match the nodes up properly, which leads to an error and to the whole subtree being repainted on the client, which negates SSR's advantages.\\n\\nThe main causes of a mismatch\\n\\n- Different data: the server and the client may use different sets of data for the render (because of caching or asynchronous requests, for example).\\n- Client-specific logic: addressing the `window`, `document` or `localStorage` objects directly while the component renders. Those objects do not exist on the server, so the result will differ.\\n- Different times: the generation of content that depends on the current time or on random values, without synchronization.\\n- Third-party libraries: some UI libraries can behave differently in the Node.js environment and in the browser.\\n\\nHow to avoid a mismatch\\nThe key principle is to guarantee that the component's initial render gives an absolutely identical result both on the server and on the client.\\n- Use `useEffect` for client-specific code: any operations requiring a browser API should be performed only after the component has mounted, inside `useEffect`. That rules out their affecting the server render.\\n- Synchronize the data: pass every piece of data needed for the initial render from the server to the client (through props or global state, for example). In Next.js `getServerSideProps` is often used for this.\\n- Control the rendering by means of state: for components that have to look different on the client, use an `isClient` flag, which is set to `true` only after mounting.\\n- Use the lazy loading of dynamic components: in Next.js, components that should not render on the server can be wrapped in `next/dynamic` with the option `ssr: false`.\\n\\nAn example of the code with a solution\\nLet us look at a component that shows the current time but has to avoid a mismatch.\\n\\nIn this example, on the server and on the very first paint on the client (during the hydration) the component will output \\\"Loading...\\\". Only after mounting will the state be updated in `useEffect` and the real time shown. Since the initial HTML is identical, no mismatch will happen.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA hydration mismatch is a problem of the integrity of the data and of the render logic between the server and the client. Avoiding one is necessary in order to keep SSR's advantages: the speed of the first load and SEO. The main approach is a clear separation of the code: everything that has to be in the initial HTML has to render identically, and any client-specific logic has to be isolated in a `useEffect` or initiated only after hydration.\",\"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimeComponent() {\\n  // 1. We initialize the state identically for the server and the client\\n  const [currentTime, setCurrentTime] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // 2. We run the browser-dependent code ONLY on the client\\n    setCurrentTime(new Date().toLocaleTimeString());\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = it runs only after mounting\\n\\n  // 3. On the server and on the first paint on the client we show a placeholder\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The current time: {currentTime || 'Loading...'}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nexport default TimeComponent;\"],[2331,\"With SSR (server-side rendering) the main problem is the load on the server and a slower Time to First Byte (TTFB), since the server generates the HTML for every request. With CSR (client-side rendering) the main problem is poor SEO optimization and a long initial load, because the browser has to download all of the JavaScript before painting the content. CSR can also suffer from problems with being indexed by search engines and with the display in social networks. A hybrid approach (static generation or partial hydration, for example) is often used in order to strike a balance.\",\"Server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR) are the two main strategies for displaying web content, each with its own unique problems. Understanding those problems helps architects and developers choose the right approach for their project.\\n\\nThe problems of server-side rendering (SSR)\\nWith SSR the HTML page is generated on the server and sent to the client ready to be displayed. That creates several difficulties:\\n- A heavy load on the server: every page request requires the server's computing resources for the render. With high traffic that can require the server infrastructure to be scaled.\\n- A slower Time to First Byte (TTFB): the user waits longer for the first portion of data from the server, since the server has to perform the render before sending its response.\\n- Difficulty with dynamic content: pages that depend heavily on the user's state or on real time can require constant re-renders on the server, which is inefficient.\\n- Problems with hydration in an SPA: if after the SSR the application becomes a single-page application (SPA), the process of \\\"hydration\\\" (binding the JavaScript to the static HTML) can be complicated and can lead to a mismatch between the server's and the client's DOM tree.\\n\\nThe problems of client-side rendering (CSR)\\nWith CSR the server sends an almost empty HTML file and a large JavaScript bundle, which then renders the interface in the user's browser. That brings other problems:\\n- Poor SEO: search bots may not run the JavaScript, or may run it with a delay, which leads to the content being indexed badly.\\n- A slow initial load: the user sees a blank page until all of the JavaScript has loaded and run. That is especially critical on slow connections.\\n- Problems with social shares: when someone tries to share a link, social networks (Facebook, Twitter) may not receive the meta tags that are inserted dynamically by the JavaScript.\\n- A dependence on the browser's capabilities: if the user has JavaScript disabled, the application will not work.\\n\\nAn example of the code and a comparison\\nLet us look at a simple React component that displays a list of posts. With CSR it might look like this:\\n\\nTo implement SSR by means of Next.js, the same component can be pre-rendered on the server:\\n\\nConclusion: SSR is worth applying to content-oriented sites, where SEO and the speed of the first paint are critical, whereas CSR suits complex web applications with rich interactivity after the load, where SEO is not a priority. Modern frameworks often offer hybrid solutions (static generation, incremental static regeneration, partial hydration) in order to minimize the disadvantages of both approaches.\",\"// The pure CSR approach (React)\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch('/api/posts')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => setPosts(data));\\n  }, []);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The blog</h1>\\n      {posts.length === 0 ? (\\n        <p>Loading...</p>\\n      ) : (\\n        <ul>{posts.map(post => <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>)}</ul>\\n      )}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// On the first request the server will send almost empty HTML.\\n// The content will appear only once the fetch has run in the browser.\\n\\n// The SSR approach with Next.js (getServerSideProps)\\nexport async function getServerSideProps() {\\n  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts');\\n  const posts = await res.json();\\n  return { props: { posts } };\\n}\\nfunction Blog({ posts }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The blog</h1>\\n      <ul>{posts.map(post => <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>)}</ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// The server will render the HTML with the posts and send it to the client.\"],[2332,\"Server-side rendering (SSR) generates the HTML on the server and sends a ready-made page to the browser, which improves SEO and the time to the first paint. Client-side rendering (CSR) loads minimal HTML and JavaScript, which then builds the interface in the browser, giving fast transitions after the load. To reconcile them, hydration is used, where React 'brings to life' the static HTML received from the server. The choice of approach depends on the requirements for SEO, for loading speed and for interactivity.\",\"Server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR) are two fundamental approaches to displaying web content, each with its own trade-offs. SSR means the server generates a complete HTML document for every request and sends it to the client. It is the traditional way, used in multi-page applications. CSR, characteristic of single-page applications (SPAs), loads the page's skeleton and a JavaScript bundle, which then renders the interface in the browser dynamically, managing the routing and the state on the client side.\\n\\nThe key differences and their consequences\\n\\n- SEO and indexing: search bots crawl HTML generated by the server (SSR) easily. With CSR the initial HTML is often empty, which makes indexing harder, although modern bots can run JavaScript.\\n- Perceived performance: SSR gives a fast display of the first content (FCP), since the user sees the HTML immediately. CSR may show a loading indicator until a large JS bundle has loaded and run.\\n- Interactivity: once loaded, CSR gives smooth, fast transitions without the page reloading. With SSR every navigation requires a new request to the server, although that can be optimized.\\n- The load on the server: SSR creates a load on the server for every render, which can require scaling. CSR shifts the computational load onto the client's devices.\\n\\nThe modern hybrid approaches and hydration\\nFrameworks such as Next.js offer hybrid models. You can use static generation (SSG) for pages that do not change often, for example, and SSR for dynamic data. The key process is hydration. The server sends pre-rendered HTML. Then the same React JavaScript code is loaded, which 'picks up' (hydrates) that DOM, binding the event handlers and making the page interactive.\\n\\nAn example of the code with Next.js (the App Router)\\n\\nIn this example `HomePage` is rendered on the server with its data, which gives good SEO and a fast load. The `ClientCounter` component, marked with the 'use client' directive, is hydrated on the client and adds the interactivity.\\n\\nPractical recommendations for handling the differences\\n\\n- Use SSR or SSG for public, SEO-critical pages (landing pages, blogs, catalogues).\\n- Apply CSR to admin panels or to the heavily interactive parts of an application, where SEO does not matter.\\n- For universal applications use frameworks (Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit) that abstract the complexity away and let you mix the approaches.\\n- Allow for \\\"flicker\\\" during hydration: if the client's initial state (the theme, for example) does not match the server's render, the page may flash. The solution is synchronization through scripts or holding the state in cookies.\\n- Avoid manipulating the DOM directly in components that may render on the server; use hooks such as `useEffect` or a check for `typeof window !== 'undefined'`.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice between SSR and CSR is a trade-off between the speed of the initial load, SEO and rich interactivity. The hybrid approaches with hydration that the modern meta-frameworks provide let you get the best of both worlds: a fast initial render for SEO and the subsequent smooth running of an SPA.\",\"// app/page.js — a server component by default in Next.js 13+\\nexport default async function HomePage() {\\n  // The data is requested on the server\\n  const data = await fetchData();\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The home page (SSR)</h1>\\n      <p>The data: {data.message}</p>\\n      {/* A client component for the interactivity */}\\n      <ClientCounter />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// app/components/ClientCounter.js\\n'use client'; // It states that this is a client component\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nexport default function ClientCounter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The counter: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2333,\"Visual testing is a method of checking that UI components look right. Tools such as Loki, Chromatic and Percy take screenshots of the components automatically and compare them against reference images. That helps you find visual bugs that may be missed in manual testing. Such tools are often integrated with Storybook in order to test components in isolation. They save time and increase confidence in the interface's stability.\",\"Visual regression testing is the practice of detecting unwanted changes in a user interface's appearance automatically. Unlike functional tests, which check the logic, visual tests focus on the pixels, revealing problems with the layout, the colours, the fonts or the placement of elements that may have appeared after a code update.\\n\\nThe main tools\\nSpecialized tools and services are used for this:\\n- Loki: an open-source tool for capturing screenshots of components from Storybook and comparing them. It works locally or in CI/CD.\\n- Chromatic: a cloud service from Storybook's creators. It publishes the Storybook automatically, takes screenshots and provides a convenient interface for reviewing the visual changes.\\n- Percy: a popular commercial service from BrowserStack. It integrates with a great many frameworks and performs a \\\"smart\\\" comparison of the screenshots, ignoring insignificant differences.\\n- Applitools Eyes: it uses AI for visual validation, which lets it find differences more accurately and reduces the number of false positives.\\n\\nHow it works\\nThe typical process looks like this: you create your components and describe their states in Storybook. The testing tool (Loki, for example) starts a browser, renders each story (a component's state), takes a screenshot and compares it against a previously saved reference. If the differences exceed the given threshold, the test fails and the developer is notified.\\n\\nAn example of setting Loki up\\nHere is a basic example of the configuration and the commands for Loki in a project with React and Storybook:\\n\\nAfter the first run of `npm run test:visual` the screenshots are saved as references. On subsequent runs the new screenshots are compared against them. If the changes are deliberate, they can be approved with the command `npm run approve:visual`.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nVisual testing is especially useful in large teams working on complex interfaces, where even a small change to the global styles can break the appearance of dozens of components. It is integrated into the CI/CD pipeline in order to block the merging of pull requests with visual regressions.\\n\\nConclusion: tools such as Loki are worth applying in projects with a developed library of UI components (especially using Storybook) in order to keep automatic control of the visual integrity and prevent regressions, which saves a great deal of time on checking the interface by hand.\",\"// package.json (a fragment of the scripts)\\n\\\"scripts\\\": {\\n  \\\"storybook\\\": \\\"start-storybook -p 6006\\\",\\n  \\\"build-storybook\\\": \\\"build-storybook\\\",\\n  \\\"test:visual\\\": \\\"loki test\\\",\\n  \\\"approve:visual\\\": \\\"loki approve\\\"\\n}\\n\\n// The configuration file .loki/config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  configurations: {\\n    'chrome.laptop': {\\n      target: 'chrome.docker',\\n      width: 1366,\\n      height: 768,\\n    },\\n  },\\n  fetchFailIgnore: 'localhost:6006',\\n};\"],[2334,\"For profiling a frontend, the browser's tools such as Chrome DevTools and specialized utilities are used. Chrome DevTools provides the Performance and Memory tabs for recording and analysing the rendering, the JavaScript execution and memory leaks. Lighthouse assesses the performance, the accessibility and the SEO, generating a report with recommendations. React DevTools profiles React components, showing their render times and unnecessary repaints. These tools help you find bottlenecks and improve the user experience.\",\"Profiling a frontend is the process of measuring and analysing a web application's performance in the browser, in order to identify bottlenecks such as slow JavaScript, unnecessary DOM repaints or memory leaks. It is necessary in order to ensure smooth interaction for the user and to meet the Core Web Vitals metrics.\\n\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- Chrome DevTools: the set of tools built into the Chrome browser. The key tabs:\\n- Performance: it records the timeline of the page's load and of the execution of the JS, the styles, the layout and the paint. It lets you find Long Tasks.\\n- Memory: it helps you detect memory leaks by taking heap snapshots and tracking the allocation of objects.\\n- Network: it analyses the loading times of the resources and the waterfall of requests, and it can simulate slow networks.\\n\\n- Lighthouse: an automated audit tool, built into DevTools or available as a CLI/Node module. It runs a series of tests and generates a report on performance, accessibility, best practices and SEO, giving specific recommendations for improvement.\\n- React DevTools: a browser extension. The Profiler tab lets you record the renders of React components, in order to see which components repaint, how long they take, and to identify unnecessary re-renders.\\n\\nPractical use and an example\\nSuppose you have a React application in which the interface \\\"hangs\\\" when you type in the search field. You can use a combination of tools to diagnose it.\\n\\nFirst start a recording in Chrome DevTools' Performance tab, type in the field and stop the recording. Look for long yellow bars (JavaScript execution) or purple ones (Layout/Reflow). Then, if the problem is in React, go to the React DevTools Profiler, start a recording and repeat the action. You will see which components render and how long they take.\\n\\nIt is also useful to run Lighthouse periodically in order to get an overall report on the application's health and to track the Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), First Input Delay (FID) and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) metrics.\\n\\nConclusion: frontend profiling tools (Chrome DevTools, Lighthouse, React DevTools) are necessary for finding and eliminating performance problems systematically. They are worth applying at the development stage, during regression testing and when preparing for a release, in order to make the application fast and responsive for its users.\",\"// An example of code that can cause a performance problem\\nfunction SearchComponent() {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  const filteredItems = hugeList.filter(item => \\n    item.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())\\n  ); // An expensive operation on every character typed\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />\\n      <ul>\\n        {filteredItems.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// Profiling will help you find that filtering hugeList on every onChange is the bottleneck.\\n// The solution: use useMemo to memoize filteredItems, or debounce the input.\"],[2335,\"The interaction between several SPAs can be organized through several approaches. The simplest is the use of iframes, where each application runs in an isolated context and they communicate through the postMessage API. The more modern way is a micro-frontend architecture, where the applications are loaded as independent modules and communicate through a global Event Bus or shared state. You can also use Web Components in order to create reusable widgets that are embedded in different SPAs. The choice of method depends on the requirements for isolation, for performance and on the complexity of the integration.\",\"When several SPA applications have to work together — in a portal or a complex control panel, for example — the key task is to ensure they can communicate and coordinate without the page reloading completely. The main approaches differ in their degree of coupling and in the complexity of the implementation.\\n\\nThe main approaches to integration\\n\\n- Iframe + postMessage: the classic method, which gives complete isolation. Each SPA is loaded in a separate iframe. For exchanging data the `window.postMessage` API is used.\\n- Micro frontends: an architectural style in which each SPA is an independent application, deployed separately. They can be loaded dynamically and communicate through a shared global object or an event bus.\\n- Web Components: they let you encapsulate functionality in reusable custom HTML elements that can be embedded in any SPA.\\n- Module Federation (Webpack 5): it lets one application load modules from another dynamically at run time, creating the feeling of a single application.\\n\\nAn example: communication through an Event Bus\\nLet us create a simple global event bus that every SPA on the page will use. That lets the applications publish and subscribe to events without knowing about one another directly.\\n\\nAn example: integration through an iframe\\nThis approach is good when you need to run a completely independent third-party application with maximum isolation.\\n\\nConclusion: for fast integration with maximum isolation, use an iframe. To create a single user experience out of independently developed parts, choose a micro-frontend architecture with a shared event bus or Module Federation. Web Components are suited to creating reusable UI components that have to work in different frameworks.\",\"// shared-event-bus.js\\n// This file is loaded onto the page before the SPAs are loaded\\nclass EventBus {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.listeners = {};\\n  }\\n\\n  on(event, callback) {\\n    if (!this.listeners[event]) this.listeners[event] = [];\\n    this.listeners[event].push(callback);\\n  }\\n\\n  emit(event, data) {\\n    if (this.listeners[event]) {\\n      this.listeners[event].forEach(cb => cb(data));\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// We create the global instance\\nwindow.globalEventBus = new EventBus();\\n\\n// In SPA App 1 (a notifications panel, for example)\\nwindow.globalEventBus.emit('userLoggedIn', { userId: 123 });\\n\\n// In SPA App 2 (a chat, for example)\\nwindow.globalEventBus.on('userLoggedIn', (data) => {\\n  console.log('We initialize the chat for the user:', data.userId);\\n  // The chat's initialization...\\n});\\n\\n// host-app.html (the main application)\"],[2336,\"A monolithic frontend is a single application in which all of the code is connected and deployed as one whole. Its main advantages are the simplicity of development, of debugging and of deployment at the early stages. Its main disadvantages are the difficulty of scaling for large teams and slow deployments, since the whole application has to be rebuilt on any change. Micro-frontends solve those problems by breaking the application into independent parts, but they add complexity in coordination and in setting the infrastructure up.\",\"A frontend application's architecture determines how the code is organized, how the teams work on it and how it is deployed. The two main approaches — monolithic and micro-frontends — offer different trade-offs.\\n\\nA monolithic frontend\\nIn a monolithic architecture the whole application (every page, component, piece of logic and style) sits in one repository, is built by a single build and is deployed as one whole. It is the classic approach, which was used in web development for decades.\\n- The advantages: the simplicity of the initial set-up and of development. A single codebase makes navigation, refactoring and ensuring the consistency of the styles and the behaviour easier. Debugging and testing are also easier, since everything runs in one context.\\n- The disadvantages: as the application and the team grow, the monolith becomes the bottleneck. Any change, even a small one, requires the whole application to be rebuilt and redeployed, which slows the delivery process down. It is harder for the teams to work independently, since their changes can conflict. The technology stack is fixed rigidly for the whole application.\\n\\nMicro-frontends\\nMicro-frontends are an architectural style in which a large application is broken into independent, autonomous parts (the micro-frontends), which can be developed, built and deployed by separate teams. Those parts are then integrated at the browser's or the server's level.\\n- The advantages: the teams' independence and faster development. The ability to use different technologies and frameworks in different parts of the application. A safer, faster deployment — updating one micro-frontend does not require the whole application to be rebuilt.\\n- The disadvantages: a considerable complication of the infrastructure: mechanisms are needed for building, integrating, routing and communication between the micro-frontends. The size of the final bundle grows because of duplicated dependencies. It is harder to ensure a uniform UI/UX and to debug the application as a whole.\\n\\nAn example of integrating micro-frontends\\nLet us look at a simple example of using Module Federation in Webpack in order to load a remote micro-frontend:\\n\\nIn this example the host application loads the \\\"products\\\" micro-frontend from another domain dynamically. That lets the \\\"products\\\" team develop and deploy their own part entirely independently.\\n\\nConclusion: a monolithic frontend is worth choosing for small projects or start-ups, where the speed of getting to market and ease of maintenance matter. Micro-frontends become justified for large, long-lived projects with several autonomous teams, where independence of development and the frequency of updates are critically important.\",\"// The host's configuration (the host app)\\n// webpack.config.js\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'host',\\n      remotes: {\\n        mf_products: 'products@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js',\\n      },\\n      shared: ['react', 'react-dom'], // The shared dependencies\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// The code in the host application for loading it dynamically\\nconst ProductsApp = React.lazy(() => import('mf_products/ProductsPage'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The main application</h1>\\n      <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n        <ProductsApp />\\n      </React.Suspense>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2337,\"Without Module Federation, micro-frontends are integrated at build time or on the server side. The main approaches: building every module into a single bundle (build-time), using iframes for isolation, composition on the server (SSI, Edge-Side Includes), or client-side routing with the loading of separate applications. These methods often require the whole application to be rebuilt in order to update it, and they create strong coupling or problems with performance and with sharing state.\",\"Micro-frontends are an architectural style in which a large web application is broken into independent, autonomous parts developed by different teams. Before tools such as Webpack Module Federation appeared, integrating those parts was more complicated and often created points of friction.\\n\\nThe main approaches without Module Federation\\n\\n- Build-time integration: the separate applications are built as packages (npm packages) and imported into the main \\\"container\\\" application as dependencies. To update any micro-frontend, the whole application has to be rebuilt and redeployed. That negates the independence of deployment.\\n- Server-side composition: the server (by means of Nginx, or a backend application) assembles HTML fragments from the various services. Technologies such as Server-Side Includes (SSI) or Edge-Side Includes (ESI) are used. The user receives a page that has already been assembled. That gives good performance but complicates the server logic and can create a dependence on the network.\\n- Integration through iframes: each micro-frontend is loaded in a separate iframe. That gives complete isolation of the CSS and the JavaScript, but it severely limits the interaction between the parts (complicated data exchange, problems with navigation and SEO) and creates overhead.\\n- Client-side integration through routing\\nIn this approach the \\\"container\\\" application is responsible for the routing. Depending on the URL, it loads and mounts the JavaScript bundle of the micro-frontend it needs dynamically. That can be done by loading the scripts systematically.\\n\\nThis method preserves the independence of deployment, but it requires careful management of the global namespace, of the libraries' versions (the problem of duplicate dependencies) and complicated coordination of the loading.\\n\\nThe problems and the limitations\\n\\n- Duplicated dependencies: each micro-frontend can include its own copies of React, Vue and so on, which increases the amount of code to be downloaded.\\n- The absence of a shared runtime: it is hard to share state or components between micro-frontends without workarounds (through the global window object, for example).\\n- The difficulty of updates: in the build-time approach, to update a button in one micro-frontend you have to rebuild the whole monolith.\\n- Problems with style isolation: one micro-frontend's CSS can affect the others, unless strict methodologies or iframes are used.\\n\\nConclusion: the classic approaches to micro-frontends let you achieve organizational decomposition and the independence of the teams, but they often sacrifice technical efficiency, performance or the simplicity of the integration. They are useful in scenarios where server-side assembly is critically important for SEO, or where the teams work on entirely isolated parts of an application with minimal interaction.\",\"// A simplified example: the container loads a micro-frontend's script\\nconst loadMicroFrontend = (name, scriptUrl) => {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    const script = document.createElement('script');\\n    script.src = scriptUrl;\\n    script.onload = () => {\\n      // It is assumed that the script exports a global function for rendering\\n      window[`render${name}`](`${name}-container`);\\n      resolve();\\n    };\\n    script.onerror = reject;\\n    document.head.appendChild(script);\\n  });\\n};\\n// On navigating to the route '/app1'\\nloadMicroFrontend('App1', 'http://cdn.example.com/app1.bundle.js');\"],[2338,\"Circular dependencies in FSD arise when modules from different layers or slices refer to one another directly or indirectly, creating a closed loop. To avoid them you have to observe the direction of the dependencies strictly: from the upper layers (shared, entities) to the lower ones (features, widgets, pages). Use dependency inversion through abstractions (interfaces) or each slice's public API. Check the project regularly with linters (madge, for example) in order to detect cycles at an early stage.\",\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for frontend applications, which organizes the code by business logic (the slices) and by technical layer. Circular dependencies break FSD's main rule — the unidirectional flow of data and of dependencies — which leads to difficulties in maintenance, in testing and in the reuse of code.\\n\\nThe causes and the consequences of cycles\\nA cycle arises when module A imports module B while module B, directly or through a chain of other modules, imports module A. In the context of FSD that can be between layers (a widget imports from a feature, for example, while that feature imports something from the same widget) or between the slices of one feature. It makes the code coupled, complicates refactoring and can lead to errors during initialization.\\n\\nThe main strategies for preventing them\\n\\n- Observe the direction of the layers: the dependencies should go only from the upper layers to the lower ones: `shared` → `entities` → `features` → `widgets` → `pages` → `app`. A lower layer may import from a higher one, but never the other way round.\\n- Use each slice's public API: import only through a slice's `index` file, rather than reaching into its internals. That makes the boundaries explicit and prevents accidental cross-imports.\\n- Apply dependency inversion: if two modules really do need each other, extract the shared contract (types, interfaces) into `shared` and have both depend on that abstraction rather than on one another.\\n- Check for cycles automatically: run a linter or a tool such as `madge` in CI, so that a cycle is caught the moment it appears rather than months later.\\n\\nConclusion: circular dependencies in FSD are a symptom of the layer rule being broken. Keeping the dependencies flowing in one direction, importing only through public APIs and extracting shared contracts into `shared` are what keep the architecture clean and maintainable.\",\"// WRONG: a circular dependency\\n// widgets/user-card/ui/Avatar.tsx\\nexport const Avatar = ({ src }) => <img src={src} alt=\\\"\\\" />;\\n\\n// widgets/user-card/ui/UserCard.tsx\\nimport { useUserData } from 'features/user-profile';\\nexport const UserCard = () => {\\n  const user = useUserData(); // An import from features\\n  return <Avatar src={user.avatar} />;\\n};\\n\\n// features/user-profile/lib/useUserData.ts\\nimport { Avatar } from 'widgets/user-card/ui/Avatar'; // The import back again!\\nexport const useUserData = () => {\\n  // ... logic using Avatar\\n};\\n\\n// RIGHT: we remove the cycle through the shared layer\\n// shared/ui/Avatar/Avatar.tsx\\nexport const Avatar = ({ src }) => <img src={src} alt=\\\"\\\" />;\\n\\n// widgets/user-card/ui/UserCard.tsx\\nimport { useUserData } from 'features/user-profile';\\nimport { Avatar } from 'shared/ui/Avatar';\\nexport const UserCard = () => {\\n  const user = useUserData();\\n  return <Avatar src={user.avatar} />;\\n};\\n\\n// features/user-profile/lib/useUserData.ts\\n// It no longer imports Avatar from widgets\\nimport { Avatar } from 'shared/ui/Avatar'; // If it is needed — an import from shared\\nexport const useUserData = () => {\\n  // ...\\n};\"],[2339,\"When useEffect's dependencies change, React compares the current values of the dependencies against the previous ones. If at least one value has changed, React runs the previous effect's clean-up function (if one was defined) and then runs the effect itself again. If the array of dependencies is empty, the effect runs only when the component mounts and unmounts. If no array of dependencies is passed, the effect runs on every render.\",\"The `useEffect` hook in React lets you perform side effects in functional components. The key element controlling its behaviour is the array of dependencies passed as its second argument.\\n\\nHow the comparison of the dependencies works\\nReact holds the values of the dependencies from the previous render. Before every render (repeated ones included) it performs a shallow comparison (`Object.is`) of each element of the array against its previous value. If at least one value has changed, the following sequence is triggered:\\n- The cleanup function from the effect's previous run is executed, if one was returned.\\n- The body of the effect itself (the synchronous function passed to `useEffect`) runs again.\\n\\nPractical examples of the behaviour\\nLet us look at a component that subscribes to the data of a user by ID.\\n\\nOn the first render with `userId=1` the effect will run and the subscription will be created. If on the next render `userId` stays 1, the effect will not run again. If `userId` changes to 2, React will first run the clean-up function (unsubscribing from user 1's data) and then run the effect again, subscribing to user 2's data.\\n\\nThe special cases of the array of dependencies\\n\\n- An empty array `[]`: the effect runs once after the component has mounted, and the clean-up runs when it unmounts. The equivalent of `componentDidMount` and `componentWillUnmount`.\\n- The absence of the array: the effect runs after every render of the component. That can lead to performance problems.\\n- Objects/arrays in the dependencies: since the comparison is shallow, a new object with the same contents counts as a change. That is a frequent cause of infinite loops.\\n\\nConclusion: the array of dependencies in `useEffect` is the tool for precise control over when a side effect is to run. Using it properly prevents unnecessary computation and memory leaks and keeps the data in a component current, synchronizing the effects with particular props or state.\",\"useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log('The effect is running for userId:', userId);\\n  const subscription = subscribeToUserData(userId);\\n\\n  // The clean-up function\\n  return () => {\\n    console.log('The clean-up for the previous userId:', userId);\\n    unsubscribeFromUserData(userId);\\n  };\\n}, [userId]); // The dependency: userId\"],[2340,\"A re-render in React is the process in which a component calls its render function again in order to update the user interface. It is started when the component's state changes or when it receives new props. React compares the result of the new render against the previous one (the virtual DOM) and makes the minimum necessary changes to the real DOM. By default a re-render of a parent component causes a re-render of all of its children.\",\"A re-render is React's key mechanism for updating a user interface in response to changes in the data. It does not always mean the browser's real DOM tree is manipulated directly, which is what makes it efficient.\\n\\nWhat starts a re-render?\\n\\n- A change in the component's state through `setState` (or the `useState` hook).\\n- A change in the props passed to the component by its parent.\\n- A change in a Context the component is subscribed to.\\n- A re-render of the parent component (if no optimization measures have been taken).\\n\\nThe re-render process\\nWhen a re-render starts, React calls the component's render function (the render method or the functional component's body). That function returns a new description of the UI from the JSX, which consists of React element objects (the virtual DOM). React then starts the reconciliation process, comparing the new tree of elements against the previous one. It determines which parts of the tree really have changed and computes the minimal set of operations for updating the real DOM. That makes the updates fast, even if the application is large.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nOptimizing the re-renders\\nTo avoid unnecessary re-renders and improve performance, the following are used:\\n- `React.memo`, for memoizing functional components.\\n- `useMemo` and `useCallback`, for memoizing values and functions.\\n- Structuring the state properly, so that changes touch only the components that need them.\\n\\nIn summary: understanding re-rendering is critically important to writing performant React applications. It should be applied deliberately, using optimizations in order to prevent unnecessary updates of components that have not in fact changed.\",\"import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // The state\\n\\n  // This function starts a re-render on every call\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // A change of state -> a re-render\\n  };\\n\\n  // This call happens on every re-render\\n  console.log('Counter rendered, count:', count);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The parent component\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [message, setMessage] = useState('Hello');\\n  // A change to message will cause a re-render of App, and then of Counter too,\\n  // since Counter is its child component.\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Counter />\\n      <button onClick={() => setMessage('Updated')}>Update Parent</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2341,\"In React, state — normally an array — is used in order to add and remove the items of a list. To add one, a new array is created containing the old items and the new one, by means of the spread operator or concat. To remove one, filter is used, which returns an array without the item being removed. Once the state has been changed through setState or the state-update function (in functional components), React repaints the component with the updated list automatically. The key point is to avoid mutating the original array, so that React tracks the changes properly.\",\"In React the management of dynamic lists is one of the fundamental tasks. Since React relies on a declarative approach, you describe what the UI should look like given the state, and React takes care of updating the DOM itself. To work with lists, the state holds an array of data and the rendering is done by means of the map method.\\n\\nThe basic approach with state\\nIn a functional component, use the useState hook to hold the array of items. Initialize it with an empty array or with some initial data.\\n\\nAdding an item\\nTo add an item, create a new array containing every existing item and the new one. Never modify the original array with the push or splice methods directly, since React will not detect the change. Use the spread operator or concat instead.\\n\\nRemoving an item\\nTo remove an item by its index or by a unique identifier (an id, for example), use the filter method, which returns a new array without the item in question.\\n\\nRendering the list\\nTo display the list, use map, giving each item a unique key (normally the id, or the index if the items are static). The key helps React update the DOM efficiently.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nThis pattern is used everywhere in interfaces: to-do lists, shopping carts, news feeds, data tables. It is important to remember the immutability of the state — always create new arrays or objects when updating, so that React determines the changes properly and optimizes the re-renders.\\n\\nConclusion: use useState to hold the array, the spread operator or concat to add, filter to remove and map to render. This approach gives a predictable update of the UI and is the standard in React development.\",\"import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TodoList() {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState(['Item 1', 'Item 2']);\\n  // ... the add/remove logic\\n}\\n\\nconst addItem = (newItem) => {\\n  setItems([...items, newItem]); // The spread operator\\n  // Or: setItems(items.concat(newItem));\\n};\\n\\n// An example call: addItem('Item 3');\\n\\nconst removeItem = (id) => {\\n  setItems(items.filter(item => item.id !== id));\\n};\\n\\n// If there is no id, you can remove by index (less reliable):\\n// const removeItemByIndex = (index) => {\\n//   setItems(items.filter((_, i) => i !== index));\\n// };\\n\\nreturn (\\n  <ul>\\n    {items.map((item, index) => (\\n      <li key={item.id}>\\n        {item.name}\\n        <button onClick={() => removeItem(item.id)}>Delete</button>\\n      </li>\\n    ))}\\n  </ul>\\n);\"],[2342,\"The useEffect hook expects its callback either to return undefined or to return a clean-up function. An asynchronous function always returns a Promise, which breaks that expectation and can lead to unpredictable behaviour. React cannot handle a Promise properly as the return value for cleaning an effect up. Asynchronous logic has to be placed inside useEffect's synchronous callback instead — by declaring and calling an asynchronous function inside it, for example.\",\"The `useEffect` hook is a fundamental tool in React for performing side effects such as loading data, subscribing to events or changing the DOM by hand. Its internal logic expects the function passed as its first argument either to return nothing (that is, `undefined`) or to return another function, which will be run in order to clean the effect up before the next render or before the component unmounts.\\n\\nThe problem with asynchronous functions\\nAn asynchronous function declared with the `async` keyword always returns a `Promise` object. If React allowed an `async` function to be passed to `useEffect` directly, it would receive a Promise as the return value. That would break the hook's contract, because:\\n- React cannot use a Promise as a clean-up function. The clean-up has to be a synchronous operation that releases the resources immediately.\\n- React does not \\\"wait\\\" for a Promise inside an effect to settle. Effects run synchronously after the render, and React moves on to the next stage of the lifecycle without waiting for the asynchronous operations.\\n- That can lead to memory leaks or to a race condition, if the component unmounts before the asynchronous operation has finished.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nAsynchronous logic should be encapsulated inside `useEffect`'s synchronous callback instead. The most widespread way is to declare an asynchronous function and call it immediately (an IIFE), or to create a named function and call it.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nThis pattern is critically important when working with any asynchronous operations in React components: loading data from an API, working with timers (`setTimeout`/`setInterval`), subscribing to external events or to a WebSocket. Understanding this limitation helps you avoid hidden bugs and manage a component's lifecycle properly.\\n\\nConclusion: `useEffect` cannot be made an asynchronous function directly, because that breaks the return contract React expects (either undefined or a clean-up function). Always place your asynchronous logic inside the effect's synchronous callback, which makes the clean-up work properly and prevents memory leaks.\",\"useEffect(() => {\\n    // We declare the asynchronous function inside the effect\\n    const fetchData = async () => {\\n        try {\\n            const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n            const data = await response.json();\\n            setData(data);\\n        } catch (error) {\\n            setError(error.message);\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    // We call it\\n    fetchData();\\n\\n    // Where necessary we return a synchronous clean-up function\\n    return () => {\\n        // Cancelling the request or cleaning the subscriptions up\\n    };\\n}, []); // The dependencies\"],[2343,\"Mutating state directly breaks the principle of immutability, which is the basis of predictability in modern UI frameworks. When an object is mutated directly, the reference to it stays the same and the system cannot detect that the state has changed. The result is that components do not repaint, caches are not invalidated and the history of changes is lost. You have to create new objects or arrays instead — by using the spread operator or methods such as map and filter, for example.\",\"Mutating state directly is changing the values inside an existing object or array without creating a new reference. In the context of React, Redux and many other state-management systems that counts as an anti-pattern, because it breaks the principle of immutability.\\n\\nWhy is this a problem?\\n\\n- It breaks the reactivity: React and similar libraries rely on comparing references (a shallow comparison) in order to determine whether the state has changed. If you mutate an object in place, its reference stays the same and React \\\"does not see\\\" the changes, which leads to missed re-renders.\\n- The difficulty of debugging: when state is mutated in different parts of an application, it becomes extremely hard to trace where and when the change happened, especially in asynchronous code. That makes bugs harder to reproduce.\\n- It breaks PureComponent and React.memo: these optimizations depend on a shallow comparison of the props. If you pass a mutated object, the comparison will not work and the component may fail to update when it should, or update when it should not.\\n- Problems with history and undo: in libraries such as Redux, the devtools and the middleware (redux-thunk, for example) rely on every state being a new immutable snapshot. A mutation breaks the ability to \\\"time travel\\\" and to log.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at the typical mistake when updating an array in a React component:\\n\\nWhere is this relevant?\\nThe principle of the immutability of state is a cornerstone not only in React and Redux but in libraries for global state (MobX, although it uses observable objects, also recommends careful mutations), in functional programming, and even in systems such as Immer, which provide an API for writing mutable-looking code but generate new immutable structures under the hood.\\n\\nConclusion: avoid mutating state directly, in order to ensure predictability, to make debugging easier and to guarantee that reactive systems and their optimizations work properly. Always create new objects or arrays when updating.\",\"// ❌ BAD: a direct mutation\\nconst [todos, setTodos] = useState([{ id: 1, text: 'Learn React' }]);\\nconst addTodoBad = (newTodo) => {\\n  todos.push(newTodo); // A mutation of the existing array!\\n  setTodos(todos); // The reference has not changed; React may ignore the update\\n};\\n\\n// ✅ GOOD: creating a new array\\nconst addTodoGood = (newTodo) => {\\n  setTodos([...todos, newTodo]); // A new array, a new reference\\n};\\n\\n// An example with an object\\nconst [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 });\\n// ❌ user.age = 31;\\n// ✅ setUser({ ...user, age: 31 });\"],[2344,\"The clean-up of effects in React is organized by means of the clean-up function returned from the useEffect hook. That function runs before the effect runs again (when the dependencies change) and before the component unmounts. It lets you cancel subscriptions, timers or network requests, which prevents memory leaks and errors from updating the state of a component that no longer exists. Always return a clean-up function if the effect creates something that has to be removed.\",\"The `useEffect` hook in React lets you perform side effects in functional components. Those effects often create resources such as event subscriptions, timers or connections to a server, which have to be released when the component is removed from the DOM or before the effect runs again, in order to avoid memory leaks and unexpected behaviour.\\n\\nHow the clean-up function works\\nThe clean-up function is a function you return from the callback passed to `useEffect`. React will call it in two cases:\\n- Before the effect runs again (if the values in the array of dependencies have changed).\\n- Immediately before the component unmounts.\\n\\nThat guarantees that the resources created in the previous render will be removed properly before the new ones are created.\\n\\nPractical examples\\nLet us look at an example with an event subscription and a timer:\\n\\nIn this example the clean-up function removes the event handler and stops the interval. Without it, every update of the component would create new subscriptions and timers, which would lead to a memory leak and to incorrect behaviour (the counter would speed up, for example).\\n\\nWhen and where this is relevant\\nClean-up functions are necessary for any effects that:\\n- Create subscriptions (WebSockets, DOM events, observers).\\n- Start asynchronous operations that may try to update the state after the component has unmounted.\\n- Use timers (`setInterval`, `setTimeout`).\\n- Make API requests that can be cancelled (by means of an `AbortController`, for example).\\n\\nFor asynchronous operations such as fetch requests, an `AbortController` is often used inside the clean-up function, in order to cancel the request if the component unmounts before it has finished.\\n\\nConclusion: always return a clean-up function from `useEffect` if your effect creates resources that have to be released. It is a key practice for writing reliable React applications that are not prone to memory leaks, especially when working with external APIs, with events or with timers.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ExampleComponent() {\\n  const [position, setPosition] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // Effect 1: a subscription to the mousemove event\\n    const handleMouseMove = (e) => {\\n      setPosition({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });\\n    };\\n    window.addEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);\\n\\n    // Effect 2: starting an interval\\n    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function for both effects\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);\\n      clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect runs on mounting only\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The mouse's position: {position.x}, {position.y}</p>\\n      <p>The counter: {count}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2345,\"A ref is used for direct access to DOM elements or for holding mutable values that do not require the component to repaint. State is intended for data that changes and has to cause the component to re-render in order to update the UI. Changing a ref causes no re-render, whereas changing state does. Refs are often used for integration with DOM APIs, for timers or for holding previous values.\",\"React has two main hooks for holding data inside a functional component: `useState` and `useRef`. They serve different purposes, and understanding the differences between them is critical to writing efficient, predictable components.\\n\\nThe purpose of state\\nState is intended for holding data that can change over the course of the component's lifecycle and, when it changes, has to cause the component to render again in order to update the user interface. The value of an input field, a checkbox or a list of items visible to the user, for example.\\n\\nThe purpose of a ref\\nA ref is used for accessing DOM elements directly or for holding mutable values that should not cause a re-render. A ref keeps its value between renders, but changing it does not lead to the component updating. That is useful for integrating with native DOM APIs (focusing a field, for example), for holding the ids of timers or for keeping the previous value of a piece of state.\\n\\nPractical examples\\nAn example of using state for a counter:\\n\\nHere every click updates the state and the component repaints, displaying the new value.\\n\\nAn example of using a ref to focus an input field:\\n\\nHere the ref is used to gain direct access to the input DOM element without the component having to repaint.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n - Re-rendering: changing state causes a re-render; changing a ref does not.\\n - Updating the value: state is updated asynchronously and through a setter function; a ref is updated synchronously by assigning to `ref.current`.\\n - Uses: state is for data that affects the UI. A ref is for DOM operations, timers, holding previous values or any mutation without a re-render.\\n\\nConclusion: use state for data that has to be kept in sync with the interface and to cause it to update. Use a ref when you need direct access to the DOM or need to hold a value that should not affect the component's painting.\",\"import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n        Increment\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction InputFocus() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    inputRef.current.focus();\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input ref={inputRef} type=\\\"text\\\" />\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>\\n        Focus the input\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2346,\"A UI kit is a set of reusable interface components such as buttons, input fields and modal windows, created according to a single set of design rules. It lets developers assemble interfaces quickly without reinventing every element, and it ensures visual consistency across the whole application. A UI kit normally includes the basic components, a theming system, documentation and usage examples. Introducing one speeds development up and makes maintenance easier.\",\"A UI kit (or component library) is a systematized collection of ready-made interface elements created for reuse in a project. Its main aim is to ensure a single visual language and to speed the development process up, saving the team from having to create buttons, forms or cards from scratch every time.\\n\\nThe key elements of a UI kit\\n\\n- The basic components: primitives such as Button, Input, Select and Modal, which are configured through props.\\n- The composite components: more complex blocks assembled from the basic ones (a search form or a profile header, for example).\\n- A system of design tokens: centralized variables for the colours, the typography, the spacing and the shadows, often implemented through CSS variables or a theme in styled-components.\\n- Documentation and a sandbox: an interactive environment (Storybook, for example) for demonstrating the components and their properties.\\n\\nAn example of the structure and the code\\nLet us look at a simple example of a button in a React project using styled-components for the styling.\\n\\nWhere and how it is used\\nA UI kit is especially useful in large projects with several teams of frontend developers and designers. It serves as the \\\"single source of truth\\\" for the interface. It is often integrated with tools such as Storybook for developing and testing the components in isolation. In modern stacks (React, Vue, Angular) a UI kit can be published as a private npm package for use in several of a company's applications.\\n\\nConclusion: a UI kit is worth applying in medium- and large-scale projects, where development speed, design consistency and the long-term maintainability of the code matter. It reduces duplication and makes it easier to introduce global changes to the design.\",\"// Button.jsx\\nimport styled from 'styled-components';\\n\\n// We use the design tokens from the theme\\nconst StyledButton = styled.button`\\n  padding: 12px 24px;\\n  border-radius: 8px;\\n  border: none;\\n  font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;\\n  font-weight: 600;\\n  cursor: pointer;\\n  background-color: ${props => props.variant === 'primary' ? '#007bff' : '#6c757d'};\\n  color: white;\\n  &:hover {\\n    opacity: 0.9;\\n  }\\n  &:disabled {\\n    opacity: 0.5;\\n    cursor: not-allowed;\\n  }\\n`;\\n\\nexport const Button = ({ children, variant = 'primary', ...props }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <StyledButton variant={variant} {...props}>\\n      {children}\\n    </StyledButton>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Using it in another component\\n// import { Button } from './ui-kit/Button';\\n// <Button variant=\\\"primary\\\">Save</Button>\"],[2347,\"On the first render React calls the class's constructor (if the component is a class one) or runs the functional component's body. React then computes the JSX, creates a virtual DOM tree and compares it against the real DOM, making the necessary changes (the mounting stage). Once the component has been painted into the DOM, a `useEffect` with an empty array of dependencies runs, or the `componentDidMount` method for class components, which lets you perform side effects such as loading data.\",\"A React component's first render, also known as mounting, is the process in which the component is added to the DOM tree for the first time. Understanding this process is critical to initializing state, making API requests and setting subscriptions up.\\n\\nThe stages of the first render\\n\\n- Initialization: for a functional component React runs the function's body, calling every hook (`useState`, `useRef`, for example) in the order they are declared. For a class component the constructor is called first, where the state is normally initialized.\\n- The render: React calls the `render` method (for classes) or the component function itself, in order to obtain the JSX. That JSX is turned into React elements (the virtual DOM).\\n- Reconciliation: React compares the virtual DOM tree it has obtained against the current real DOM (which is initially empty for this component) and computes the minimal set of changes.\\n- The commit: React applies the computed changes to the real DOM, actually creating the HTML elements on the page.\\n- Running the mounting effects: once the component has been displayed in the DOM, React schedules the side effects to run. For functional components that is the call of the function inside a `useEffect` with an empty array of dependencies `[]`. For class components the `componentDidMount` method is called.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a functional component that loads data on mounting:\\n\\nIn this example the order on the first render is: the state is declared, the component's body runs (returning `Loading...`), it mounts into the DOM, and then the asynchronous `fetch` call inside the `useEffect` happens.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nA knowledge of the mounting stages is used for:\\n- Optimizing performance (avoiding unnecessary renders).\\n- Initializing third-party libraries properly (maps or charts, for example), which need access to the DOM.\\n- Managing subscriptions to events or to external data, in order to avoid memory leaks.\\n\\nConclusion: understanding the first render lets you place your initialization code properly (in a `useEffect` or in `componentDidMount`), avoiding the common mistakes of performing side effects during the render or trying to access the DOM before it is ready.\",\"import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  // 1. The state is initialized on the first render\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  // 3. The effect, which runs after mounting\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUser(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n    // The empty array of dependencies guarantees it runs once\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  // 2. The JSX is returned for the render\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h2>{user.name}</h2>\\n      <p>{user.email}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2348,\"The main alternatives to Redux include the built-in Context API, MobX, Zustand, Recoil and Jotai. The Context API suits simple global state without middleware. MobX uses reactive programming and is simpler to set up. Zustand is a minimalist library with hooks, popular for its simplicity. Recoil and Jotai offer atomic state management and integrate well with React. The choice depends on the project's complexity and on the team's preferences.\",\"Redux was the standard for managing state in React applications for a long time, but its complexity and boilerplate led to the appearance of lighter, more convenient alternatives. These alternatives solve the same problems — changing state predictably and distributing it across the components — but with different approaches and trade-offs.\\n\\nThe built-in solution: the Context API\\nThe Context API is React's native means of passing data through the component tree without props. It suits simple global state excellently — the theme or the user's data, for example. It is not optimized for frequent updates, however, and can cause unnecessary re-renders unless it is combined with useMemo or useReducer.\\n\\nLibraries with a reactive approach: MobX\\nMobX uses the principles of reactive programming: you declare observable state and the library tracks and updates the dependent components automatically. That requires less boilerplate code than Redux and is often considered more intuitive.\\n\\nMinimalist solutions: Zustand\\nZustand is a small library built on React's hooks. It lets you create stores with minimal configuration, with no need for providers. The state is updated directly, and the subscription to changes happens automatically.\\n\\nAtomic state managers: Recoil and Jotai\\nRecoil (from Facebook) and Jotai (a lighter equivalent) represent state as atoms — independent pieces that can be combined. That lets you manage derived state efficiently and avoid unnecessary re-renders.\\n\\nIn summary: the choice of an alternative to Redux depends on the scale of the project. For small applications the Context API or Zustand is enough. For complex ones requiring fine optimization, MobX or the atomic libraries (Recoil/Jotai) are suitable. Redux remains a good choice for large projects with clear discipline and a need for powerful middleware (for logging or asynchronous actions, for example).\",\"// Creating the context\\nconst ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\n\\n// The provider at the application's root\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Using it in a child component\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  const { theme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return <div>The current theme: {theme}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { makeAutoObservable } from 'mobx';\\nimport { observer } from 'mobx-react-lite';\\n\\n// Creating the store\\nclass CounterStore {\\n  count = 0;\\n  constructor() {\\n    makeAutoObservable(this);\\n  }\\n  increment() {\\n    this.count += 1;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst store = new CounterStore();\\n\\n// A component that responds to the changes\\nconst Counter = observer(() => {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <span>{store.count}</span>\\n      <button onClick={() => store.increment()}>+</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n});\\n\\nimport create from 'zustand';\\n\\n// Creating the store\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  bears: 0,\\n  increasePopulation: () => set((state) => ({ bears: state.bears + 1 })),\\n  removeAllBears: () => set({ bears: 0 }),\\n}));\\n\\n// Using it in a component\\nfunction BearCounter() {\\n  const bears = useStore((state) => state.bears);\\n  const increase = useStore((state) => state.increasePopulation);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <span>{bears} bears</span>\\n      <button onClick={increase}>Add a bear</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2349,\"The Provider pattern is a way of passing data (state or functions, for example) deep into a component tree, bypassing the intermediate levels. It solves the problem of \\\"prop drilling\\\", where props have to be passed through a great many components that do not use them themselves. In React the Context API is often used for this, where a Provider wraps part of the application and provides a value to every child component. That makes the code cleaner and makes managing global state easier.\",\"The Provider pattern is an architectural approach that lets you \\\"provide\\\" particular data or services to the components in a hierarchy without passing them explicitly through props at every level. Its main aim is to avoid cumbersome \\\"prop drilling\\\", where data passes through a great many intermediate components, which complicates maintenance and the reuse of code.\\n\\nHow a Provider works in React\\nIn the React ecosystem this pattern is most often implemented through the built-in Context API. You create a context by means of `React.createContext()`, which returns an object with two components: `Provider` and `Consumer` (or the `useContext` hook is used). The `Provider` component takes a `value` prop and wraps part of the component tree. Any child component inside that wrapper can gain access to the `value` by subscribing to the context.\\n\\nAn example of the code with React Context\\n\\nWhere else is the Provider pattern used?\\nBesides React Context, the Provider idea underlies many state-management libraries (Redux, MobX, Zustand, for example). In Redux the `<Provider>` component makes the Redux store available to every connected component. The pattern is also widespread in other frameworks and languages as a form of Dependency Injection, where a \\\"provider\\\" registers a service and then \\\"injects\\\" it into the client classes.\\n\\nConclusion: the Provider pattern is worth applying when you need to share data (the theme, the authentication, the locale, global state) between many components at different levels of nesting. It reduces the coupling of the code, saves you from passing props by hand and centralizes the data-management logic.\",\"// 1. We create the context\\nexport const ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\n\\n// 2. The application component, which provides the value\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');\\n\\n  return (\\n    // The Provider wraps the child components\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 3. The intermediate component (it receives no props explicitly)\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  return <ThemedButton />;\\n}\\n\\n// 4. The child component, which consumes the value\\nfunction ThemedButton() {\\n  // The useContext hook subscribes to the nearest Provider higher up the tree\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button\\n      className={theme}\\n      onClick={() => setTheme(prev => prev === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark')}\\n    >\\n      Toggle Theme\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2350,\"A custom alert above every screen is implemented by creating an element outside the main DOM tree, so as to avoid the influence of the parent's styles. In React a React Portal is used for this, which mounts the component in a separate div — in the body, for example. You have to manage the z-index so that the alert sits above the other elements, and handle the events (clicks outside the alert, the Escape key) in order to close it. It is also important to ensure accessibility (ARIA attributes) and to block the scrolling of the page behind it.\",\"Creating a custom alert that has to be displayed above every element of the interface is a widespread task in web development. The main difficulty is making sure the component is not constrained by the styles (`overflow: hidden`, for example) of its parent container in the DOM tree and has priority on the Z axis (sits above the other elements).\\n\\nThe key principles of the implementation\\n\\n- Leaving the document flow: the alert has to render outside the main component tree, normally directly in the `<body>`. That solves the problem with the ancestors' CSS properties.\\n- Managing the z-index: the alert's element and its overlay (the semi-transparent background) need a high `z-index` value — 9999 or 10000, for example.\\n- Accessibility (a11y): it is important to add the ARIA attributes (`role=\\\"alertdialog\\\"`, `aria-labelledby`, `aria-modal=\\\"true\\\"`) and to manage the focus (the focus should move inside the alert and be \\\"trapped\\\" there).\\n- Managing the state: you have to handle closing on a click on the overlay, on the Escape key and on the \\\"OK\\\" button inside the alert.\\n\\nAn example implementation with a React Portal\\nReact provides the `ReactDOM.createPortal` API for rendering children into a DOM node that exists outside the parent component's hierarchy.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is used not only for alerts but for modal windows, toasts (notifications), sidebars and action-confirmation dialogues. In large applications this logic is often moved into a global context or a state manager (Redux, for example) in order to manage the display of notifications centrally from anywhere in the code.\\n\\nConclusion: custom alerts above the screen are worth implementing through portals (or their equivalents in other frameworks), which guarantees they are displayed above the UI. That gives isolation from the parents' styles, makes managing the z-index easier and makes the component a reusable global element of the interface.\",\"import React, { useEffect } from 'react';\\nimport ReactDOM from 'react-dom';\\n\\nconst CustomAlert = ({ message, isOpen, onClose }) => {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handleEscape = (e) => {\\n      if (e.key === 'Escape') onClose();\\n    };\\n    if (isOpen) {\\n      document.addEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);\\n      document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden'; // We block the scrolling\\n    }\\n    return () => {\\n      document.removeEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);\\n      document.body.style.overflow = 'unlocked';\\n    };\\n  }, [isOpen, onClose]);\\n\\n  if (!isOpen) return null;\\n\\n  return ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n    <>\\n      <div className=\\\"alert-overlay\\\" onClick={onClose} />\\n      <div className=\\\"alert-container\\\" role=\\\"alertdialog\\\" aria-modal=\\\"true\\\">\\n        <p>{message}</p>\\n        <button onClick={onClose}>OK</button>\\n      </div>\\n    </>,\\n    document.body\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// The styles (CSS)\\n// .alert-overlay {\\n//   position: fixed;\\n//   top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;\\n//   background: rgba(0,0,0,0.5);\\n//   z-index: 9999;\\n// }\\n// .alert-container {\\n//   position: fixed;\\n//   top: 50%; left: 50%;\\n//   transform: translate(-50%, -50%);\\n//   background: white;\\n//   padding: 2rem;\\n//   border-radius: 8px;\\n//   z-index: 10000;\\n// }\"],[2351,\"An object's lifecycle determines when it is created, used and destroyed by the garbage collector. If the object that initiated a network request is destroyed before the request has finished, that can lead to memory leaks or to errors, since the callback may try to address an object that no longer exists. In languages with manual memory management you have to cancel the requests explicitly. In JavaScript, with its automatic garbage collector, it is important to avoid keeping references to objects you no longer need inside callbacks, so that they can be removed properly.\",\"An object's lifecycle is the period from its creation in memory to its removal by the garbage collector (or its explicit release). Network requests are asynchronous operations that run independently of the main thread. The link between them is that the object initiating a request often passes a callback or a promise in order to handle the response. If that object is destroyed before the request has finished, the response handler may try to update the state or the properties of an object that no longer exists, which will lead to errors (addressing null, for example) or to memory leaks, if the handler keeps a reference to the object and prevents it being collected.\\n\\nThe problems and some examples\\nLet us look at a typical scenario in a web application. A UI component (a React component, for example) sends a request when it mounts, but the user quickly navigates to another page and the component unmounts. If the request finishes after the unmount and tries to update the component's state, an error will arise.\\n\\nHow to manage the lifecycle\\nTo avoid problems, the cancellation of requests has to be managed explicitly when the object is destroyed:\\n - In modern browsers use an `AbortController` in order to cancel fetch requests.\\n - In libraries (Axios, for example) use cancellation tokens.\\n - In React applications clean your subscriptions up in `componentWillUnmount` or use hooks with effects.\\n\\nWhere this is relevant\\nThis approach is critical in single-page applications (SPAs), in mobile applications and in any system with a dynamic interface, where components are created and removed often. It also matters in server applications in Node.js when working with long HTTP requests or WebSocket connections, in order to free the resources up.\\n\\nConclusion: explicit management of an object's lifecycle relative to its network requests is necessary in order to prevent memory leaks and runtime errors. Always cancel or clean up pending requests when the object is destroyed, especially in UI frameworks and asynchronous environments.\",\"// An example in JavaScript with React and fetch\\nclass UserProfile extends React.Component {\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    fetch('/api/user')\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        // The danger: if the component has already unmounted,\\n        // this.setState will cause an error.\\n        this.setState({ user: data });\\n      });\\n  }\\n\\n  render() { /* ... */ }\\n}\\n\\n// An example with an AbortController and a React hook\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const controller = new AbortController();\\n    const signal = controller.signal;\\n\\n    fetch('/api/user', { signal })\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => setUser(data))\\n      .catch(err => {\\n        if (err.name === 'AbortError') {\\n          console.log('The request was cancelled');\\n        }\\n      });\\n\\n    // The clean-up function: it cancels the request on unmounting\\n    return () => controller.abort();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return /* JSX */;\\n}\"],[2352,\"The order in which hooks are called matters because React relies on it in order to manage state and side effects properly. Hooks have to be called at the top level of a component rather than inside conditionals, loops or nested functions. That guarantees that on every render the hooks are called in the same sequence, which lets React match the state and the effects up correctly between renders. Breaking that rule leads to bugs — a mismatch of state or missed updates, for example.\",\"Hooks in React are functions that let you use state and React's other capabilities in functional components. For them to work properly, React tracks the order in which the hooks are called on every render of the component internally.\\n\\nWhy the order matters\\nReact holds the hooks' state as a list associated with the component. On the first render React remembers the order in which the hooks were called (`useState` first, then `useEffect`, for example). On subsequent renders React expects that order to stay unchanged, so that it can match the previous state up with the new call of the hook correctly. If the order changes (because of a conditional statement, for example), React will \\\"lose\\\" the connection, which will lead to bugs.\\n\\nAn example of breaking the order\\nLet us look at code where the call of a hook depends on a condition:\\n\\nIf `isVisible` changes from `true` to `false`, then on the next render there will be no `useState` hook. React expected a second call (for the `useEffect`) but will get the first, which will cause confusion in the state and, most likely, an error.\\n\\nThe correct usage\\nHooks always have to be called at the top level of the component, which guarantees the same sequence on every render:\\n\\nThis rule applies to every hook, custom ones included. It makes a component's behaviour predictable and stable.\\n\\nConclusion: observing the order in which hooks are called is critical to managing state and the lifecycle properly in React components; always call your hooks at the top level, avoiding conditionals and loops.\",\"function MyComponent({ isVisible }) {\\n  if (isVisible) {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // The hook is called only when isVisible = true\\n  }\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Effect');\\n  });\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent({ isVisible }) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // It is always called first\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    if (isVisible) {\\n      console.log(count); // The logic inside the effect can be conditional\\n    }\\n  }, [isVisible, count]);\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\"],[2353,\"useCallback is not needed when the function is not passed down the component tree as a prop. If the function is used only inside the component and does not depend on changing dependencies, memoizing it is unnecessary. There is also no point in using useCallback for simple components that rarely repaint, since the overhead of the memoization can exceed the benefit. Inline event handlers that cause no unnecessary re-renders of child components also need no useCallback.\",\"The `useCallback` hook in React is intended for memoizing functions, in order to prevent them being recreated on every render of a component. That is useful when the function is passed down as a prop into child components, especially ones wrapped in `React.memo`, in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders. Its use is not always justified, however, and it can even make performance worse.\\n\\nWhen useCallback is not needed\\n\\n- The function is used only locally: if the function is not passed down into child components, memoizing it will not prevent unnecessary re-renders of other parts of the application.\\n- The component is simple and rarely re-renders: for small, isolated components the overhead of calling `useCallback` (comparing the dependencies, holding things in memory) can be greater than the benefit of preventing the function being recreated.\\n- The function has no dependencies, or they are stable: if the array of dependencies is empty or contains primitives that never change, the function will be stable anyway, but memoizing it still adds a small cost.\\n- The child components are not optimized: if the child components are not wrapped in `React.memo` or `useMemo` and are not pure class components, they will repaint regardless of how stable the props are.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a component where `useCallback` is redundant:\\n\\nHere `handleClick` is not passed as a prop, so its being recreated on every render has no negative consequences. Adding a `useCallback` will only complicate the code to no purpose.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `useCallback` purposefully: only when the function is passed into optimized child components and its instability causes redundant re-renders. In every other case, especially in simple components, do without it in favour of readable, simple code.\",\"function SimpleForm() {\\n  // A local handler; it is not passed down\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    console.log('Button clicked');\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={handleClick}>\\n      Click me\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2354,\"In React timers are created by means of JavaScript's native setTimeout and setInterval functions. They should be started inside the useEffect hook, in order to tie them to the component's lifecycle. The key point is cleaning the timers up in the cleanup function returned from useEffect. That prevents the callbacks running after the component has unmounted, which can cause errors and memory leaks. To manage a timer's state you can use the useRef hook.\",\"Working with timers in React calls for particular care, because of the component model and the lifecycle. If you simply call setTimeout or setInterval in a functional component's body, the timer will be created on every render, which will lead to an uncontrolled accumulation of timers and to memory leaks. The right approach is to use the useEffect hook in order to manage the side effects, which timers are.\\n\\nA basic example with setTimeout\\nHere is how a one-off timer can be implemented:\\n\\nAn example with setInterval\\nFor periodic actions the logic is the same, but the clean-up is even more important:\\n\\nUsing useRef to hold the timer's identifier lets you access it from the component's other functions (a click handler for stopping it, for example).\\n\\nThe important aspects\\n\\n- The clean-up: always return a clean-up function from useEffect that calls clearTimeout or clearInterval. That guarantees the timer will not fire on an unmounted component.\\n- The dependencies: if the timer has to restart when some prop or piece of state changes, state them in useEffect's array of dependencies. In that case the clean-up function will run before every new run of the effect.\\n- useRef vs useState: to hold the timer's ID it is better to use useRef, since changing it does not cause the component to re-render, unlike useState.\\n\\nTimers are used for deferred actions (search with a debounce delay, for example), for animations, for polling the server periodically or for creating countdowns.\\n\\nConclusion: use the useEffect hook to create and — critically — to clean your timers up. This pattern prevents the common mistakes and makes working with asynchronous operations in React safe and predictable.\",\"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimerComponent() {\\n  const [message, setMessage] = useState('');\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // We set the timer\\n    const timerId = setTimeout(() => {\\n      setMessage('The timer fired!');\\n    }, 2000);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function\\n    return () => {\\n      clearTimeout(timerId);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies = the effect runs once, on mounting\\n\\n  return <div>{message}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useEffect, useState, useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n    };\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const stopTimer = () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={stopTimer}>Stop</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2355,\"The criteria for choosing a state manager include the application's complexity, the performance requirements, the team's preferences and the ecosystem. For small projects lightweight solutions such as Zustand or the Context API are suitable. In large applications with frequent state updates, predictability and developer tools matter, as in Redux. It is also worth assessing the learning curve and the integration with the framework.\",\"The choice of a state-management library is an architectural decision that affects an application's maintainability, performance and development speed. There is no single \\\"best\\\" solution; the choice depends on the particular requirements of the project and the team.\\n\\nThe key criteria for the choice\\n\\n- The application's complexity: for simple SPAs with little state, React's Context API or lightweight libraries (Zustand, Valtio) are often enough. For large enterprise applications with a great deal of interrelated data and complex business logic, Redux, MobX or XState are suitable.\\n- Performance and optimization: it matters how the library handles updates. Some (such as Redux) require immutable updates and can cause unnecessary re-renders without optimizations. Others (MobX, Valtio) use proxies and track changes automatically, minimizing the re-renders.\\n- Predictability and debugging: libraries such as Redux offer a strict unidirectional data flow and powerful developer tools (the Redux DevTools), which makes debugging and reproducing states easier. That is critical in large teams.\\n- The learning curve and the team's preferences: Redux requires an understanding of reducers, actions and middleware. MobX uses reactive programming. Zustand offers a simpler API. The choice should match the team's experience.\\n- Integration with the ecosystem: check the compatibility with your framework (React, Vue, Angular) and the availability of middleware (Redux Thunk/Saga for asynchronous operations, for example). The bundle size and the TypeScript support also matter.\\n\\nAn example of a comparison in practice\\nLet us look at a simple counter implemented with Zustand and with Redux Toolkit (the simplified version of Redux).\\n\\nZustand offers more concise code out of the box, whereas Redux Toolkit requires more boilerplate but gives a better structure for scaling.\\n\\nConclusion: choose lightweight libraries (Zustand, Jotai) for start-ups and small projects, where development speed matters. For large, long-term projects with a big team, solutions with a strict architecture and good debugging tools, such as Redux or MobX, are preferable. Always weigh the trade-off between simplicity and control.\",\"// An example with Zustand (simplicity)\\nimport create from 'zustand';\\n\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n  decrement: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count - 1 })),\\n}));\\n\\n// In a component:\\n// const { count, increment } = useStore();\\n\\n// An example with Redux Toolkit (structure)\\nimport { createSlice, configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'counter',\\n  initialState: { value: 0 },\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment: (state) => { state.value += 1; },\\n    decrement: (state) => { state.value -= 1; },\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nconst store = configureStore({ reducer: counterSlice.reducer });\\n// Then it is connected through a Provider and used with the useSelector/useDispatch hooks\"],[2356,\"Business logic and UI components are separated in order to isolate the rules of the domain from the interface-painting code. That makes the logic independent of the framework and easy to test. The UI components become \\\"dumb\\\" and are responsible only for displaying things and for passing the user's events on. This approach makes refactoring easier and lets you reuse the logic on different platforms.\",\"The separation of business logic and UI components is a key design principle that improves the quality of code. The business logic contains the rules, computations and data operations specific to the application's domain. The UI components are responsible for painting the interface and handling the user's input. Mixing these layers leads to \\\"fat\\\" components that are hard to test and to maintain.\\n\\nThe main approaches to separating them\\n\\n- The Container/Presenter pattern: the container components (\\\"smart\\\" ones) manage the data and the logic, while the presentational ones (\\\"dumb\\\") only display them.\\n- Custom hooks (React): the logic is moved into reusable hooks, which the components use in order to obtain state and functions.\\n- Services/a store (Redux, for example): the state and the business logic are held and managed centrally outside the components.\\n- MVVM/MVP: architectural patterns in which a ViewModel/Presenter acts as the intermediary between the View and the Model.\\n\\nA practical example in React with hooks\\nInstead of putting the counting logic and the state directly in the component, we move it into a custom hook.\\n\\nIn this example the `useCounter` hook encapsulates all of the counter's logic. It can be tested separately from React. The `Counter` component becomes simple and predictable: it only displays the data and calls the functions it is given.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is critically important in large applications, where the logic is complex and changes often. It is used in every modern framework (React, Vue, Angular) and is the basis of patterns such as Flux/Redux. The separation lets teams work in parallel: some develop the services and the hooks, others the interfaces.\\n\\nConclusion: separate your business logic and your UI, in order to build flexible, testable, maintainable applications. It is especially useful when the requirements change often or when the same logic has to be used in different parts of the interface (on the web and in a mobile application, for example).\",\"// useCounter.js — the business logic\\nimport { useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nexport function useCounter(initialValue = 0) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialValue);\\n\\n  const increment = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const decrement = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev - 1);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const reset = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(initialValue);\\n  }, [initialValue]);\\n\\n  // We return the state and the API for changing it\\n  return { count, increment, decrement, reset };\\n}\\n\\n// Counter.js — the \\\"dumb\\\" UI component\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport { useCounter } from './useCounter';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const { count, increment, decrement, reset } = useCounter();\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>+</button>\\n      <button onClick={decrement}>-</button>\\n      <button onClick={reset}>Reset</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nexport default Counter;\"],[2357,\"Integrating UI libraries with SSR requires you to solve the problems of hydration, of the absence of the DOM API on the server and of synchronizing the state. On the server the components are rendered into static HTML, but on the client the library has to \\\"bring that HTML to life\\\" by attaching the event handlers. The key difficulties: working with browser APIs (window, document), which are unavailable on the server, and ensuring the component tree is identical between the server and the client so that the hydration succeeds.\",\"Server-Side Rendering (SSR) is the technique in which the HTML for a page is generated on the server and sent to the client ready-made. That improves SEO and the time to the First Paint. When using modern UI libraries such as React, Vue or Svelte, however, the integration with SSR has a number of particulars that have to be taken into account for the application to work properly.\\n\\nThe key particulars and problems\\n\\n- Hydration: once the server has sent the static HTML, the UI library on the client has to \\\"bring it to life\\\" — to bind the event handlers and restore the interactivity. That process is called hydration. The library compares the HTML generated by the server against what it would itself have rendered on the client. If the trees do not match, a hydration error arises.\\n- The absence of browser APIs: on the server there are no `window`, `document` or `localStorage` objects. Code that addresses them has to run only on the client; otherwise the application will crash with an error.\\n- Synchronizing the state: the application's state (data from an API, for example) used during the server render has to be passed to the client, in order to avoid repeated requests and to ensure an identical initial render.\\n- Styles (CSS-in-JS): many styling libraries generate their classes dynamically. On the server the generated CSS rules have to be extracted and inserted into the response; otherwise the styles will not be applied.\\n\\nPractical examples and solutions\\nLet us look at the typical problem of using the `window` object in a React component.\\n\\nThe solution is to use a check for running in the browser, or hooks that fire only on the client (`useEffect`, for example).\\n\\nTo synchronize the state in React applications, Next.js is often used, which serializes the state obtained in the `getServerSideProps` functions automatically and passes it to the client.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nSSR with UI libraries is critically important for public web applications where search optimization (SEO) and the speed of the first load matter. That is typical of marketplaces, news portals, blogs and any site with dynamic content that has to be indexed by search engines.\\n\\nConclusion: integrating UI libraries with SSR calls for attention to the hydration, to running browser code conditionally and to synchronizing the state. Use frameworks (Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit) that provide built-in solutions to these problems, or organize your code carefully, separating the logic for the server and for the client.\",\"// The problem component, which will break under SSR\\nfunction UserLocation() {\\n  // window is available only in the browser\\n  const [city, setCity] = useState(window.location.pathname);\\n  return <div>Your city: {city}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// The fixed component\\nfunction UserLocation() {\\n  const [city, setCity] = useState('');\\n  // useEffect runs only on the client\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setCity(window.location.pathname);\\n  }, []);\\n  // We show a placeholder during the SSR\\n  return <div>Your city: {city || 'Loading...'}</div>;\\n}\"],[2358,\"The hydration of state is the process of passing data from the server to the client, in order to avoid repeated requests. The server renders the page with the initial data and embeds that data in the HTML — in a `<script>` tag, for example. On the client the application reads that state and uses it as its initial state instead of making a new request. That speeds the first paint up and avoids the interface flickering.\",\"The hydration of state is a key technique in the architecture of modern web applications, especially ones that use server-side rendering (SSR) or static generation (SSG). Its aim is to avoid the situation in which the client application, having received static HTML from the server, is forced to request the same data again through an API, which leads to delays and to the interface flickering.\\n\\nHow it works\\nThe process can be divided into several stages:\\n- The server-side preparation: the application's code runs on the server (in React with Next.js, or Nuxt.js for Vue, for example). The application requests the data it needs (from a database or an API) and renders the components into HTML using that state.\\n- Embedding the state: the resulting state (normally a JavaScript object) is serialized into a JSON string and embedded directly in the HTML document sent to the client. Most often that is done inside a `<script>` tag with a particular identifier.\\n- The client-side hydration: the browser receives the HTML and displays it (a fast paint), then downloads the application's JavaScript bundle. The application's code 'mounts' onto the DOM structure that already exists. At that stage it reads the embedded state from the `<script>` tag and uses it as its initial state, instead of initializing the state as empty or making a new request.\\n\\nA practical example (Next.js with React)\\nIn Next.js the `getServerSideProps` or `getStaticProps` method is often used for this.\\n\\nOn the client the framework will pick that data up automatically. In other ecosystems (Vue with Nuxt, for example) the principle is the same, but the implementation can differ in its syntax.\\n\\nWhere and why it is used\\nThis approach is critically important for:\\n- Performance: it removes an unnecessary network request, speeding the time to interactivity up.\\n- SEO: search bots see a fully formed page with its data.\\n- The user experience: it rules out flicker or a loading state when moving between an SPA's pages.\\n\\nConclusion: the hydration of state is worth applying in any application where the speed of the first load and SEO matter, especially in online shops, media sites and catalogues, where the initial data is known at the moment the page is rendered.\",\"// The page pages/product/[id].js\\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context) {\\n  const { id } = context.params;\\n  // The data request on the server\\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/products/${id}`);\\n  const product = await res.json();\\n\\n  // We return the props, which will reach the page\\n  return {\\n    props: {\\n      product // This state will be passed to the client\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The page component will receive product as a prop\\nfunction ProductPage({ product }) {\\n  // On the client product is already available without a request\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{product.name}</h1>\\n      <p>{product.description}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Next.js serializes the `product` object automatically\\n// and inserts it into the HTML inside __NEXT_DATA__.\"],[2359,\"State management in micro-frontends can be organized in several ways. The simplest is isolation: each micro-frontend manages its own state independently. To exchange data between them you can use the Event Bus pattern, publishing and subscribing to events through window or a library. In more complex scenarios Shared Global State is used — a shared store available to every micro-frontend, through Redux or the host application's context, for example. The key task is to minimize the coupling, so that changes in one module do not break the others.\",\"Micro-frontends are an architectural style in which a web application is broken into independent, autonomous parts, developed and deployed separately. Managing state in such a system is a non-trivial task, since you have to balance the modules' independence against the need to exchange data.\\n\\nThe main approaches to managing state\\n\\n- Isolated state: each micro-frontend (MFE) manages its own state inside its own bundle. That is ideal for maximum independence and reuse. If the MFEs need to display the same data (the user's data, for example), however, it will have to be loaded several times.\\n- Shared state through an Event Bus: the MFEs communicate with one another by publishing and listening for events. That can be implemented through the browser's native CustomEvent API or through libraries such as `mitt`. When the cart is updated in one MFE, for example, an event can be sent that the other modules displaying the number of items subscribe to.\\n- Shared Global State: a single store is created (an instance of Redux or MobX, or even a simple object on window), available to every MFE. Initializing and providing it is normally the host application's (the shell's) responsibility. This approach requires clear contracts and can increase the coupling.\\n- State at the level of the URL and the browser: part of the state (the ID of the selected item, the filter parameters) can be held in the URL's query parameters or in localStorage/sessionStorage. That is a simple way of synchronizing between MFEs that are mounted on the same page.\\n\\nA practical example with an Event Bus\\nLet us look at a simple example where two independent micro-frontends exchange information about the user.\\n\\nRecommendations and conclusion\\nThe choice of approach depends on the level of integration between the micro-frontends. Begin with maximum isolation, adding shared-state mechanisms (an Event Bus, URL parameters) only as a real need arises. Avoid direct dependencies between the MFEs' bundles. A shared global store is worth applying to data that really is critical to the whole application, such as the authentication or the theme, and its schema has to be managed carefully.\\nIn summary: isolated state is the basis of micro-frontends' independence. An Event Bus and URL parameters are good tools for loose coupling. Use a shared global store judiciously for cross-cutting functionality, so as not to turn your micro-frontends into a monolith at the level of the data.\",\"// In the host application or in a shared module we create a simple Event Bus\\nconst eventBus = {\\n  events: {},\\n  on(event, callback) {\\n    if (!this.events[event]) this.events[event] = [];\\n    this.events[event].push(callback);\\n  },\\n  emit(event, data) {\\n    (this.events[event] || []).forEach(cb => cb(data));\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// Micro-frontend A (the header, for example) subscribes to updates of the user's name\\neventBus.on('user-updated', (userData) => {\\n  document.getElementById('user-name').textContent = userData.name;\\n});\\n\\n// Micro-frontend B (the login form) publishes the event after a successful login\\nfunction handleLoginSuccess(user) {\\n  // ... MFE B's logic\\n  eventBus.emit('user-updated', { name: user.name, id: user.id });\\n}\"],[2360,\"Centralized state is a single global source of data for the whole application, available from any component. Modular state is local state divided into independent parts, each of which is managed by a separate component or module. The centralized approach makes synchronizing data between distant components easier, but it can lead to excessive complexity in small applications. The modular approach makes components more independent and more reusable, but it complicates the exchange of data between them.\",\"State management is a key part of any modern application's architecture. It determines how the data is held, updated and passed between the components. The two main approaches — centralized and modular — offer different trade-offs between simplicity, predictability and flexibility.\\n\\nCentralized state\\nIn this approach all of the application's state is held in one place — a single global store. The components do not own the data directly but subscribe to its changes and dispatch actions in order to update it. That makes the data flow unidirectional and predictable.\\n\\n- The advantages: it is easy to track and to debug the changes of state. Synchronizing the data between a great many components that are not related hierarchically becomes easier. It lets you implement complex business logic in one place.\\n- The disadvantages: it can create excessive complexity (boilerplate) for simple tasks. The global store can become a \\\"black box\\\" into which all of the data is dumped, which makes it harder to understand where it came from.\\n- Where it is used: large applications with complex business logic, a great many interrelated components and a need for time-travel debugging. Examples of the tools: Redux, Zustand, Vuex.\\n\\nModular state\\nHere the state is decentralized and encapsulated in individual components or modules. Each component manages its own data, passing it down the hierarchy through props (in React) or similar mechanisms.\\n\\n- The advantages: the components become more independent and more reusable. The architecture is easier to understand in small projects. There is less boilerplate code.\\n- The disadvantages: exchanging data between components that are not directly related (sibling ones, for example) becomes difficult and can lead to \\\"prop drilling\\\". It is harder to see the overall picture of the application's state.\\n- Where it is used: small and medium applications, widgets, components with isolated logic. The frameworks' native approaches: local state in React (useState), Vue components, services in Angular.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a simple counter. In the modular approach the state lives inside the component:\\n\\nIn the centralized approach the state is moved into a global store (the example uses Redux Toolkit):\\n\\nConclusion: centralized state is worth applying in large applications, where a great many components have to respond to the same data or where strict predictability of the changes matters. Modular state is ideal for small projects, for isolated components, or when you want to avoid the overhead of global state management.\",\"// Modular state (a React component)\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // The local state\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Centralized state (a Redux slice)\\nimport { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'counter',\\n  initialState: { value: 0 },\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment(state) { state.value += 1; },\\n  },\\n});\\n// The component connects to the store\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector(state => state.counter.value);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch(counterSlice.actions.increment())}>\\n        Increment\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2361,\"When a component uses a lot of useState calls, that can lead to frequent re-renders and reduced performance. It can be optimized in several ways: combine the related pieces of state into a single object by means of useReducer, so as to update them in one action. Use memoization through React.memo, useMemo and useCallback, in order to prevent unnecessary computation and renders of the child components. It is also worth splitting a large component into smaller ones, isolating the pieces of state that change independently. That will reduce the area that repaints.\",\"When a React component contains a great many `useState` calls, every state update causes the whole component to re-render. That can become a performance problem, especially if some pieces of state change often while others rarely do, or if the render involves heavy computation.\\n\\nThe main optimization strategies\\n\\n- Combining the state: instead of a great many separate `useState` calls for related data, use `useReducer` or a single `useState` with an object. That lets you update several fields in one action, cutting the number of re-renders down.\\n- Memoization: apply `React.memo` to the component, in order to prevent a re-render if its props have not changed. Use `useMemo` to cache expensive computations and `useCallback` for stable references to the functions passed down to child components.\\n- Splitting the components: break a large component into smaller ones, each with its own state. That isolates the re-renders: only the subcomponent whose state has changed updates.\\n- Lazy initialization: for initial state that requires complex computation, use lazy initialization by passing a function to `useState`.\\n\\nAn example of using useReducer\\nInstead of a great many `useState` calls, combine the logic in a reducer:\\n\\nAn example of memoization with React.memo and useCallback\\n\\nIn this example `ExpensiveChild` will not repaint when `count` updates, because its `text` and `handleTextChange` props stay unchanged (thanks to the `useCallback`).\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nSuch optimizations are critical in complex interfaces: dashboards, forms with a great many fields, interactive tables or charts. They help you keep the interface smooth during frequent state updates.\\n\\nConclusion: the optimization of a great many `useState` calls is worth applying when a component starts to \\\"drag\\\" because of frequent re-renders. Begin by combining the state through `useReducer` and splitting the components up, and then add memoization for targeted control over the rendering.\",\"const initialState = { count: 0, text: '', flag: false };\\n\\nfunction reducer(state, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'increment':\\n      return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };\\n    case 'updateText':\\n      return { ...state, text: action.payload };\\n    case 'toggleFlag':\\n      return { ...state, flag: !state.flag };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);\\n  // Now there is one state update instead of three separate ones\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'increment' })}>\\n        Count: {state.count}\\n      </button>\\n      <input\\n        value={state.text}\\n        onChange={e => dispatch({ type: 'updateText', payload: e.target.value })}\\n      />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst ExpensiveChild = React.memo(({ value, onChange }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <input value={value} onChange={onChange} />;\\n});\\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\\n  \\n  // useCallback keeps the reference to the function between renders\\n  const handleTextChange = useCallback((e) => setText(e.target.value), []);\\n  \\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment: {count}</button>\\n      <ExpensiveChild value={text} onChange={handleTextChange} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2362,\"In FSD components within a single layer can and should be reused. That is one of the architecture's main aims — to avoid duplicated code. A button from shared/ui, for example, can be used in several widgets. The reuse should be sensible, however, and must not break the boundaries of the layers. Components must not know about the implementation details of other layers.\",\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for frontend applications, which organizes code by business capability (by feature) and by technical layer. One of its key principles is the management of dependencies and of code reuse.\\n\\nReuse within a layer\\nWithin a single layer (`shared`, `entities`, `features`, for example) the components, functions and other modules are actively reused. That is not only permitted but encouraged, since it reduces duplication and improves consistency. The `shared` layer is created specifically to hold reusable primitives such as UI components, utilities and helpers.\\n\\nAn example of reuse\\nImagine you have a button component in the `shared/ui` layer. It can be used in dozens of places across the whole application, including inside other components of the same `shared` layer (in a modal-window component, for example) or in the components of the layers above.\\n\\nThe important restrictions\\nDespite the freedom within a layer, it is important to observe the direction of the dependencies between the layers. The dependencies can go only from the top down (from the higher layers such as `pages` or `features` to the lower ones such as `entities` and `shared`). A component inside the `features` layer should not import and use a component from another slice of the same `features` layer, unless the shared business logic justifies it — that can lead to strong coupling.\\n\\nIn summary: reusing components within a single layer in FSD is standard, recommended practice for building a consistent, maintainable codebase, but it has to be done with the rules of slice isolation and the direction of the dependencies in mind.\",\"// shared/ui/Button/Button.tsx\\nexport const Button = ({ children, onClick }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <button className=\\\"button\\\" onClick={onClick}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// shared/ui/Modal/Modal.tsx\\nimport { Button } from '../Button/Button';\\nexport const Modal = ({ onClose }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\\"modal\\\">\\n      <p>Modal Content</p>\\n      <Button onClick={onClose}>Close</Button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};\"],[2363,\"Duplicating components in React leads to difficulties in maintenance and to the code growing in size. The main approach is to follow the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle. You can create reusable components by extracting the shared logic and UI. Use component composition, higher-order components (HOCs) and custom hooks in order to separate the shared functionality out. That makes the code cleaner and makes changes easier to introduce.\",\"Duplicated code, especially in React components, is a frequent problem that complicates maintenance and increases the risk of bugs. To avoid it you have to apply the principles of reuse and composition.\\n\\nThe main strategies\\n\\n- Creating reusable components: extract the common parts of the UI (buttons, input fields, cards) into separate components that can be configured through props.\\n- Component composition: use composition rather than inheritance, passing components as children or through special props (render props, for example).\\n- Higher-order components (HOCs): functions that take a component and return a new one with added logic (authentication or data loading, for example).\\n- Custom hooks: they let you extract shared state logic and side effects out of your components.\\n\\nAn example with a custom hook\\nSuppose several components use a counter. Instead of duplicating the useState logic and the handlers, let us create a useCounter hook:\\n\\nAn example with a wrapper component\\nIf several components have to share a style or a behaviour, create a wrapper component:\\n\\nConclusion: avoid duplication by extracting the shared logic into hooks and the shared UI into reusable components. That is especially useful in large projects, where it matters to maintain consistency and to make refactoring easier.\",\"import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useCounter(initialValue = 0) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialValue);\\n  const increment = () => setCount(count + 1);\\n  const decrement = () => setCount(count - 1);\\n  const reset = () => setCount(initialValue);\\n  return { count, increment, decrement, reset };\\n}\\n\\n// Using it in a component\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const { count, increment } = useCounter();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Card({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\\"card\\\">\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  return (\\n    <Card>\\n      <h2>User Name</h2>\\n      <p>Some description</p>\\n    </Card>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2364,\"To reuse a UI component with different sources of data, you have to separate the display logic from the data-fetching logic. The component should receive its data through props — as an array of objects, for example. The data-loading logic can be moved into the parent component, into a custom hook, or the Render Props pattern can be used. That lets one and the same list or table component work with an API, with local state or with mock data.\",\"Reusing a UI component with different sources of data is a fundamental principle of the component-based approach, which improves the flexibility and the testability of code. The basic idea is a separation of responsibility: the component is responsible only for the presentation, while the loading and preparation of the data (the business logic) is delegated outwards.\\n\\nThe key approaches\\n\\n- Props and composition: the simplest way is to pass the data into the component through props. The component knows nothing of where the data came from.\\n- Render Props: the pattern in which a component takes a function (a render prop) that returns a React element. That function receives the data as its argument, which lets a container component manage the data logic.\\n- Custom hooks: ideal for encapsulating the data-fetching logic (fetching, the loading state, the errors). The UI component uses the hook in order to obtain the data and then renders it.\\n- Provider components (Context): useful when the data is needed by many components at different levels of nesting, while the UI component itself still has to stay independent of the particular context.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a `UserList` component that has to display a list of users from an API, from local state (Redux) or from a static array.\\n\\nIn this example `UserList` is entirely reusable. We can easily create another hook, `useUsersFromRedux`, or pass mock data in directly, without changing the list component itself.\\n\\nConclusion: this approach is worth applying whenever you create components that display data. It makes the code predictable, makes testing easier (you can test the component with dummy data) and lets you adapt the interface to changes in the business logic or in the sources of data quickly.\",\"// 1. A purely presentational component, taking its data through props\\nfunction UserList({ users, isLoading, error }) {\\n  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n  if (error) return <p>Error: {error.message}</p>;\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {users.map(user => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 2. A custom hook for loading the data from an API\\nfunction useUsersFromAPI() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setIsLoading(true);\\n    fetch('/api/users')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(setUsers)\\n      .catch(setError)\\n      .finally(() => setIsLoading(false));\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return { users, isLoading, error };\\n}\\n\\n// 3. The parent component, which connects the hook and the UI\\nfunction App() {\\n  const apiData = useUsersFromAPI();\\n  const staticData = { users: [{id: 1, name: 'Alice'}], isLoading: false, error: null };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h2>Users from API:</h2>\\n      <UserList {...apiData} />\\n      <h2>Static Users:</h2>\\n      <UserList {...staticData} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2365,\"Entities in Feature-Sliced Design are separated out when business logic appears that is shared between several features or layers. They are the objects that describe the key concepts of the domain — User, Order, Product, for example. They are separated out so as to avoid duplicating code and to provide a single point of truth for the business rules. That normally happens at the design stage, when it is clear that one and the same data and operations are used in different places.\",\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for frontend applications, which offers clear rules for organizing code. The entities layer sits at the very bottom of the hierarchy and contains the business entities, which are the foundation of the application's domain.\\n\\nWhen should entities be separated out?\\nEntities are worth separating out in the following cases:\\n\\n- Repeated business logic: when one and the same data and the rules for processing it (validation, computation) are used in several features or widgets. A `User` object with methods for checking access rights, for example.\\n- The key concepts of the domain: when the application has central objects that describe the essence of the business (`Order`, `Product`, `Invoice`, for example). Separating them out makes the code more comprehensible and more resistant to change.\\n- Independence from the UI and from external sources: when the logic has to exist separately from the way it is displayed (the UI) or from a particular API. Entities know nothing of React components or HTTP requests.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nLet us look at an example of a `Product` entity in an online-shop application:\\n\\nThis entity encapsulates a product's data and the basic business rules. It can be used by various features: \\\"the product card\\\", \\\"the cart\\\", \\\"the admin panel for managing the products\\\".\\n\\nWhere are entities used?\\nEntities are used in the layers above:\\n\\n- In features, in order to build particular pieces of user logic.\\n- In widgets, in order to compose interfaces that work with that data.\\n- In the shared layer there may be utilities for working with the entities (specific formatters, for example).\\n\\nIt matters that the entities contain no code related to the UI, to the application's state (as Redux is) or to network requests. They represent pure business logic.\\n\\nConclusion: entities in FSD are worth separating out at the early stages of the design, once the stable, reusable concepts of the domain have been identified. That creates a solid foundation, reduces duplication and makes testing the business logic easier.\",\"// src/entities/product/model/product.ts\\ntype Currency = 'USD' | 'EUR';\\n\\nexport class Product {\\n  constructor(\\n    public readonly id: string,\\n    public name: string,\\n    public price: number,\\n    public currency: Currency,\\n    public stockCount: number\\n  ) {}\\n\\n  // A business rule: the product is available to order\\n  isAvailable(): boolean {\\n    return this.stockCount > 0;\\n  }\\n\\n  // A business rule: the formatted price\\n  getFormattedPrice(): string {\\n    return `${this.price} ${this.currency}`;\\n  }\\n\\n  // A business rule: checking whether a discount can be applied\\n  canApplyDiscount(discountPercent: number): boolean {\\n    return discountPercent > 0 && discountPercent <= 50;\\n  }\\n}\"],[2366,\"Composition in React is the approach to building user interfaces in which complex components are created by combining simpler, more specialized ones. Rather than inheritance, React recommends you use composition, passing data and other components in through props (including the special children prop). That makes the code more predictable, more reusable and simpler to test. A button with an icon, for example, can be composed out of a Button and an Icon component instead of being created as a single monolithic component.\",\"Composition is a fundamental principle of React, which means assembling a user interface out of independent, reusable components, as out of building blocks. Instead of creating enormous components that do everything, you break the UI into small, manageable parts and then combine them in order to obtain the result you need. That is the opposite of inheritance, where you might extend classes; React encourages composition through props.\\n\\nHow composition works\\nThe main mechanisms of composition in React:\\n\\n- Passing data through props: you pass data (strings, numbers, arrays, objects) from a parent component to a child one, configuring its behaviour.\\n- Passing React elements and components through props: you can pass whole components or JSX elements as props. The special `children` prop is especially important, as it lets you nest content between a component's opening and closing tags.\\n- Using patterns: patterns such as container and presentational components, or render props, are also based on composition.\\n\\nExamples of composition\\nThe simplest example is using `children`:\\n\\nThe `Card` component knows nothing of its content — it simply renders the `children` it is given. That makes it maximally reusable.\\n\\nA more complex example is composition through explicitly passing components in props:\\n\\nWhere and why to apply it\\nComposition is applied everywhere in React applications, in order to create a flexible, maintainable architecture. It is useful when you need to:\\n\\n- Create reusable wrapper components (modal windows, cards, panels, for example).\\n- Separate the responsibilities between components (a container obtains the data, for example, while a presentational component displays it).\\n- Implement complex UI patterns such as Higher-Order Components (HOCs) or custom hooks, which compose logic.\\n\\nConclusion: composition is the key approach in React to building interfaces. It makes the code modular, easy to test and easy to reuse. Always prefer composition to inheritance when developing in React.\",\"function Card({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\\"card\\\">\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Card>\\n      <h2>A heading</h2>\\n      <p>Some text inside the card.</p>\\n    </Card>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction PageLayout({ header, sidebar, content }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\\"layout\\\">\\n      {header}\\n      {sidebar}\\n      {content}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <PageLayout\\n      header={<Header />}\\n      sidebar={<Sidebar />}\\n      content={<Content />}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}\"],[2367,\"Dependency Inversion is the SOLID principle which states that the modules of the upper level must not depend on the modules of the lower level. Both must depend on abstractions. The abstractions must not depend on the details. The details must depend on the abstractions. On the frontend that means components (React components, for example) must not depend directly on the particular implementations of services (API clients, stores) but must depend on their interfaces (the abstractions). That lets you substitute the implementations easily — for testing (with mocks) or when the backend changes, for example — which makes the code more flexible and more maintainable.\",\"The dependency inversion principle (DIP) is one of the five SOLID principles, which helps you create an architecture that is resistant to change. In frontend development its application is especially important for managing the dependencies between the components of the user interface and the business logic or external services.\\n\\nThe basic idea\\nRather than a component creating an instance of, or importing, a particular service directly (`axios` for HTTP requests, or `localStorage` for working with data, for example), it should receive that service from outside through parameters (props, constructor arguments, context hooks). The dependency is defined through an abstraction — an interface or a class with a particular contract of methods.\\n\\nHow this is applied\\nLet us look at a typical example: a component that loads a list of users. Without DIP the component depends directly on a particular HTTP-request library.\\n\\nWith DIP applied, we define an abstraction (an interface `UserService`, for example) and inject its implementation from outside.\\n\\nThe practical advantages\\n\\n - Testability: it is easy to substitute a mock implementation for the real service in unit tests.\\n - Flexibility: replacing the backend (moving from REST to GraphQL, for example) requires changing only one implementation of the service, not all of the components.\\n - Loose coupling: the components know only about the contract and not about the details of the implementation, which makes refactoring and reuse easier.\\n\\nConclusion: apply Dependency Injection on the frontend for the key services (the API, the state, local storage), especially in large applications where testability and the ability to replace implementations matter. It makes the code cleaner, more modular and readier for change.\",\"// BAD: a direct dependency on axios\\nimport axios from 'axios';\\n\\nfunction UserList() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    axios.get('/api/users').then(response => setUsers(response.data));\\n  }, []);\\n  // ... the rendering\\n}\\n\\n// GOOD: a dependency on an abstraction\\n// 1. We define an interface (in TypeScript) or a contract\\ninterface UserService {\\n  getUsers(): Promise<User[]>;\\n}\\n\\n// 2. The component receives the service through props (or context)\\nfunction UserList({ userService }: { userService: UserService }) {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    userService.getUsers().then(setUsers);\\n  }, [userService]);\\n  // ... the rendering\\n}\\n\\n// 3. A particular implementation somewhere at the top level of the application\\nconst axiosUserService: UserService = {\\n  getUsers: () => axios.get('/api/users').then(res => res.data)\\n};\\n\\n// 4. We inject the implementation when using the component\\n<UserList userService={axiosUserService} />\"],[2368,\"In React JSX can be passed inside a component in two main ways. The first is through the special `children` prop, by placing the JSX between the component's opening and closing tags. The second is through any other prop, by passing the JSX as its value. The `children` prop is the standard and the most intuitive way of passing markup, and it makes the component a wrapper. Passing through a named prop gives more control — for rendering content in different places within the component, for example.\",\"In React the composition of components is a fundamental concept, and passing JSX is a key part of it. JSX is a syntactic extension of JavaScript that looks like HTML and compiles into `React.createElement()` calls. Passing JSX inside a component lets you create abstractions and reuse logic with different content.\\n\\nThe main ways of passing JSX\\nThere are two main approaches to passing JSX into a component.\\n\\n1. Using the `children` prop\\nThis is the most widespread and the most idiomatic way. When you place JSX between a component's opening and closing tags, React passes that JSX into the `props.children` prop automatically.\\n\\nInside `Card`, everything between the `<Card>` and `</Card>` tags becomes the value of `children`. That can be simple text, an element, an array of elements or even another component.\\n\\n2. Passing JSX through named props\\nSometimes you need to pass several independent fragments of JSX, or to state explicitly where they should be inserted. In that case you can use ordinary props whose value is JSX.\\n\\nThis approach gives more clarity and control over the component's structure, especially in complex layouts.\\n\\nThe practical aspects and the application\\n\\n - Wrapper components: `children` is ideal for creating containers, modal windows, cards or context providers, where the inner content is dynamic.\\n - Render Props: this is an advanced pattern in which a component takes a function (often in a prop named `render`) that returns JSX. That lets the component share its internal state with the function it is given.\\n - Conditional rendering: the JSX you pass can be rendered conditionally inside the receiving component, using the standard JavaScript constructs (`if`, `&&`, the ternary operator).\\n\\nConclusion: use the `children` prop for the simple cases, where the component acts as a wrapper for arbitrary content. Apply named props with JSX when you need to separate several areas of content explicitly or when greater structural clarity is required. Both methods underlie the composition and reuse of code in React applications.\",\"// A wrapper component\\nfunction Card({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\\"card\\\">\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Card>\\n      <h2>A heading</h2>\\n      <p>This is the card's content, passed in as children.</p>\\n    </Card>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// A component with several slots for content\\nfunction Layout({ header, sidebar, mainContent }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\\"layout\\\">\\n      {header}\\n      {sidebar}\\n      {mainContent}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Layout\\n      header={<h1>My site</h1>}\\n      sidebar={<nav>Menu</nav>}\\n      mainContent={<article>The main article...</article>}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}\"],[2369,\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) can run into difficulties when modelling complex processes that cut across several features or layers. The main limitations include the difficulty of managing the dependencies between isolated slices, potential duplication of logic and more complicated routing for end-to-end processes. The methodology suits clearly separated functional blocks best, rather than tightly interwoven workflows. Complex processes may require an additional global state-management layer or an adaptation of FSD's rules.\",\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for frontend applications, which offers a clear separation of code by business feature and by technical layer. When building complex processes, however — multi-stage payment, user onboarding or a workflow with feedback between the modules — its limitations can show.\\n\\nFSD's key limitations for complex processes\\n\\n- Rigid slice boundaries: complex processes often require close interaction between several features (\\\"the cart\\\", \\\"payment\\\", \\\"notifications\\\", for example). In FSD addressing one slice directly from another is forbidden, which can lead to redundant proxy layers or to the data flow becoming more complicated.\\n- Managing the process's state: the state that relates to the process (the current step of placing an order, for example) can end up smeared across several slices. Placing it in a global store (Redux) partly breaks FSD's principle of isolation.\\n- Routing and navigation: complex processes often have their own multi-step routing (/checkout/step1, /checkout/step2), which has to be coordinated between the \\\"features\\\" and \\\"pages\\\" layers. That can create a pile-up in the pages layer.\\n- Potential duplication of logic: if different features in the process require similar validations or data transformations, there can be a temptation to duplicate the code so as not to break the slice boundaries.\\n\\nAn example of the code: the problem with the process's state\\nLet us look at the process of placing an order, which touches the `cart`, `shipping` and `payment` features. In ideal FSD each feature manages its own state. But the state of the process itself (`currentStep`, for example) belongs to none of them explicitly.\\n\\nPractical approaches to softening the limitations\\n\\n- Separating \\\"processes\\\" out as a distinct entity: you can create a \\\"processes\\\" layer or use shared hooks for the coordination, while observing FSD's rules about the direction of the dependencies (the higher layers depend on the lower ones).\\n- Using a global event bus or a state manager: for communication between slices you can apply carefully designed global events, without creating direct imports.\\n- Adapting FSD's rules: in large projects the strictness of the rules is sometimes relaxed for cross-cutting concerns, by introducing explicit exceptions for complex business processes.\\n\\nConclusion: Feature-Sliced Design is excellent for structuring applications with clear, independent functional blocks. For complex, multi-step processes that require intensive interaction between the features, however, an additional architectural layer or a compromise in the methodology's strictness may be needed, in order to avoid excessive complexity in the code.\",\"// The problematic placement of the process's state inside a single feature\\n// features/checkout/model/store.ts\\nexport const $checkoutProcess = createStore({\\n  currentStep: 'cart',\\n  isCompleted: false,\\n  // Does the data from the other features have to be held here?\\n  shippingData: null,\\n  paymentData: null,\\n});\\n\\n// Now the shipping feature has to interact with this store,\\n// which creates a dependency on the checkout slice.\"],[2370,\"Frontend development uses various architectural approaches, such as MVC, MVVM, Flux (and its implementation Redux), as well as modular and component-oriented architectures. Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is a methodology for structuring a project, which groups code by business capability (by feature) rather than by technical layer. Its essence is the separation of the application into isolated, loosely coupled modules (layers) such as shared, entities, features, widgets, pages and app. That improves the reuse of code, makes navigating the codebase easier and reduces the risk of unintentional dependencies between unrelated parts of the application.\",\"In modern frontend development the choice of an architecture is critically important for supporting the growth of the application and of the team. Besides the classic patterns such as MVC, approaches oriented towards components and state management, such as Flux/Redux, have gained popularity. As projects grow more complex, however, the problem of \\\"spaghetti code\\\" arises, where the dependencies between the modules become tangled. Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) offers a solution through a clear separation of responsibility on the basis of the business logic.\\n\\nFSD's main principles\\nFSD organizes code as vertical slices (features), each of which represents a complete business capability. The architecture is built on several key layers:\\n\\n- Shared: reusable utilities, UI kits and helpers that are not tied to the business logic.\\n- Entities: the business entities (User, Product, for example), their models and basic operations.\\n- Features: particular user actions and interactive logic (authentication, adding a product to the cart, for example).\\n- Widgets: compositional blocks, which assemble several features and entities for display.\\n- Pages: the application's entry points, composing widgets for particular routes.\\n- App: the global configuration, the providers, the styles, the initialization.\\n\\nHow and where FSD is applied\\nFSD is especially useful in large projects with long-term maintenance and big teams, where several developers work on different features simultaneously. It is applied in the React/Next.js, Vue and Angular ecosystems and in other frameworks. The approach provides a predictable folder structure, which makes onboarding new developers and refactoring easier.\\n\\nA practical example of the structure\\nLet us look at a simplified structure for an e-commerce project, organized by FSD:\\n\\nIn this example the `add-to-cart` feature is isolated and contains all of the logic and the UI needed for adding a product to the cart. It can use the `product` entity and the common components from `shared/ui`, but it does not depend directly on other features, which minimizes the coupling.\\n\\nConclusion: Feature-Sliced Design is worth applying in medium and large frontend projects, where scalability, a clear separation of responsibility and ease of maintenance matter. It helps you avoid chaos in the codebase, by making the dependencies explicit and manageable.\",\"src/\\n  app/                 # The application's initialization, the routing\\n    providers.tsx\\n    router.tsx\\n  pages/              # The pages\\n    catalog/\\n      index.tsx\\n    cart/\\n      index.tsx\\n  widgets/            # The widgets (compositional blocks)\\n    product-list/\\n      ui/ProductCard.tsx\\n    header/\\n      ui/Header.tsx\\n  features/           # The features (business actions)\\n    add-to-cart/\\n      api/addToCart.ts\\n      ui/AddToCartButton.tsx\\n    product-search/\\n      lib/searchLogic.ts\\n      ui/SearchBar.tsx\\n  entities/           # The entities\\n    product/\\n      model/types.ts\\n      api/productApi.ts\\n    user/\\n      model/userSlice.ts\\n  shared/             # The common modules\\n    ui/\\n      Button.tsx\\n      Input.tsx\\n    lib/\\n      axiosInstance.ts\\n      constants.ts\"],[2371,\"Decomposing components is the separation of a large component into several small ones, each of which is responsible for one particular task. That makes the code cleaner, more comprehensible and easier to test. The main principles: the Single Responsibility Principle (one component — one responsibility), extracting reusable logic into custom hooks, and separating presentational and container components. Always begin by extracting the repeated parts of the interface and the complex logic.\",\"Decomposing components is a fundamental skill in React development, which turns monolithic components that are hard to understand into a tree of small, manageable and often reusable parts. The main aim is to improve readability, to make testing easier and to facilitate working together in a team.\\n\\nThe key principles of decomposition\\n\\n- The single responsibility principle (SRP): every component should solve one particular task. If a component starts doing too much (loading the data, rendering a complex list and handling a form, for example), it is worth splitting up.\\n- Extracting the reusable parts of the UI: the repeated elements of the interface (buttons, cards, input fields with shared logic) should be moved into separate components.\\n- Separating the logic and the presentation: use the \\\"containers and presentational components\\\" pattern. The containers manage the state and the logic, while the presentational components only render the data they receive through props.\\n- Using custom hooks: complex business logic or the logic of working with side effects can be encapsulated in custom hooks, which makes the components cleaner.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a monolithic user-profile component, which loads the data and displays it.\\n\\nNow let us decompose it:\\n\\nAs a result we have obtained several independent modules: a hook for the data, a pure display component, an indicator component and the main component that coordinates them. Each part can now be tested and changed separately.\\n\\nConclusion: decomposition is worth applying whenever a component becomes too big or performs several unrelated tasks. It is especially useful in large applications, for maintaining architectural cleanliness and making refactoring easier.\",\"// Bad: one big component\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetchUser().then(data => {\\n      setUser(data);\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{user.name}</h1>\\n      <img src={user.avatar} alt=\\\"\\\" />\\n      <p>Email: {user.email}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => alert('Edited!')}>Edit Profile</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 1. A custom hook for the data-loading logic\\nfunction useUser(userId) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetchUser(userId).then(data => {\\n      setUser(data);\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    });\\n  }, [userId]);\\n\\n  return { user, loading };\\n}\\n\\n// 2. A presentational component for displaying the profile\\nfunction UserProfileView({ user, onEdit }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{user.name}</h1>\\n      <img src={user.avatar} alt=\\\"\\\" />\\n      <p>Email: {user.email}</p>\\n      <button onClick={onEdit}>Edit Profile</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 3. A reusable loading component\\nfunction Loader() {\\n  return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n}\\n\\n// 4. The main container component, which ties it all together\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const { user, loading } = useUser(userId);\\n  const handleEdit = () => alert('Edited!');\\n\\n  if (loading) return <Loader />;\\n  return <UserProfileView user={user} onEdit={handleEdit} />;\\n}\"],[2372,\"The extensibility of a component means it can easily be modified or supplemented with new logic without breaking the existing code. The main approaches: using composition (children, render props, slots, for example), passing configuration in through props, and applying patterns such as Higher-Order Components or hooks. It matters to design components in accordance with the open/closed principle: they should be open for extension but closed for modification. That reduces the coupling and makes maintenance easier.\",\"The extensibility of a component is the ability to add new behaviour or to change existing behaviour without changing its source code. That is a key principle for supporting scalability and the reuse of code in large applications.\\n\\nThe main approaches to implementing it\\n\\n- Composition through children or slots: it lets you nest arbitrary content or other components inside a component, delegating part of its responsibility to the outside code.\\n- Configuration through props: passing parameters, callbacks, styles and other data in through props makes a component flexible. You can add a new event handler, for example, simply by passing it in as a prop.\\n- Render Props and scoped slots: the patterns in which a component receives a function for rendering part of its UI, which gives complete control over the content displayed from the outside.\\n- Higher-Order Components (HOCs): functions that take a component and return a new one, enriched with additional logic. They let you add functionality (a subscription to data, for example) without changing the original component.\\n- Custom hooks (in React): they let you extract and reuse state and side effects, making components cleaner and more extensible through the composition of logic.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a button component that has to be made extensible, in order to support different icons, themes and sizes.\\n\\nIn this example the `Button` component is extensible thanks to: taking `children` for any text, the `variant` and `size` parameters for the styles, the `icon` prop for displaying an icon, and the use of `...restProps` for passing any additional attributes (`disabled`, `aria-*`, for example). To add a new theme or type you do not have to change the component itself — it is enough to pass new props.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe principles of extensibility are critically important when developing design systems, libraries of UI components (Material-UI, Ant Design, for example) and in any long-term project where the requirements change often. They also help with teamwork, by letting different developers modify parts of the system independently.\\n\\nConclusion: create extensible components when you foresee that the functionality will grow or change over time, especially in public libraries or in large applications with many teams. That reduces the technical debt and makes adding new features easier.\",\"// An extensible Button component in React\\nconst Button = ({ \\n  children, \\n  onClick, \\n  variant = 'primary', \\n  size = 'medium', \\n  icon: Icon, // The icon component can be passed in as a prop\\n  ...restProps // All of the remaining props (className, disabled, for example) are passed on\\n}) => {\\n  const baseClasses = 'btn';\\n  const variantClass = `btn-${variant}`; // primary, secondary, etc.\\n  const sizeClass = `btn-${size}`;       // small, medium, large\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button\\n      className={`${baseClasses} ${variantClass} ${sizeClass}`}\\n      onClick={onClick}\\n      {...restProps}\\n    >\\n      {Icon && <Icon className=\\\"btn-icon\\\" />}\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst MyComponent = () => (\\n  <div>\\n    <Button onClick={() => alert('Clicked!')}>\\n      An ordinary button\\n    </Button>\\n    <Button variant=\\\"secondary\\\" size=\\\"large\\\" icon={SendIcon} disabled>\\n      Send\\n    </Button>\\n  </div>\\n);\"],[2373,\"Feature-Sliced Design divides an application into several horizontal layers, each of which has a strictly defined area of responsibility. The main layers: 'app' (the application's initialization and the global settings), 'pages' (the composition of entities and features to form the pages), 'features' (interactive logic tied to the domain), 'entities' (the basic business entities, such as User or Product), 'shared' (reusable technical components and utilities). Such a separation lets you manage the dependencies (a layer can depend only on the ones below it) and makes navigating the codebase easier.\",\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for frontend applications, which organizes code by horizontal layers and vertical slices. The layers define the level of abstraction and the responsibility of the code, while the slices group the code by business domain. The main aim is to create a predictable, scalable, maintainable project structure in which the dependencies are strictly regulated: code can depend only on layers that are at the same or a lower level of abstraction.\\n\\nFSD's main layers (from the top down)\\n\\n- app — the topmost layer; it is responsible for the application's initialization, the global settings, the providers (the routing, the styles, the store, for example) and the general start-up logic. This is the application's entry point.\\n- pages — the layer that composes entities and features to form the application's particular pages. Every page corresponds to a route and normally contains no complex business logic but only assembles components from the layers below.\\n- features — the layer for interactive logic tied to the domain (authentication, comments, the cart, for example). The user scenarios (features) are placed here, and they can include components, hooks and stores specific to that feature.\\n- entities — the layer of the basic business entities, such as User, Product, Order. These entities are independent of the particular features and represent the fundamental concepts of the domain. They normally contain display components, types and data models.\\n- shared — the lowest layer, containing reusable technical components, utilities, helpers, the UI kit (buttons, inputs) and the configurations. This layer is not tied to the business logic and is used by all of the layers above.\\n\\nAn example of a project's structure\\nHere is how a file structure reflecting FSD's layers might look:\\n\\nHow and where FSD is applied\\nFSD is especially useful in large frontend projects with long-term maintenance and team-based development. It helps you:\\n\\n- Separate the responsibilities between the parts of the application clearly.\\n- Manage the dependencies (a feature cannot import code from pages, for example, but it can from entities and shared).\\n- Make navigating the code easier, thanks to a single structure.\\n- Improve the reusability of the components and the logic.\\n\\nThe methodology is often used with the React/TypeScript stack, but it can be adapted to other frameworks too.\\n\\nConclusion: Feature-Sliced Design is worth applying in medium and large frontend applications, where scalability, a clear organization of the code and the management of the dependencies between the modules matter. For small projects or prototypes, adopting FSD can be excessive.\",\"src/\\n  app/                    # The application's initialization, the providers\\n    providers/\\n    styles/\\n    App.tsx\\n  pages/                  # The application's pages\\n    home/\\n      ui/\\n        HomePage.tsx\\n    about/\\n  features/               # The features (user scenarios)\\n    auth-by-phone/\\n      ui/\\n      model/\\n      lib/\\n    toggle-theme/\\n  entities/               # The business entities\\n    user/\\n      ui/\\n        UserCard.tsx\\n      model/\\n        types.ts\\n    product/\\n  shared/                 # The reusable code\\n    ui/\\n      Button.tsx\\n      Input.tsx\\n    lib/\\n      api/\\n      helpers/\"],[2374,\"The open/closed principle states that components should be open for extension but closed for modification. In the context of the UI that means a base component should not change when new functionality is added. Instead, the new logic is added through composition, inheritance (rarely) or the passing of parameters. A button can be extended by passing new props or by wrapping it in a HOC, for example, rather than by changing its source code. That makes the code more predictable and makes testing easier.\",\"The open/closed principle (OCP) is the second SOLID principle, which asserts that software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension but closed for modification. In the world of components, especially in React, Vue or Angular, that means a base component should be designed so that its behaviour can be extended without changing its source code.\\n\\nHow does this work in components?\\nThe main idea is to anticipate the points of change and to make them configurable. Rather than adding conditions (if/else) or new props for every new case, a component should provide abstractions (slots, render props, HOCs, for example) for the configuration.\\n\\n- Composition: the most frequent approach. A base component is created that takes child elements or functions for rendering parts of the UI. A `Modal` component can take a `header`, a `body` and a `footer` as children, for example.\\n- Props and configuration: the component is configured through props. It matters that adding new functionality should not require changing the existing checks inside the component but should simply be passed in as a new parameter.\\n- Render Props / children as a function: they let you determine dynamically what to render inside a component and how, delegating the rendering logic to the calling code.\\n- Higher-order components (HOCs): wrappers that add new behaviour (logic, props) to an existing component without changing its source.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nLet us look at a button component that initially supports only a primary colour. If later on an icon, sizes and loading states have to be added, the bad approach is to add a heap of props and conditional logic to a single component. The good approach is to make the component extensible through composition.\\n\\nIn this example `Button` stays simple and stable. To add an icon we do not reach inside `Button` but create a new `IconButton` component, which composes the original `Button` and adds the markup needed. That is OCP in action.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nThe principle is critically important in design systems and libraries of components, where the base components (buttons, inputs, cards) have to be resistant to change while still allowing customization for different projects. OCP also helps when developing plug-in architectures, where third-party developers can extend the core's functionality without rewriting it.\\n\\nConclusion: apply the open/closed principle in components when you are creating a library or a set of base UI elements that will be used in different parts of the application or by different teams. That reduces the risk of breaking the existing code when new functionality is added and makes the system more flexible.\",\"// The base Button component (closed for modification)\\nconst Button = ({ children, onClick, variant = 'primary', ...props }) => {\\n  const baseStyle = 'px-4 py-2 rounded font-medium';\\n  const variants = {\\n    primary: 'bg-blue-600 text-white',\\n    secondary: 'bg-gray-300 text-black',\\n  };\\n  return (\\n    <button className={`${baseStyle} ${variants[variant]}`} onClick={onClick} {...props}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Extension through composition (open for extension)\\nconst IconButton = ({ icon, label, ...buttonProps }) => (\\n  <Button {...buttonProps}>\\n    <span className=\\\"flex items-center gap-2\\\">\\n      {icon}\\n      {label}\\n    </span>\\n  </Button>\\n);\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <div>\\n    <Button onClick={() => alert('Clicked!')}>Basic Button</Button>\\n    <IconButton\\n      icon={<StarIcon />}\\n      label=\\\"With Icon\\\"\\n      variant=\\\"secondary\\\"\\n      onClick={() => console.log('Icon button clicked')}\\n    />\\n  </div>\\n);\"],[2375,\"The single responsibility principle (SRP) in React means that every component should solve one particular task. A component for displaying a list of users should not also manage the state of a form for adding them, for example. That makes the components simpler to understand, to test and to reuse. Observing SRP helps you avoid \\\"bloated\\\" components that are hard to maintain.\",\"The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) is one of the key SOLID principles, which states that a module (in the context of React a component, a hook or a function) should have one and only one reason to change. In React that means designing components that are responsible for one clear part of the user interface or of the logic.\\n\\nHow to apply SRP in components\\nRather than creating a large component that displays the data, handles the events and manages the state, you should break it into several small ones:\\n\\n- Presentational (\\\"dumb\\\") components: they are responsible only for rendering the UI on the basis of the props they receive. `UserList`, for example, which simply maps an array of users into a list of elements.\\n- Container (\\\"smart\\\") components: they manage the state and the business logic and pass the data into the presentational components. `UserListContainer`, for example, which loads the data from an API and passes it into `UserList`.\\n- Custom hooks: move repeated logic (a subscription to data, managing a form, for example) into separate hooks. That makes the components cleaner.\\n\\nAn example of the code: breaking SRP and observing it\\nLet us look at a component that breaks SRP, since it combines loading the data, displaying it and handling the clicks:\\n\\nNow let us separate the responsibilities, using a custom hook for the data logic and extracting a presentational component:\\n\\nWhere and why to apply it\\nApplying SRP is especially important in medium and large applications. It makes the following easier:\\n\\n- Testing: small components with a clear task are easy to cover with unit tests.\\n- Reuse: presentational components such as `UserList` can be used in different parts of the application with different data.\\n- Maintenance: changing the data-loading logic does not touch the rendering code, and vice versa.\\n- Collaboration: different developers can work on different responsibilities (the logic vs. the UI) with minimal conflicts.\\n\\nConclusion: apply the single responsibility principle in React, in order to create modular, flexible, easily maintainable components. Separate your components into ones that manage the logic (containers, hooks) and ones that are responsible for the display. That is especially useful in long-term projects and in teams, where the readability and the testability of the code matter.\",\"// BAD: a component with a great many responsibilities\\nfunction BadUserComponent() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    fetch('/api/users')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUsers(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const handleDelete = (id) => {\\n    // The deletion logic...\\n  };\\n\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h2>User List</h2>\\n      <ul>\\n        {users.map(user => (\\n          <li key={user.id}>\\n            {user.name}\\n            <button onClick={() => handleDelete(user.id)}>Delete</button>\\n          </li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// A hook for managing the users' data (one responsibility)\\nfunction useUsers() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    fetch('/api/users')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUsers(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const deleteUser = (id) => {\\n    // The deletion logic...\\n  };\\n\\n  return { users, loading, deleteUser };\\n}\\n\\n// A presentational component for the list (one responsibility — the rendering)\\nfunction UserList({ users, onDelete }) {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {users.map(user => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>\\n          {user.name}\\n          <button onClick={() => onDelete(user.id)}>Delete</button>\\n        </li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The main container component, which ties the logic and the presentation together\\nfunction GoodUserComponent() {\\n  const { users, loading, deleteUser } = useUsers();\\n\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h2>User List</h2>\\n      <UserList users={users} onDelete={deleteUser} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2376,\"When an element is deleted and the key equals the array index, React can update the DOM incorrectly, which leads to errors in the components' state. That happens because the keys help React identify the elements between renders. If the key is the index and an element is deleted, the indexes of all of the following elements shift, and React can confuse which element corresponds to which piece of state. The state (the input in a field) can \\\"jump\\\" onto another element, for example. You should therefore always use stable unique identifiers (the id from the data, for example).\",\"In React, when rendering lists by means of `map()`, you have to set a unique `key` for every element. The keys help React compare the virtual DOM between updates efficiently, determining which elements have been added, changed or deleted.\\n\\nThe problem with key=index\\nIf an element's index in the array is used as the key, that can lead to serious problems when the order of the elements changes (sorting, addition, deletion). Let us look at an example of a list of tasks where every task has an input field.\\n\\nSuppose the user has typed \\\"Updated B\\\" in the second input field (index 1) and has then deleted the first task (index 0). After the deletion the array of tasks will change: the element with id=2 (the old index 1) will become the first (the new index 0). React, however, seeing the keys 0, 1, 2, will think that the element with key=0 (the old task A) has been deleted and that the elements with key=1 and key=2 have simply shifted up. As a result the input state (\\\"Updated B\\\") will stay bound to key 1, which now corresponds to task C rather than to task B. The user will see their input \\\"jump\\\" onto another row.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nInstead of the index you should use a stable unique identifier from the data — `task.id`, for example. Then React will be able to track every element properly, independently of its position in the list.\\n\\nThat guarantees the component's state (including the local input state, the focus and other DOM attributes) will be preserved correctly for the right element even after changes in the list.\\n\\nConclusion: using the index as the key is permissible only for static lists that never change their order, are not filtered and have no state. In dynamic lists always apply unique stable keys from the data, in order to avoid hidden bugs and unpredictable behaviour of the interface.\",\"const TaskList = () => {\\n  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([\\n    { id: 1, text: 'Task A' },\\n    { id: 2, text: 'Task B' },\\n    { id: 3, text: 'Task C' },\\n  ]);\\n\\n  const removeTask = (indexToRemove) => {\\n    setTasks(tasks.filter((_, index) => index !== indexToRemove));\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {tasks.map((task, index) => (\\n        <li key={index}>\\n          <input defaultValue={task.text} />\\n          <button onClick={() => removeTask(index)}>Delete</button>\\n        </li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n{tasks.map((task) => (\\n  <li key={task.id}>\\n    <input defaultValue={task.text} />\\n    <button onClick={() => removeTask(task.id)}>Delete</button>\\n  </li>\\n))}\"],[2377,\"The main hooks for optimization in React are useMemo, useCallback and React.memo (although memo is a HOC rather than a hook). useMemo caches the result of a computation, so as not to recompute it on every render if the dependencies have not changed. useCallback caches the function itself, which is useful when it is passed into child components, so that they do not repaint because a new function has been created. React.memo wraps a component and prevents it re-rendering if its props have not changed.\",\"React provides several tools for optimizing the rendering, among which the `useMemo` and `useCallback` hooks, together with the higher-order component `React.memo`, play the key role. Their main aim is to prevent expensive computations being performed and to prevent redundant re-renders of child components, which is critical for maintaining high performance in complex interfaces.\\n\\nuseMemo\\nThe `useMemo` hook caches (memoizes) the result of a computation. It takes a function that returns a value, plus an array of dependencies. The value is recomputed only when one of the dependencies changes. That is ideal for heavy computations — filtering or sorting large lists, for example.\\n\\nuseCallback\\nThe `useCallback` hook caches the function itself rather than its result. It returns a memoized version of the function, which changes only when the dependencies change. That matters especially when the function is passed as a prop into a child component optimized by means of `React.memo`, in order to avoid its redundant re-renders caused by a new function being created on every render of the parent.\\n\\nReact.memo\\n`React.memo` is a higher-order component (HOC) rather than a hook, but it is closely bound up with optimization. It wraps a functional component and prevents it re-rendering if its props have stayed the same (they are compared shallowly). To control the comparison you can pass a comparison function as the second argument.\\n\\nThese tools should be applied deliberately. Premature optimization can complicate the code. Begin optimizing only once real performance problems appear that can be measured with the React DevTools Profiler.\\n\\nConclusion: use `useMemo` to cache expensive computations, `useCallback` to stabilize the references to the functions passed downwards, and `React.memo` to prevent re-renders of pure components. These are the main tools for optimizing the rendering in React applications.\",\"import { useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ExpensiveComponent({ list, filter }) {\\n  const filteredList = useMemo(() => {\\n    console.log('Recalculating filtered list...');\\n    return list.filter(item => item.includes(filter));\\n  }, [list, filter]); // It will be recomputed only if list or filter changes\\n\\n  return <div>{filteredList.join(', ')}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    console.log('Button clicked, count:', count);\\n    // Some logic\\n  }, [count]); // The function will be updated only when count changes\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n      <ChildComponent onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst ChildComponent = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click me</button>;\\n});\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(function MyComponent({ text }) {\\n  console.log('MyComponent rendered with text:', text);\\n  return <div>{text}</div>;\\n});\\n\\n// The component will re-render only if the `text` prop changes\"],[2378,\"SCSS Modules is the approach that combines the capabilities of the SCSS preprocessor with the concept of CSS Modules. CSS Modules is a system that generates unique class names for the selectors automatically, isolating the styles within a particular component. SCSS adds mixins, nesting, variables and other conveniences to that. As a result the developer writes SCSS code in separate files (Button.module.scss, for example), and the bundler (Webpack, for example) transforms it into CSS with unique class names, guaranteeing that one component's styles will not affect another.\",\"SCSS Modules is a technology that unites the power of the SCSS preprocessor with CSS Modules' mechanism for isolating styles. It solves the classic problem of CSS's global namespace, where styles can conflict unexpectedly in large applications.\\n\\nHow this works\\nYou create a file with the extension .module.scss (`Button.module.scss`, for example). Inside it you write ordinary SCSS code, using all of its capabilities: variables, nesting, mixins. During the build (normally with Webpack and the `sass-loader` loader together with `css-loader` in modules mode) the system:\\n- Compiles the SCSS into CSS.\\n- Transforms the class names (`.button`, for example) into unique hashed identifiers (`.Button_button__a5bKc`, for example) automatically.\\n- Creates a mapping object (a JavaScript object), which matches the original class names to the generated ones.\\n\\nThe practical application and an example\\nThis approach is especially popular in React applications. You import the styles object as a module and use the generated class names through dot notation.\\n\\nIn the compiled HTML the button will receive a unique class — `class=\\\"Button_button__a5bKc Button_button--large__d7fGt\\\"`, for example. That guarantees this particular `Button` component's styles will not intersect with anything.\\n\\nThe key advantages\\n\\n- Complete isolation: the styles are limited to the component, which eliminates side effects.\\n- The use of SCSS: all of the preprocessor's advantages (variables, mixins, functions) stay available.\\n- Ease of refactoring: you can rename classes inside a module safely, without fear of breaking other parts of the application.\\n- Explicit dependencies: if a component is deleted, its styles stop being included automatically.\\n\\nIn summary: SCSS Modules is worth applying in component-oriented projects (especially in React or Vue), where modularity and maintainability matter and conflicts of styles have to be avoided. It is the golden mean between purely global styles and CSS-in-JS solutions, offering predictability together with the power of a preprocessor.\",\"// Button.module.scss\\n$primary-color: #3498db;\\n\\n.button {\\n  background-color: $primary-color;\\n  padding: 10px 20px;\\n  border: none;\\n  border-radius: 4px;\\n  color: white;\\n  font-size: 16px;\\n\\n  &:hover {\\n    background-color: darken($primary-color, 10%);\\n  }\\n\\n  &--large {\\n    padding: 15px 30px;\\n    font-size: 18px;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Button.jsx (a React component)\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport styles from './Button.module.scss'; // The import of the styles object\\n\\nconst Button = ({ children, size }) => {\\n  // We choose the class dynamically\\n  const buttonClass = `${styles.button} ${size === 'large' ? styles['button--large'] : ''}`;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button className={buttonClass}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport default Button;\"],[2379,\"Copying data structures during recursion prevents the original data being changed accidentally. Without a copy every call can mutate the shared object, which leads to unpredictable results. That matters especially in functional programming, where the data has to be immutable. A copy guarantees that every recursive step works with its own version of the data.\",\"Why copy the data during recursion?\\nRecursive functions often work with one and the same data structures at different levels of the call. If a copy is not created, the changes made in one call will affect the other calls, which will break the algorithm's logic. That is called a side effect.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at a function that removes an element from an array recursively:\\n\\nHere `arr.splice` changes the original array, which can cause errors in the calling code.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nCreate a copy before changing it:\\n\\nNow every call works with a new copy, and the original array stays untouched.\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\n- Functional programming (React, Redux)\\n- Algorithms on graphs and trees\\n- Processing data without mutation\\n\\nConclusion: copying data during recursion is a basic practice for writing predictable, safe code, especially in immutable architectures.\",\"function removeItem(arr, item) {\\n  const index = arr.indexOf(item);\\n  if (index === -1) return arr;\\n  arr.splice(index, 1); // a mutation of the original array\\n  return removeItem(arr, item);\\n}\\n\\nfunction removeItemSafe(arr, item) {\\n  const index = arr.indexOf(item);\\n  if (index === -1) return arr;\\n  const newArr = [...arr]; // a copy\\n  newArr.splice(index, 1);\\n  return removeItemSafe(newArr, item);\\n}\"],[2380,\"To handle millions of records on the frontend, you must not load all of the data at once. Use server-side pagination with a limit and an offset/cursor, in order to transfer the data in portions. For the display, apply virtual scrolling (react-window, for example), which renders only the visible elements. You can also use lazy loading and the chunking of the data on the server.\",\"The problem of loading large data\\nWhen millions of records have to be delivered to the frontend, transferring the whole array directly will lead to the browser's memory overflowing, to the interface hanging and to an enormous loading time. Techniques are therefore applied that minimize the volume of the data transferred and optimize the rendering.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Server-side pagination: the server returns only one page of data (50 records, for example). The client requests the next page by offset or cursor. That reduces the load on the network and on the memory.\\n- Virtual scrolling: libraries such as react-window or react-virtualized render only those DOM elements that are visible in the browser's window. The remaining records exist only in the data and not in the DOM.\\n- Lazy loading: the data is loaded as the user scrolls (infinite scroll) or on the user's request (a \\\"Load more\\\" button).\\n\\nAn example of implementing pagination on the server (Node.js + Express)\\n\\nAn example of virtual scrolling in React\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo work with millions of records on the frontend, you must combine server-side pagination with virtual scrolling. That will provide fast loading, low memory consumption and smooth scrolling. This approach is applied in tables, lists of messages, logs and any interface with large sets of data.\",\"app.get('/api/users', async (req, res) => {\\n  const { page = 1, limit = 50 } = req.query;\\n  const offset = (page - 1) * limit;\\n  const users = await db.query(\\n    'SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY id LIMIT $1 OFFSET $2',\\n    [limit, offset]\\n  );\\n  res.json({ data: users, total: 1000000 });\\n});\\n\\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n  <div style={style}>Row {index}</div>\\n);\\n\\nconst BigList = () => (\\n  <List height={400} itemCount={1000000} itemSize={35} width={300}>\\n    {Row}\\n  </List>\\n);\"],[2381,\"Testing prefilled forms includes checking that the data is displayed correctly, that it matches the expected values, and how the form behaves when edited. It matters to make sure the data is loaded from the right source (an API or local storage, for example) and is displayed in the fields correctly. You also have to test the scenarios in which the data is absent or incorrect, in order to be sure the errors are handled.\",\"The main aspects of testing prefilled forms\\nPrefilled forms are often used for editing a profile, settings or orders. The testing should cover several key areas: the correctness of loading the data, its display, the interaction with the user and the handling of the edge cases.\\n\\nChecking the loading of the data\\nMake sure the data is loaded from the right source. An API may be called in order to obtain the user's data when the component mounts, for example. The test should check that the request is sent with the right parameters and that the response is handled correctly.\\n\\nTesting the display and the editing\\nOnce the data has loaded, check that the form's fields display the right values. Then test the editing: change a value in a field and make sure the state updates. It also matters to check that the save button becomes active only after a change.\\n\\nThe edge cases and the handling of errors\\nTest the scenarios in which the data is absent (a new user, for example) or in which the API returns an error. The form should display empty fields or an error message correctly. Also check that when the form is submitted with incorrect data (an invalid email, for example), the corresponding messages appear.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTesting prefilled forms requires you to check the loading, the display, the editing and the handling of errors. Use mocks in order to isolate the external dependencies, and test both the successful and the failing scenarios. That ensures the reliability of the user interface and improves the user experience.\",\"// An example of a test in React using Jest and React Testing Library\\nimport { render, screen, waitFor } from '@testing-library/react';\\nimport userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';\\nimport ProfileForm from './ProfileForm';\\n\\njest.mock('./api', () => ({\\n  fetchUserData: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ name: 'John', email: 'john@example.com' }),\\n}));\\n\\ntest('loads and displays user data', async () => {\\n  render(<ProfileForm userId={1} />);\\n  await waitFor(() => {\\n    expect(screen.getByLabelText('Name')).toHaveValue('John');\\n    expect(screen.getByLabelText('Email')).toHaveValue('john@example.com');\\n  });\\n});\\n\\ntest('allows editing and shows save button', async () => {\\n  render(<ProfileForm userId={1} />);\\n  await screen.findByLabelText('Name');\\n  const nameInput = screen.getByLabelText('Name');\\n  await userEvent.clear(nameInput);\\n  await userEvent.type(nameInput, 'Jane');\\n  expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /save/i })).toBeEnabled();\\n});\\n\\ntest('shows error when API fails', async () => {\\n  jest.spyOn(api, 'fetchUserData').mockRejectedValue(new Error('Network error'));\\n  render(<ProfileForm userId={1} />);\\n  await waitFor(() => {\\n    expect(screen.getByText(/failed to load data/i)).toBeInTheDocument();\\n  });\\n});\"],[2382,\"To structure large frontend applications, people use a modular architecture, a separation into features (feature-based), a layered architecture (Feature-Sliced Design, for example) and micro-frontends. The main aim is to separate the code into independent, loosely coupled modules, which makes development, testing and maintenance easier. The choice of approach depends on the scale of the team and of the project.\",\"The main approaches to structuring\\nAs a frontend application grows, a chaotic structure leads to difficulties in maintenance and scaling. There are several proven approaches to organizing code.\\n\\nA modular architecture (feature-based)\\nThe code is grouped by functional module (by feature) rather than by technical role. The `auth` folder, for example, contains its own components, hooks, API requests and styles.\\n\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD)\\nThis is a methodology that divides an application into layers (app, pages, widgets, features, entities, shared) with clear rules about the dependencies. Every layer can use only the ones below it.\\n\\nMicro-frontends\\nThe application is broken into independent parts, which are developed and deployed separately. Each micro-frontend can use its own stack. The integration happens through iframes, Web Components or Module Federation (Webpack 5).\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor small projects a modular architecture is enough. Feature-Sliced Design suits medium and large projects with clear business requirements. Micro-frontends are justified in big teams, where the independent development and deployment of the parts of the application is required.\",\"src/\\n  features/\\n    auth/\\n      components/\\n      hooks/\\n      api.ts\\n      types.ts\\n    profile/\\n      components/\\n      hooks/\\n      api.ts\\n      types.ts\\n  shared/\\n    ui/\\n    lib/\\n\\nsrc/\\n  app/          // the initialization, the routing\\n  pages/        // the pages\\n  widgets/      // self-contained blocks\\n  features/     // the user scenarios\\n  entities/     // the business entities\\n  shared/       // the reusable utilities\\n\\n// An example of the integration through Module Federation\\nnew ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n  name: \\\"app1\\\",\\n  remotes: {\\n    app2: \\\"app2@http://.../remoteEntry.js\\\"\\n  }\\n});\"],[2383,\"When a class component mounts, constructor is called first, then render, then componentDidMount. For functional components the order is analogous: the function's body (the analogue of render), then useEffect. On an update render is called first, then componentDidUpdate or a useEffect with dependencies.\",\"The order in which components are called during the rendering in React\\nReact renders components recursively, starting with the root one. For class components the lifecycle includes several phases: mounting, updating and unmounting. On mounting, constructor is called first, then render, after which React updates the DOM and calls componentDidMount. For functional components the order is simpler: the function's body runs first (this is the analogue of render), then React updates the DOM, and only after that are the effects from useEffect started.\\n\\nAn example for a class component\\n\\nAn example for a functional component\\n\\nThe order on an update\\nWhen the state or the props change, render (or the function's body) is called first, then React updates the DOM, and after that componentDidUpdate (for classes) or a useEffect with the corresponding dependencies (for functions) is called. It matters to remember that useEffect runs asynchronously after the paint, so it does not block the rendering.\\n\\nConclusion: an understanding of the order in which the methods are called helps you avoid mistakes when working with side effects and optimize the performance, especially when using useEffect with dependencies.\",\"class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    console.log('1. constructor');\\n  }\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    console.log('3. componentDidMount');\\n  }\\n  render() {\\n    console.log('2. render');\\n    return <div>Hello</div>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  console.log('1. render (body)');\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('2. useEffect (after mount)');\\n  }, []);\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\"],[2384,\"It is better to do the filtering at the level of the data rather than in the component. That improves the performance, since the component does not repaint needlessly, and it makes testing easier. The component should only display data that has already been filtered.\",\"Where should the filtering of the data be done?\\nIn a React application it is recommended that the filtering of the data be done at the level of the data (in a Redux store, in a server API or in a useMemo hook, for example) rather than directly in the component. That follows from the principle of the separation of responsibility: the component is responsible for the display, while the logic of processing the data should be moved out separately.\\n\\nWhy does this matter?\\n\\n- Performance: filtering in the component can cause redundant repaints, especially if the data is large or the filter changes often.\\n- Testing: filtering logic is simpler to test in isolation, without a tie to the UI.\\n- Reuse: the filtered data can be used in different components.\\n\\nAn example of bad practice (filtering in the component):\\n\\nAn example of good practice (filtering at the level of the data):\\n\\nConclusion: the filtering should be done at the level of the data, in order to preserve the performance, to make testing easier and to make the code more modular. That is especially relevant for large lists or complex filtering conditions.\",\"function UserList({ users }) {\\n  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');\\n  const filteredUsers = users.filter(u => u.name.includes(filter)); // ❌ Filtering in the component\\n  return <div>...</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// In a Redux selector or in a hook\\nconst useFilteredUsers = (users, filter) => {\\n  return useMemo(() => users.filter(u => u.name.includes(filter)), [users, filter]);\\n};\\n\\nfunction UserList({ users }) {\\n  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');\\n  const filteredUsers = useFilteredUsers(users, filter); // ✅ The logic has been moved out\\n  return <div>...</div>;\\n}\"],[2385,\"To filter a tree while preserving the parent nodes, a recursive function is used. It checks every node: if it matches the condition, it is included in the result. If not, the function checks the child nodes recursively. If at least one descendant fits, the parent is preserved. That lets you display only the relevant branches without losing the context.\",\"The basic concept\\nFiltering a tree while preserving the parent nodes is a task that often arises when working with hierarchical data such as file systems, navigation menus or organizational structures. The aim is to filter the nodes by a particular criterion but to preserve all of the parent nodes leading to the filtered elements. That is necessary so that the user sees the complete path to the data found and can understand its context.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe solution is based on a recursive traversal of the tree. For every node we check whether it matches the filtering condition. If it does, the node is included in the result together with all of its descendants (or without them, depending on the requirements). If not, we process the child nodes recursively. If at least one child node has passed the filter, the current parent node is also included in the result, in order to preserve the structure.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Searching in file managers\\n- Filtering the categories in online shops\\n- Navigation menus with a search\\n- Organizational charts\\n\\nConclusion\\nFiltering a tree while preserving the parent nodes is a powerful tool for working with hierarchical data. It lets users find the information they need quickly without losing sight of its context. This approach is especially useful in interfaces where navigating nested structures matters, and implementing it requires an understanding of recursion and of working with trees.\",\"function filterTree(tree, predicate) {\\n  return tree.reduce((acc, node) => {\\n    const children = node.children ? filterTree(node.children, predicate) : [];\\n    if (predicate(node) || children.length > 0) {\\n      acc.push({ ...node, children });\\n    }\\n    return acc;\\n  }, []);\\n}\\n\\n// An example of the usage\\nconst tree = [\\n  { id: 1, name: 'Documents', children: [\\n    { id: 2, name: 'Work', children: [\\n      { id: 3, name: 'Report.pdf' },\\n      { id: 4, name: 'Notes.txt' }\\n    ]},\\n    { id: 5, name: 'Photos', children: [\\n      { id: 6, name: 'Vacation.jpg' }\\n    ]}\\n  ]}\\n];\\n\\nconst filtered = filterTree(tree, node => node.name.includes('Report'));\\nconsole.log(filtered);\\n// The output: [{ id: 1, name: 'Documents', children: [{ id: 2, name: 'Work', children: [{ id: 3, name: 'Report.pdf' }] }] }]\"],[2386,\"There are several approaches to the architecture of frontend applications. MVC (Model-View-Controller) separates the data, the presentation and the control logic. MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) adds a ViewModel layer for binding the data. The component-based approach (React, Vue) builds the interface out of isolated components. Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) organizes code by business feature. Flux/Redux manages the state through a unidirectional data flow.\",\"The main approaches to the architecture of frontend applications\\nA frontend application's architecture determines how the code is organized, how the components interact with one another and how the state is managed. The choice of approach affects the scalability, the testability and the speed of development. Let us look at the most popular approaches.\\n\\nMVC (Model-View-Controller)\\nThe classic pattern, which separates an application into three parts: the Model (the data and the business logic), the View (the display) and the Controller (handling the user's input). It is used in Angular and Backbone.js.\\n\\nMVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)\\nAn extension of MVC, in which the ViewModel binds the View and the Model through two-way data binding. It is actively used in Knockout.js and Vue.js.\\n\\nThe component-based approach\\nThe application is built out of isolated, reusable components. Every component contains its own logic, styles and template. It is characteristic of React, Vue and Svelte.\\n\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD)\\nA modern methodology, which organizes code by business feature rather than by technical layer. It includes the layers: app, processes, pages, features, entities, shared.\\n\\nFlux/Redux\\nAn architecture with a unidirectional data flow. The state is held in a single store, and the changes happen through actions and reducers. It provides predictability and makes debugging easier.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of architecture depends on the scale of the project and of the team. For small applications the component-based approach will do; for large ones FSD or Redux. MVC and MVVM remain relevant for frameworks such as Angular. The main thing is to observe the principles of the separation of responsibility and of the reuse of code.\",\"// An example of MVC in JavaScript\\nclass Model {\\n  constructor() { this.data = []; }\\n  add(item) { this.data.push(item); }\\n}\\nclass View {\\n  render(data) { console.log(data); }\\n}\\nclass Controller {\\n  constructor(model, view) {\\n    this.model = model;\\n    this.view = view;\\n  }\\n  addItem(item) {\\n    this.model.add(item);\\n    this.view.render(this.model.data);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An example of MVVM with Vue.js\\nnew Vue({\\n  el: '#app',\\n  data: { message: 'Hello' },\\n  methods: {\\n    update() { this.message = 'Updated'; }\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// An example of a React component\\nfunction Button({ label, onClick }) {\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;\\n}\\n\\n// The FSD structure\\nsrc/\\n  features/\\n    auth/\\n      ui/LoginForm.jsx\\n      model/authSlice.js\\n  entities/\\n    user/\\n      api/userApi.js\\n  shared/\\n    ui/Button.jsx\\n\\n// An example of a Redux reducer\\nconst counterReducer = (state = 0, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT': return state + 1;\\n    default: return state;\\n  }\\n};\"],[2387,\"No, an animation cannot be made with a Promise alone. A Promise manages the order in which asynchronous operations are performed, but it provides no mechanism for changing state smoothly over time. An animation requires requestAnimationFrame or setInterval, which cause a frame to update. A Promise can be used to organize a sequence of animations, but not for the rendering itself.\",\"Why a Promise is unsuitable for an animation\\nA Promise is an object representing the result of an asynchronous operation. It lets you run code after a task completes, but it provides no means of managing the time or the frequency of the updates. An animation requires an element's properties (the position or the opacity, for example) to change at a particular frequency (normally 60 frames per second). A Promise cannot generate such repeated calls.\\n\\nHow a Promise can be used in an animation\\nA Promise can be useful for organizing a sequence of animations. You can create a function that returns a Promise resolving after one animation step completes, for example. Then, by means of a chain of .then() calls, the next step can be started. The animation step itself, however, has to be implemented by means of requestAnimationFrame or setInterval.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nA Promise cannot be the only tool for an animation, since it does not manage the frames. In combination with requestAnimationFrame, however, it lets you create complex sequences of animations with clear control over the order of execution.\",\"function animateElement(element, targetPosition, duration) {\\n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\\n    const start = performance.now();\\n    const startPosition = element.offsetLeft;\\n\\n    function step(currentTime) {\\n      const elapsed = currentTime - start;\\n      const progress = Math.min(elapsed / duration, 1);\\n      const currentPosition = startPosition + (targetPosition - startPosition) * progress;\\n      element.style.left = currentPosition + 'px';\\n\\n      if (progress < 1) {\\n        requestAnimationFrame(step);\\n      } else {\\n        resolve();\\n      }\\n    }\\n\\n    requestAnimationFrame(step);\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// The usage\\nconst box = document.getElementById('box');\\nanimateElement(box, 300, 1000)\\n  .then(() => animateElement(box, 100, 500));\"],[2388,\"setState in React works asynchronously, in order to optimize the performance. React groups several setState calls into one batch (batching) and updates the state once the handling of all of the events has completed. Immediately after a setState call the state's value therefore stays the old one. To obtain the current state, use the callback in setState, or useEffect.\",\"Why is setState asynchronous?\\nReact uses asynchronous state updates in order to improve the performance. Rather than repainting the component after every setState call, React gathers all of the changes into one batch (batching) and applies them in one go. That lets it avoid redundant repaints and improve the responsiveness of the interface.\\n\\nHow this works in practice\\nWhen you call setState, React does not change the state at once. Instead it adds an update request to a queue. Once the current event handler (of a click, for example) has completed, React processes all of the accumulated requests and updates the state, causing the component to render again.\\n\\nHow do you obtain the current state?\\nIf you need to perform an action after the state has updated, use:\\n- The callback in setState (for class components): `this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 }, () => console.log(this.state.count))`\\n- The useEffect hook (for functional components): `useEffect(() => { console.log(count); }, [count]);`\\n- The functional form of setState, if the new state depends on the previous one: `setCount(prev => prev + 1);`\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe asynchrony of setState is a feature of React aimed at optimization. An understanding of this mechanism helps you avoid mistakes when working with state and use callbacks or effects properly in order to handle the updates.\",\"// An example: the state is not updated at once\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1);\\n    console.log(count); // It will print 0, not 1\\n  };\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>{count}</button>;\\n}\"],[2389,\"Functions inside a component are created anew on every render, which can cause redundant re-renders of child components if they are passed in as props. For optimization, use useCallback, or move functions that do not depend on the props and the state outside the component. Moving them outside is preferable for pure functions, since that reduces the load on the garbage collector and makes testing easier.\",\"The problem of creating functions inside a component\\nWhen you declare a function inside a React component, it is created anew on every render. That is fine for simple applications, but it can become a performance problem if the function is passed as a prop into child components wrapped in `React.memo` or using `PureComponent`. Each time, a new reference to the function will cause the child component to re-render, even if its props have not changed.\\n\\nWhen to move a function outside\\nIf a function does not use the props, the state or other closures from the component, it can and should be declared outside the component. That guarantees the reference to the function will never change, and it improves the readability of the code. An example:\\n\\nWhen to use useCallback\\nIf a function depends on the props or the state, it cannot be moved outside. In that case use the `useCallback` hook, in order to memoize the function and prevent it being recreated on unnecessary renders. An example:\\n\\nConclusion\\nMove functions outside the component where that is possible — it improves the performance and the readability. Use `useCallback` only for functions that depend on closures and are passed into optimized child components. Do not memoize everything indiscriminately, since that can make the performance worse through redundant computation.\",\"// A function moved outside — the reference is stable\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  console.log('Clicked');\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent({ id }) {\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    console.log(id);\\n  }, [id]);\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;\\n}\"],[2390,\"Side effects in React are handled by means of the useEffect hook. It lets you run code after the component renders — requests to an API, subscriptions or timers, for example. It matters to state the dependencies properly, in order to avoid redundant calls. To unsubscribe from the effects, the clean-up function returned from useEffect is used.\",\"What are side effects in React?\\nSide effects are the operations that affect the external environment or depend on it — HTTP requests, working with localStorage, timers or subscriptions to events, for example. In React such operations cannot be performed directly during the render, since that can lead to an inconsistent state of the UI. The `useEffect` hook is used for them.\\n\\nHow does useEffect work?\\n`useEffect` takes two arguments: the function with the effect, and the array of dependencies. The function runs after every render if the dependencies have changed. If the array is empty, the effect runs only once, after the component has mounted. If no dependencies are stated, the effect runs after every render.\\n\\nCleaning the effects up\\nIf an effect creates a subscription or a timer, you have to return a clean-up function, in order to avoid memory leaks. React will call that function before the effect runs again, or when the component unmounts.\\n\\nThe rules of use\\n\\n- Do not call hooks inside conditions or loops — they must be at the top level of the component.\\n- State all of the variables used in the effect in the array of dependencies, in order to avoid bugs with stale data.\\n- For complex logic use several `useEffect` calls rather than one big one.\\n\\nConclusion: `useEffect` is the main tool for working with side effects in functional React components. The proper management of the dependencies and of the clean-up guarantees the application works stably and prevents memory leaks.\",\"import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => setUser(data));\\n  }, [userId]); // the effect runs when userId changes\\n\\n  return <div>{user ? user.name : 'Loading...'}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const timer = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log('Tick');\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  return () => clearInterval(timer); // the clean-up\\n}, []);\"],[2391,\"In FSD every layer has a strict area of responsibility: shared contains the reusable utilities, entities the business entities, features the user scenarios, widgets the compositional blocks, pages the pages, and app the global configuration. A layer can import only the ones below it, which provides a unidirectional direction of the dependencies and makes maintaining the code easier.\",\"The principle on which the layers are organized in FSD\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD) is a methodology for organizing frontend projects, which divides the code into layers with clear areas of responsibility. The main idea is that every layer should be responsible for a particular aspect of the application, while the dependencies between them are strictly unidirectional: from the upper layers to the lower ones. That prevents circular dependencies and makes refactoring easier.\\n\\nThe layers and their responsibility\\n\\n- app — the global configuration, the initialization, the providers (the Redux store, the routing, for example).\\n- pages — the composition of widgets and features for particular pages.\\n- widgets — self-contained blocks of the interface, uniting several features (the site's header, for example).\\n- features — the user scenarios (a login form, a search, for example).\\n- entities — the business entities (a user, a product, for example).\\n- shared — the reusable utilities, UI components and API clients.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nFSD helps you maintain a clean architecture in large projects, where the separation of responsibility and the predictability of the dependencies matter. This approach is especially useful in teams, where scalability and ease of introducing changes are required.\",\"// entities/user/model.ts — the user's business logic\\nexport interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\n// features/login/ui/LoginForm.tsx — the login scenario\\nimport { User } from 'entities/user';\\n\\nexport const LoginForm = () => {\\n  // it uses entities but not widgets or pages\\n};\"],[2392,\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is a methodology for organizing frontend architecture, which divides a project into layers (app, pages, widgets, features, entities, shared). The main problems: excessive complexity for small projects, a rigid structure that can hinder fast development, and the risk of creating too many abstractions. FSD also requires strict discipline in the team and can be hard to adopt in an existing project.\",\"The problems that arise when using Feature-Sliced Design (FSD)\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural approach for frontend applications, which structures code by functional module (feature) and by layer (app, pages, widgets, entities, shared). Despite its advantages in organizing large projects, FSD has a number of problems that can arise when it is applied.\\n\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Excessive complexity for small projects: FSD requires the creation of a great many layers and modules, which can be superfluous for small applications. That slows the development down and increases the time spent on refactoring.\\n- A rigid structure: the strict hierarchy of the layers can limit the flexibility. If a simple feature has to be added quickly, for example, the developer has to follow FSD's rules, which can be inconvenient.\\n- The risk of over-engineering: a striving for the ideal architecture can lead to the creation of too many abstractions, which makes the code harder to understand and to maintain.\\n- The difficulty of adoption in an existing project: moving to FSD in a project that is already running requires considerable effort in refactoring, which can be expensive and risky.\\n- The team's discipline is required: FSD is effective only if the whole team observes its rules strictly. Otherwise the architecture degrades fast.\\n\\nAn example of the code: the problem of redundant abstraction\\n\\nIn a small project such an approach can be superfluous, since it would be simpler to write it all in one file.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFSD is worth applying in large projects with long-term maintenance and a big team, where scalability and a clear separation of responsibility matter. For small or prototype projects it is better to use simpler architectural solutions, in order to avoid excessive complexity.\",\"// Instead of a simple component, several files have to be created\\n// entities/user/model.ts\\nexport const useUser = () => {\\n  // the logic\\n};\\n\\n// features/login/ui/LoginForm.tsx\\nimport { useUser } from 'entities/user';\\n\\nexport const LoginForm = () => {\\n  const { login } = useUser();\\n  return <button onClick={login}>Login</button>;\\n};\\n\\n// pages/LoginPage.tsx\\nimport { LoginForm } from 'features/login';\\n\\nexport const LoginPage = () => <LoginForm />;\"],[2393,\"An infinite state update in React leads to an infinite loop of re-rendering the component. That happens when a state update is called directly in the component's body or in a useEffect hook without the right dependencies. As a result the browser hangs or crashes with a stack-overflow error. To avoid that, state updates have to be tied to events or to conditional logic.\",\"What happens on an infinite state update in React?\\nWhen you update a component's state, React starts a re-render. If the state update happens inside the rendering process itself (in the component's body or in a useEffect hook without the right dependencies, for example), that creates an infinite loop: render -> state update -> render -> state update, and so on.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at a simple component that updates its state directly in the body:\\n\\nThis code will cause infinite rendering, since setCount is called on every render, which leads to a new render.\\n\\nHow do you fix it?\\nA state update should happen only in response to an event, or in hooks with the right dependencies. For example:\\n\\nHere setCount is called only on a click, rather than on every render.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn infinite state update is a widespread mistake that leads to the application hanging. Always update the state in event handlers or in a useEffect with the dependencies stated, in order to avoid loops.\",\"function Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  setCount(count + 1); // An infinite loop!\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n      Clicked {count} times\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2394,\"Redundant console.log calls in a child component arise because of re-rendering. To avoid them, use React.memo in order to prevent unnecessary renders if the props have not changed. You can also wrap your functions in useCallback and your values in useMemo, in order to stabilize the references. That is especially useful when passing callbacks or objects into child components.\",\"Why do redundant console.log calls arise?\\nIn React a component repaints when the state or the props change. If a child component is not optimized, it will render every time the parent updates, even if its own props have not changed. That leads to redundant calls of the console.log that sits in the component's body.\\n\\nHow do you fix it?\\nThe main tool is `React.memo`. It wraps a component and prevents a re-render if the props have stayed the same (a shallow comparison).\\n\\nIn this example `Child` will not repaint when the button is pressed, since the `value` prop does not change. If a function or an object is passed in, however, they have to be stabilized by means of `useCallback` and `useMemo`.\\n\\nAdditional advice\\n\\n- Use `React.StrictMode` in order to reveal problems, but remember that it causes a double render in dev mode.\\n- Do not abuse memoization — apply it only to components that really do repaint often.\\n- Use the React DevTools to check which components are repainting.\\n\\nConclusion: use `React.memo` for pure components, and `useCallback`/`useMemo` to stabilize the props. That reduces the number of unnecessary renders and improves the application's performance.\",\"import React, { memo } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = memo(({ value }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n      <Child value=\\\"static\\\" />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2395,\"The Provider is a component from React's Context API, which passes the context's value to all of the child components. It wraps part of the component tree and gives them access to the data without an explicit passing through props. That makes managing global state — the theme or the authentication, for example — easier.\",\"The purpose of the Provider in the Context API\\nThe Provider is a wrapper component, which makes the context's value available to all of the child components in the React tree. It solves the problem of \\\"prop drilling\\\", where data has to be passed through a great many intermediate components.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe Provider takes a `value` prop, which becomes available through the `useContext` hook or through a Consumer. When the value changes, all of the subscribed components repaint.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Provider is used for the centralized management of state in React applications, when the data is needed by many components at different levels of nesting. That is especially useful for themes, localization or the user's data.\",\"import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext();\\n\\nexport function ThemeProvider({ children }) {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction ChildComponent() {\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>\\n      Current theme: {theme}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2396,\"The key attribute helps React identify every element of a list on an update. Without it React will repaint the whole list, which reduces the performance. A key has to be unique and stable — an element's ID, for example. Using the array index as the key can lead to errors when the order of the elements changes.\",\"What are keys in lists in JSX for?\\nWhen rendering lists in React, every element has to have a unique `key` attribute. That is necessary for the reconciliation mechanism React uses in order to update the DOM efficiently. Without a key React cannot determine exactly which elements have been changed, added or deleted, and will be forced to repaint the whole list, which reduces the performance.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen React updates a list, it compares the current and the new state. The key lets React match the elements of the old and the new list, minimizing the number of changes in the DOM. If you add an element at the start of a list, for example, React with keys will understand that the remaining elements have stayed in place and will simply insert the new one. Without keys it will repaint every element.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse unique, stable values for the key, such as an ID from a database. Avoid using the array index if the order of the elements can change. That will ensure React works properly and the application performs well.\",\"// The right use of key\\nconst items = [{ id: 1, text: 'A' }, { id: 2, text: 'B' }];\\nconst list = items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.text}</li>);\\n\\n// The wrong use of key (the index)\\nconst listBad = items.map((item, index) => <li key={index}>{item.text}</li>);\\n// When the order changes or elements are added at the start, the indexes go out of step, which can cause errors.\"],[2397,\"No, hooks cannot be called inside conditions or loops. React requires that hooks be called in one and the same order on every render of a component. That is necessary for the state to be preserved properly between the renders. Breaking this rule leads to errors and unpredictable behaviour.\",\"Why can hooks not be called inside conditions or loops?\\nReact uses the order in which the hooks are called in order to bind the state to a particular component. If a hook is called inside a condition, then on different renders the order can change, which breaks React's internal mechanisms.\\n\\nAn example of the wrong use\\n\\nIn this example, when `flag = true` two hooks are called, and when `flag = false` only one. React will not be able to match the state properly on the next render.\\n\\nHow do you get round the restriction properly?\\nIf you need to use a hook conditionally, move the logic into a separate component or use conditional rendering at the level of the JSX:\\n\\nSuch an approach guarantees that the hooks are always called in the same order.\\n\\nConclusion\\nObserving the rules of hooks is the basis of React applications working stably. Always call hooks at the top level of the component, in order to avoid bugs and to ensure predictable behaviour.\",\"function MyComponent({ flag }) {\\n  if (flag) {\\n    const [value, setValue] = useState(0); // An error!\\n  }\\n  const [other, setOther] = useState('');\\n}\\n\\nfunction InnerComponent() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(0);\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent({ flag }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {flag && <InnerComponent />}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2398,\"A reducer has to be a pure function, in order to guarantee the predictability of the changes of state. A pure function has no side effects and always returns the same result for the same arguments. That makes debugging, testing and reproducing the application's states easier.\",\"Why does a reducer have to be a pure function?\\nIn Redux a reducer is a function that takes the current state and an action and returns the new state. The requirement of purity means that a reducer must not change the state it is given directly, must not perform side effects (requests to an API, mutations of the DOM, setTimeout calls) and must return a new state object rather than modifying the old one.\\n\\nThe main reasons\\n\\n- Predictability: a pure function always returns the same result for the same input data. That lets you track the changes of state and reproduce bugs easily.\\n- Testability: pure functions are easy to test — there is no need to create complex mocks or environments.\\n- Reproducibility: the Redux DevTools can record and replay sequences of actions, because a reducer has no hidden dependencies.\\n- Optimization: React can compare the previous and the new state efficiently (a shallow comparison), in order to decide about repainting.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nIn the first case React may not notice the change, since the reference to the object has stayed the same. In the second a new object is created, and React updates the UI properly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe purity of a reducer is not merely a rule but a fundamental principle, which ensures the reliability and predictability of state management. Apply pure functions wherever a deterministic transformation of data is required, especially in Redux and other Flux-like architectures.\",\"// Wrong: a mutation of the state\\nfunction badReducer(state, action) {\\n  state.count += 1; // a mutation\\n  return state;\\n}\\n\\n// Right: we return a new object\\nfunction goodReducer(state, action) {\\n  return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };\\n}\"],[2399,\"The subscription to changes in the Context API happens through a consumer component. A component that uses useContext or Consumer subscribes to the context automatically. When the value passed into the Provider changes, all of the subscribed components re-render. That lets you pass data deep down the tree without prop drilling.\",\"How the subscription to changes in the Context API works\\nIn React's Context API the subscription to changes is implemented automatically when the `useContext` hook or the `Consumer` component is used. When a component calls `useContext(MyContext)`, it is registered as a subscriber to that context. When the value passed into the `Provider` changes, React notifies all of the subscribed components and starts their re-render.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe particulars of the subscription\\n\\n- The subscription happens at the level of the context rather than to individual fields of the value. If the value is an object, any change of the object will cause all of the subscribers to re-render.\\n- For optimization you can memoize the value by means of `useMemo`, in order to avoid redundant renders.\\n- Components that do not use the context are not subscribed and do not re-render when it changes.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Context API is convenient for passing global data (the theme, the authentication, the language) without prop drilling. It is worth applying with care for data that changes often, however, since that can cause excessive renders. For complex state it is better to use Redux or other state-management libraries.\",\"import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext('light');\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Current theme: {theme}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>\\n        Toggle\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2400,\"The loading state should be set to true before the asynchronous operation begins, before the try block. In the finally block, or after the try-catch, it has to be reset to false. That guarantees loading will be active while the request runs and will be switched off properly even if an error arises.\",\"The right placement of the loading state\\nThe loading state should be managed outside the try and catch blocks, in order to ensure the interface behaves predictably. Setting loading to true happens immediately before the asynchronous function is called, while the reset happens after the operation completes, independently of the result.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- If you set loading inside try, then on an error before loading is set it will stay false, and the user will not see the loading indicator.\\n- If you reset loading only in catch, then on a successful run it will stay true for ever.\\n- The finally block guarantees the code will run in any case, which makes it the ideal place to reset loading.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSet loading to true before try and reset it in finally (or after the try-catch). That ensures the loading state is displayed properly in every scenario: success, an error or a premature termination.\",\"async function fetchData() {\\n  setLoading(true); // We set it before the try\\n  try {\\n    const response = await api.get('/data');\\n    setData(response.data);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    setError(error.message);\\n  } finally {\\n    setLoading(false); // We reset it in the finally\\n  }\\n}\"]]");export{e as default};